\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Francisco Mart\u237\'00\'EDn Moreno / A Mexican \uc2 Shimon Peres\b0\par\par\uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Israel is the most innovative country in the world. It is known, nothing more and nothing less, as the Silicon Valley of the East, as it has one of the most advanced technologies on the planet. It invests hundreds of millions of dollars in innovation and has become, in just 30 years, one of the richest countries in the world with a per capita income of 52,000 dollars, slightly higher than Germany with 51,000, the United Kingdom with 46,000 and France with 43,000, not to mention Mexico with 10,000. After the United States and China, Israel is the country with the most companies registered on the NASDAQ, the second largest stock exchange in the United States. Israel spends 5% of its GDP on research and development, more than the US and South Korea. In 1984, when \uc2 Shimon Peres\uc2  first came to power as Prime Minister, \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a socialist-oriented banana economy. It was an \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  government similar to any Latin American country.\par \par \uc2  The economic disaster was monumental, with an inflation rate of 400% (similar to that of Venezuela today), i.e., the greatest economic instability in its history, with a GDP half that of Germany and France. He immediately made a 180-degree turnaround by granting autonomy to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , cancelling the irresponsible printing of money and privatizing public enterprises to turn the terrible public deficit into a surplus. The results were not long in coming, as inflation plummeted to 2% and the economy grew at 5% in the short term. They invited the best scientists and the most advanced companies in the West to promote gigantic plans. Prosperity and true prosperity came to Israel. In 1991, countless potential projects failed for lack of capital, so the government, in an effort to create an environment of confidence, founded Yozma, a venture capital fund financed with public money, without corruption and with efficiency, in search of technological and industrial leadership of world dimensions. Since the government lacked experience in such financial complexity, it entered into an alliance with international experts to ensure success, so that for every dollar invested in the funds, Israel would put in 80 cents and retain 40% ownership. Once the project was crowned with success, the entrepreneurs involved would buy the 40% from the government at a pre-agreed interest rate to facilitate the transactions in the context of unquestionable legal certainty and transparency in the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . As time went by, the Yozma program had a 0% impact on public finances, which were strengthened with an avalanche of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that triggered the country's economic and social expansion. Israel, a desert country that supplies agricultural products to Europe thanks to advanced biotechnology technology and, incredibly, exports water to some of its neighbors that do not pose a military threat, generates 600 million cubic meters per year, with which it supplies the population, industry, commerce and the countryside. More? Yes! The government dedicated 30% of its budget to agriculture and another 30% to education and it is the nation of startups, newly created companies that by employing new technologies have great possibilities of accelerated growth with great social impact. Now, will Lopez Obrador know the meaning of a nation of startups, and the importance of creating a venture capital fund financed with public resources? If he knew the importance of science and technology, he would never have ordered the destruction of Conacyt or the national education system, nor would he have abandoned SMEs to their fate during the pandemic, thus creating 5 million more poor people. While Israel allocates 30% of its budget to agriculture, AMLO disappears the Financiera Nacional de Desarrollo Agropecuario, Rural, Forestal y Pesquero. You, kind reader, could you imagine a conversation between \uc2 Shimon Peres\uc2  and L\u243\'00\'F3pez Obrador, especially when the latter stated: "for the good of all, first the poor...? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.franciscomartinmoreno.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.franciscomartinmoreno.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230711ej7b006pj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
