\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Affirms Israel 'AA-/A-1+' Ratings; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 4,225 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2022\par 22:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 13 May 2022 16:12 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Israel 'AA-/A-1+' Ratings; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- We expect Israel's economy to expand by 5.5% in 2022 following very \par strong 8.2% growth in 2021 after a 2.2% contraction in 2020. \par      -- Domestic political and security risks have resurfaced as the governing \par coalition lost its thin majority in the Knesset in April 2022, and a series of \par violent attacks have taken place across Israel in recent weeks. \par      -- Fiscal performance is improving, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Israel\f28 \uc2 \f2   has \par commenced a cycle of monetary policy tightening, raising the key rate by 25 \par basis points in April 2022. \par      -- We affirmed our 'AA-/A-1+' sovereign ratings on Israel. \par      -- The outlook is stable. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On May 13, 2022, S&P Global Ratings affirmed its 'AA-/A-1+' long- and \par short-term foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on Israel. The \par outlook is stable. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The stable outlook balances elevated geopolitical and domestic political risks \par and the pandemic-induced deterioration of Israel's fiscal position, against \par the state's persistently resilient economy, improving budgetary performance, \par and strong balance of payments. We also forecast that Israel's net external \par asset position will remain at 40% of GDP over the next two years, providing \par the economy with substantial buffers. \par  \par Downside scenario \par Pressure on the ratings could build if geopolitical or domestic political \par risks escalated, affecting Israel's economic, fiscal, and balance-of-payments \par metrics. \par  \par Upside scenario \par We could take a positive rating action if geopolitical, as well as domestic \par political and security risks, significantly reduced, while at the same time \par fiscal outturns proved materially stronger than our projections, helping net \par general government debt fall below its pre-pandemic level more quickly. \par  \par RATIONALE \par Israel's credit strengths include its wealthy and resilient economy, its net \par external asset position, and the benefits that accrue from flexible monetary \par settings and a relatively deep pool of domestic savings. \par  \par Our ratings on Israel remain constrained by significant political, security, \par and geopolitical risks. These have been highlighted in recent months by a \par series of violent attacks across Israel, which followed the significant 11-day \par outbreak of violence between Israeli security forces and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hamas\f28 \uc2 \f2   in Gaza in May \par 2021.  Although risks are elevated, we note that historically, domestic \par political uncertainty and short bouts of conflict typically do not \par significantly affect the economy. \par  \par Institutional and economic profile: Growth remains strong but heightened \par domestic political uncertainty has resurfaced \par      -- Israel's economy expanded by a strong 8.2% last year in real terms and \par we forecast 5.5% growth in 2022. \par      -- The disparate governing coalition formed in June 2021 lost its \par parliamentary majority in April 2022. In our view, this increases political \par uncertainty and raises the likelihood of another out-of-cycle election. \par      -- We consider that domestic security and regional geopolitical risks \par remain elevated, as highlighted by several domestic firearm and knife attacks \par in recent weeks. \par  \par Israel continues to register strong growth and last year's outturn exceeded \par our previous published expectations, with output expanding by 8.2% in real \par terms (vis-a-vis our previous expectation of 6.5% growth; see our full \par analysis "Israel," published on Nov. 15, 2021). This strong result was \par bolstered by brisk performance in the fourth quarter of the year, when all key \par expenditure components--including consumption, exports, and \par investment--registered quarter-on-quarter growth of between 4% and 6%. \par Considered by sector, growth has also been widespread, with transportation and \par storage, wholesale and retail trade, construction, and professional services \par expanding quickly. \par  \par Last year's strong economic dynamic follows what we consider a mild \par pandemic-driven contraction of 2.2% in 2020, especially compared with a much \par more pronounced decline in real GDP across advanced economies, including the \par U.S. This is partly thanks to the structure of the Israeli economy, being \par centered around high value-added IT-related services exports including \par cybersecurity, fintech, digital health, defense, and broader technology. \par Global demand for such services rose throughout the pandemic, while the \par Israeli companies offering them were also typically in a better position to \par let employees work from home, meaning they were less affected by lockdowns \par compared with other businesses. Additionally, Israel's economic dynamics have \par been supported in recent years by the expansion of gas exports after the \par Leviathan offshore field became \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in December 2019. \par  \par We view further pandemic-related risks as limited for Israel. Although Israel \par was hit by an outbreak of the Omicron variant at the beginning of the year, \par new cases have quickly declined since then; no additional restrictions were \par introduced through the latest wave. Close to 70% of the population has been \par vaccinated against COVID-19, with Israel delivering a quick and effective \par immunization campaign early in 2021, well ahead of most other countries. In \par early March 2022, the Israeli government removed almost all remaining \par pandemic-related restrictions, including those pertaining to foreign travel, \par and we do not expect these to be re-introduced. \par  \par We also view the direct impact of Russia's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Ukraine as \par limited for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Israel\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Russia and Ukraine combined account for around 2% of \par Israel's total exports of goods and business services. We note that due to the \par military conflict, demand from other countries for some Israeli exports--such \par as cybersecurity and other defense-related services and goods--could actually \par rise. \par  \par Nevertheless, the full impact of the conflict in Ukraine remains difficult to \par judge, particularly as it affects Israel's growth through its repercussions on \par key trading partners, including Europe and the U.S. Israel has not directly \par imposed sanctions on Russia but many Israeli companies are complying with the \par sanctions imposed by the West. However, given that its population includes \par large numbers of people with potential links to Russia and Ukraine, Israel may \par receive immigrants from both countries. \par  \par At the same time, we consider that rising domestic inflation and the monetary \par policy tightening cycle that \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Israel\f28 \uc2 \f2   (\f28 \uc2 \f2 BOI\f28 \uc2 \f2  ) has already commenced \par will cool domestic demand. Consequently, we currently forecast headline growth \par of 5.5% for Israel for 2022. Most of this annual growth reflects the \par carry-over effect from the fourth quarter of 2021 of 4.9%, and thus the \par quarterly growth profile implies a notable slowdown of economic dynamics \par compared with last year. That said, we still expect all key expenditure \par components to grow. We forecast medium-term growth to remain around 3.5%, \par broadly in line with trends registered pre-pandemic and bolstered by Israel's \par favorable demographics. Israel's population has been increasing by around 2% \par annually over the past five years. \par  \par Economic performance aside, elevated domestic political uncertainty has \par reappeared. Following a turbulent period with four general elections over just \par two years, Israel formed a coalition government comprising parties across the \par political spectrum in June 2021. The government has subsequently been able to \par pass a budget for the remainder of 2021 and 2022 alongside a series of \par economic reforms. However, the coalition lost its thin one-seat majority in \par April 2022 when one of the MPs--a member of Prime Minister Naftali Bennett's \par Yamina party--defected. We consider that the development will once again \par increase domestic political uncertainty, raising the likelihood of another \par out-of-cycle general election. It will also make it more challenging to pass \par the 2023 budget, the deadline for which is end-March 2023, otherwise the \par Knesset dissolves according to Israeli legislation, triggering an early poll. \par We do not currently expect this to lead to any immediate economic or policy \par repercussions and note that historically, growth in Israel has remained strong \par even through periods of heightened political volatility. \par  \par Israel diverges from other sovereigns rated in the 'AA' category due to its \par elevated domestic security and regional geopolitical risks, which continue to \par constrain the sovereign ratings. This was prominently highlighted by a major \par escalation of conflict and hostilities between Israeli security services and \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hamas\f28 \uc2 \f2   in May 2021. Although ultimately relatively short-lived, the friction \par was arguably the most significant in years, being complicated by the flare-up \par of domestic inter-ethnic unrest between Arab-Israelis and Jews. \par  \par More recently, domestic security risks have re-escalated. Attacks have been \par carried out across Israel with several people reported dead in recent weeks, \par with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hamas\f28 \uc2 \f2   and other groups claiming responsibility. Palestinians and the \par Israeli police have also clashed in Jerusalem and the West Bank. As a result, \par Ra'am, an Arab party that is part of Israel's coalition government, \par temporarily suspended its support, although its leader, Mr. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mansour Abbas\f28 \uc2 \f2  , has \par subsequently announced that the party will remain part of the coalition. \par  \par In our view, further escalations remain possible and weigh on Israel's credit \par profile. While the state has recently established diplomatic relations with \par several countries--including the United Arab Emirates, Bahrain, Morocco, and \par Sudan--Israel's relations with several other neighboring countries remain very \par tense, especially with Syria and Iran. \par  \par Flexibility and performance profile: Israel's fiscal performance is \par strengthening while the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  commenced a rate-hiking cycle to combat \par rising inflation \par      -- Israel's fiscal performance is improving and we forecast net general \par government debt will reach the pre-pandemic level of 58% of GDP by 2026. \par      -- Inflation has accelerated and we expect it will average 3.8% in 2022, \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 13 May 2022 16:12 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Israel 'AA-/A-1+' -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par still lower than in most advanced countries, including the U.S. We consider \par that this gives the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  greater capacity to respond in a measured way \par and it has already commenced a rate-hiking cycle. \par      -- Israel's external position remains a key ratings strength and is \par characterized by persistent current account surpluses, a net external creditor \par position, and sizable \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves worth 40% of GDP. \par  \par Israel's fiscal performance is strengthening following the pandemic-induced \par deterioration. Last year's general government balance recorded a deficit of \par 5.5% of GDP, a marked improvement from 11.5% in 2020. This consolidation is \par primarily explained by robust revenue growth of almost 19% in nominal terms in \par 2021, reflecting the strong underlying economic dynamics. This is an \par unprecedented outturn compared with the 5% average annual revenue growth \par registered in the decade before the pandemic. More specifically, income and \par property tax receipts have risen by almost 30%, while customs, duties, and VAT \par rose by 18%. Meanwhile, the previous pandemic-related expenditure has been \par progressively scaled back and the authorities estimate that this spending will \par amount to just 0.5% of GDP in 2022, mostly related to testing and support for \par health institutions. \par  \par High frequency fiscal data suggests that the aforementioned positive trends \par have continued into 2022. The central government account registered a surplus \par of 1.9% of GDP in the first four months of 2022, which represents the \par strongest performance in decades. The key question is whether some of this \par outcome will prove to be a one-off. This could be the case for certain revenue \par items driven by strong financial and real estate market performance, including \par real estate and capital gains taxes.  In addition--given the accelerating \par inflation, tightening of Israel's monetary policy, and rapid pace of house \par price growth reducing affordability--the government may be under rising \par pressure to adopt additional spending measures to soften the effect on living \par standards. \par  \par We forecast that Israel's general government deficit, barring unforeseen \par political developments, will amount to 3% of GDP in 2022, followed by deficits \par of 2.5% of GDP over the medium term. Consequently, net general government debt \par is declining from a peak of 68% of GDP in 2020 and we expect it to reduce to \par the pre-pandemic level of 58% of GDP by 2026. \par  \par Israel's public debt structure remains favorable. Close to 90% of debt is \par denominated in local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and held domestically by local banks, pension \par funds, and other institutional investors. The country benefits from strong \par access to capital markets, both domestically and internationally. This has \par supported the government's efforts to diversify its funding base and lengthen \par the average debt maturity. Although the government traditionally accesses \par funding through the deep domestic capital markets, we also note recent \par international bond issuances, with maturities extending from 30-100 years. It \par could tap foreign markets again in light of substantial investor demand. In \par the unlikely event of funding disruptions, the government also has recourse to \par the remaining portion of a longstanding U.S. debt guarantee program. \par  \par Israel's interest expenditures remain moderate and we expect they will average \par just under 8% of general government revenues through 2025. Interest spending \par is set to rise in 2022, reflecting higher projected inflation and therefore an \par upward adjustment of inflation-indexed debt (which constitutes around half of \par overall government debt) and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOI\f28 \uc2 \f2   tightening cycle. However, given that \par inflation has so far remained below peers' and is projected to remain so, we \par consider \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOI\f28 \uc2 \f2   to have more room to tighten policy in a measured way that would \par also keep interest expenditure growth in check. \par  \par We consider Israel's balance of payments a key ratings strength. The country \par has been running a current account surplus for the past 18 years, primarily \par supported by the fast expansion of high value-added information and \par communications technology services exports (the total external services \par surplus has averaged 5% of GDP in the past few years). This has shifted Israel \par into a substantial net external asset position of about 40% of GDP--one of the \par highest among non-commodity-exporting sovereigns--and reduced its gross \par external financing needs. In 2021, the current account surplus amounted to \par 4.7% of GDP, bolstered by the resilient performance of services exports. \par Strong global demand for Israeli services, as well as gas exports from the new \par fields, should keep the current account surplus close to 4.5% of GDP over the \par medium term. \par  \par We also consider Israel's monetary policy flexibility as a credit strength. \par The \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOI\f28 \uc2 \f2   is a highly credible institution, with a track record of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par independence and a range of market-based monetary instruments, benefiting from \par the deep local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  financial and capital markets. Throughout the \par pandemic, the bank deployed its arsenal of available tools to soften its \par effects on the Israeli economy, including policy rate cuts, quantitative \par easing, and low interest rate loans to banks. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOI\f28 \uc2 \f2   has also amassed \par substantial \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves of US$198 billion as of end-April 2022 \par (37% of GDP). We note that current \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve levels cover by \par 1.4x Israel's overall external debt (including government and private sector \par foreign debt). \par  \par Although inflation in Israel picked up in recent months, it remains \par significantly below the upswing experienced in some emerging and developed \par markets, including the U.S. and the U.K. We project average annual inflation \par of 3.8% in 2022, still a notable upward revision from our previous forecast of \par 2.2%. One factor that still helps slow domestic inflation is the increased \par reliance on domestically produced gas, partially offsetting the impact of the \par global increase in energy prices. Nevertheless, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOI\f28 \uc2 \f2   has reacted to rising \par inflation, commencing a tightening cycle and raising the key interest rate by \par 25 basis points to 0.35% in April 2022. We expect more interest rate hikes to \par follow as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOI\f28 \uc2 \f2   aims to relieve domestic price pressures, stemming from the \par closed output gap, strong domestic demand, and labor market. \par  \par The Israeli banking system is well capitalized, profitable, and liquid. Banks' \par asset quality has been historically high; nonperforming loans stood at just \par 1.3% of total loans at the onset of the pandemic and have not materially \par increased since, even as the lion's share of previously deferred loans (under \par the pandemic-related \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOI\f28 \uc2 \f2   measures) have restarted repayments. The system is \par domestic deposit-funded and remains in a net external asset position. Even \par though we expect some asset quality deterioration post-pandemic, substantial \par capital and liquidity buffers should enable the banks to weather the impact. \par  \par Israeli banks have increased their exposure to real estate-related lending, \par mainly in mortgages but also to finance new construction projects, on the back \par of strong demand for housing. While we think that the risk level in mortgage \par lending is manageable--and supported by a series of macro prudential measures \par initiated by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOI\f28 \uc2 \f2   over the past decade--the risks could rise if house \par prices continued to escalate, widening imbalances. We note that house prices \par have been increasing by a substantial 15% year on year in recent months. In \par parallel, we think that the level of risk in the residential construction \par segment is rising as a result of banks' growing exposure to the sector. For \par more details on the Israeli banking sector see "Banking Industry Country Risk \par Assessment: Israel," published Sept. 10, 2021. \par  \par KEY INDICATORS \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par      -- General Criteria: Environmental, Social, And Governance Principles In \par Credit Ratings, Oct. 10, 2021 \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Principles Of Credit Ratings, Feb. 16, 2011 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments, May 18, 2009 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Sovereign Ratings Score Snapshot, May 9, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings List, May 6, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History, May 6, 2022 \par      -- Global Sovereign Rating Trends: First-Quarter 2022, April 13, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators, April 11, 2022; a free interactive version \par is available at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.spratings.com/sri }}}\uc2 \f2  \par      -- Sovereign Debt 2022: Borrowing Will Stay High On Pandemic And \par Geopolitical Tensions, April 5, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Debt 2022: Ukraine Conflict Could Push Developed EMEA's \par Commercial Borrowing Higher Than $1.6 Trillion, April 5, 2022 \par      -- Banking Industry Country Risk Assessment: Israel, Sept. 10, 2021 \par      -- Default, Transition, and Recovery: 2020 Annual Sovereign Default And \par Rating Transition Study, April 12, 2021 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to \par make an informed decision. \par  \par After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and explained the \par recommendation, the Committee discussed key rating factors and critical issues \par in accordance with the relevant criteria. Qualitative and quantitative risk \par factors were considered and discussed, looking at track-record and forecasts. \par  \par The committee's assessment of the key rating factors is reflected in the \par Ratings Score Snapshot above. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 13 May 2022 16:12 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Israel 'AA-/A-1+' -3-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The chair ensured every voting member was given the opportunity to articulate \par his/her opinion. The chair or designee reviewed the draft report to ensure \par consistency with the Committee decision. The views and the decision of the \par rating committee are summarized in the above rationale and outlook. The \par weighting of all rating factors is described in the methodology used in this \par rating action (see 'Related Criteria And Research'). \par  \par Certain terms used in this report, particularly certain adjectives used to \par express our view on rating relevant