\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Inflation may persuade \uc2 Bank of Israel\uc2  to embrace shekel strength\b0\par\par\uc2 934 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2021\par 08:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Shekel is the top EM \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since pandemic rout\par \par \uc2 * Sept CPI climbs towards top of government target range\par \par \uc2 * BOI has completed $30 bln \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchase programme for 2021\par \par \uc2 By Steven Scheer\par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, Nov 9 (Reuters) - It has taken $50 billion and two years but \uc2 the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have finally accepted it is time to pare \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and let the shekel juggernaut roll on.\par \par \uc2 What might be changing the equation - alongside the healthy 7% GDP expansion expected this year - is inflation which is gradually inching towards the top of the official target.\par \par \uc2 So without entirely abandoning \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected by economists to sharply cut back on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases, a strategy adopted years ago to curb the relentless shekel appreciation.\par \par \uc2 The shekel on Monday hit 3.08 per dollar, the highest since 1996.\par \par \uc2 It is the top performing emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the pandemic started roiling financial markets in early 2020, up 10%, while gaining 12% against its main trading partners. The shekel has gained nearly 4% in the year-to-date, making it one of the top three 2021 emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All this despite Israel - unlike many other developing countries - having yet to raise its 0.1% interest rate.\par \par \uc2 The shekel's strength has been attributed to a number of factors as well as the overall weakness of the dollar: a rapid economic recovery from the COVID-19 crisis amid a widespread vaccination programme; a large current account surplus that is expected to reach 5.5% of GDP in 2021, thanks in part to Israel's tech firms; and huge foreign direct investment in the sector that may hit $30 billion this year.\par \par \uc2 Gains in overseas stock markets also often lift the shekel because Israeli pension funds and other institutions are required to cap their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure. That has forced them to sell $20 billion into the market this year.\par \par \uc2 INFLATION EQUATION\par \par \uc2 "You have all these factors and to expect \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2  to try and prevent a sharp shekel appreciation is not realistic," Jonathan Katz, chief economist at Tel Aviv brokerage Leader Capital Markets said, adding it was possible to infer from comments by BoI governor \uc2 Amir Yaron\uc2  that he prefers to not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Yaron took office in late-2018 and stayed on the sidelines in 2019, ignoring an 8% shekel surge and an outcry from exporters. But he \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year as the pandemic necessitated support.\par \par \uc2 Now the picture is different, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expecting growth of 7% this year.\par \par \uc2 "His world view is when the economy is recovering and we're in good shape ... there's less need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But there will be pain for exporters," said Katz, predicting dollar-shekel likely at 3 by year-end.\par \par \uc2 Katz likens Israel's booming high-tech sector to the country's natural gas sector in 2013, when the BoI also bought dollars of try to offset the sector's impact on the surging shekel. Without sufficient offset for that one sector, other exporters suffer, he said.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, Yaron seemed to be following the route set by his predecessors whose \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigns have pushed BoI reserves to a record 47% of GDP. After buying $21 billion last year, the BoI announced up to $30 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases for 2021.\par \par \uc2 But last week, Yaron called the $30 billion programme, which ended on Oct. 27 when it hit that level, an "exceptional plan for exceptional times". BoI said it would not extend the programme, instead reverting back to unannounced \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  when deemed economically necessary.\par \par \uc2 EXPORTER CONCERN\par \par \uc2 Modi Shafrir, chief strategist at \uc2 Mizrahi Tefahot Bank\uc2 's finance division, expects \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to decline in 2022, given \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2.5% inflation print in September -- towards the upper end of its annual 1%-3% target.\par \par \uc2 "At the beginning of 2021, we had deflation and ... now, we are in a different, very different area," Shafrir said. "The shekel appreciation slightly reduces the inflation coming from the supply side."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has created a process that allowed the economy to gradually adapt and move to a services focus, away from production, Yaron told reporters last week, adding:\par \par \uc2 "The monetary policy committee looks at exporters but also at importers and consumers. The macroeconomic picture of the economy emerging from the crisis a good one."\par \par \uc2 Unannounced \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- and there have been a few recently according to traders -- prevent episodes of excessive strength while allowing gradual gains, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  wrote, predicting "continued, gradual shekel appreciation". They forecast a 3.05 rate in six months.\par \par \uc2 Israel's high-value tech exports, comprising 14% of GDP, are relatively insulated from exchange rate swings, but Yaron's comments and actions have not sat well with other exporters.\par \par \uc2 Israel Chemicals\uc2  for instance said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength hurt operating profit by $17 million in the third quarter over a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Ron Tomer, head of Israel's Manufacturers' Association, said a third of manufacturers had shifted some production overseas "and it keeps on growing".\par \par \uc2 "Until the latest shekel move, a lot of companies were selling at cost or even at small losses," Tomer told Reuters. "Now with the shekel at 3.10 ... we reached a point in which people can no longer dip into pocket money."\par \par \uc2 He urged policymakers to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the government to step in, because "when you have the shekel strengthening 3% in a week, you can't sustain any plan".\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Steven Scheer; Additional reporting by Karin Strohecker in London; Editing by Sujata Rao and Alison Williams) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-11-9T08:00:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020211109ehb9011vy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
