\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention \highlight\uc2  can't stop strong shekel -  \highlight22\uc2 Israel \highlight\uc2  FinMin\b0\par\par\uc2 448 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2021\par 13:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, Oct 4 (Reuters) - Israel's finance minister said on Monday the country's robust economy was a double-edged sword and is keeping the shekel too strong with little recourse to reverse its course.\par \par \uc2 While presenting the 2021-2022 state budget draft to parliament's finance committee, \uc2 Avigdor Lieberman\uc2  pointed to tax collection that has exceeded expectations, strong quarterly earnings and big overall gains in exports that have widened Israel's current account surplus.\par \par \uc2 "On the one hand, it is very encouraging. On the other hand, the shekel is too strong and it harms industrial exports," Lieberman said. "No hedging or \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can help with this."\par \par \uc2 The shekel has been holding steady at around a rate of 3.22 per dollar, having gained nearly 3% the past six months. It is largely flat since the start of 2021 despite foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of $27 billion the first eight months of the year -- which pushed Israel's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to more than $205 billion.\par \par \uc2 In January, when the shekel hit its strongest level since April 1996 at 3.11 per dollar, \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2  said it would buy $30 billion of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2021, up from $21 billion last year. Policymakers have since said they would buy more than $30 billion if needed.\par \par \uc2 Parliament last month gave its initial nod to the state budget, its first spending package in more than three years amid two years of political stalemate and four elections.\par \par \uc2 Final votes by lawmakers are expected in November.\par \par \uc2 Israel emerged from a third COVID-19 lockdown in March and the economy has rebounded sharply, posting an annualised 16.6% growth in the second quarter over the prior three months.\par \par \uc2 It has since shied away from further lockdowns, even with the spread of the highly infectious Delta variant, counting on vaccine booster shots to keep the pandemic in check. Nearly 40% of the population have received a third vaccine dose.\par \par \uc2 Lieberman used Australia and New Zealand as examples where lockdowns came up short.\par \par \uc2 "Lockdowns don't work. They harm the economy," he said, adding that the crisis has already cost Israel 137 billion shekels ($43 billion) not including loss of revenue, while the latest wave of the Delta variant has cost 4.1 billion shekels.\par \par \uc2 Official data earlier in the day showed that the broadest measure of unemployment was 7.9% in the first half of September. The economy is expected to grow more than 5% in 2021. ($1 = 3.2159 shekels) (Reporting by Steven Scheer, editing by Ed Osmond) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-10-4T12:17:41.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020211004eha401leq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
