\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Israel \highlight\uc2  to continue  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  in 2021, cenbank chief says\b0\par\par\uc2 545 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Dezember 2020\par 15:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 By Steven Scheer\par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, Dec 14 (Reuters) - Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor \uc2 Amir Yaron\uc2  on Monday played down the strength of the shekel, pointing instead to a broadly weak dollar, but he said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the shekel's appreciation got too steep.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stands at a 12-1/2-year peak of 3.25 per dollar after gaining more than 6% in 2020. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bought some $17 billion of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this year - $1.9 billion coming in November - to help push its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to a record $167 billion.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 The Bank of Israel\uc2  intends to continue the policy in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market also in 2021 and to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the amount necessary to prevent (shekel) appreciation from continuing beyond what is mandated by economic fundamentals," Yaron told the annual Eli Hurvitz economic conference.\par \par \uc2 Following his speech, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought "hundreds of millions" of dollars in its latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a markets source told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Yaron said the dollar's weakness is a "global phenomenon" and the greenback has declined against most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  while \uc2 Bank of Israel\uc2  policies helped prevent a steeper shekel appreciation.\par \par \uc2 He pointed to a more moderate gain in the shekel versus the euro, as well as in a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Israel's largest trading partners.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, a higher current account surplus in Israel's balance of payments caused by the COVID-19 crisis, which crimped imports and sent energy prices tumbling, also helped the shekel appreciate.\par \par \uc2 "The reasons that led to the strengthening of the shekel are mostly good," Yaron said, citing years of current account surpluses, large foreign capital inflows into Israeli technology firms and higher global holdings of Israeli government bonds.\par \par \uc2 While imports fell, exports have continued to grow with the exception of tourism services, he said.\par \par \uc2 Still, despite solid exports so far, "a rapid shekel appreciation may hurt export performance and the industries that produce import substitutes later - making it difficult for the economy to recover from the crisis," said Yaron, who reiterated a call for the government to quickly approve a 2021 budget.\par \par \uc2 Israel's economy is forecast to shrink 4.5% in 2020 but grow as much as 6% in 2021, depending on the impact of the virus.\par \par \uc2 Yaron said that while Israel's economy entered the crisis in good shape, with a tight labour market, and the damage has been less than in other countries, the job market will take time to recover even after the economy does, with the broad unemployment rate stabilizing at around 14%.\par \par \uc2 He pointed to data showing that more than 200,000 people have been looking for work for four months, raising "concern that it will be particularly difficult for these unemployed to return to the employment cycle".\par \par \uc2 Eran Yaacov, acting director of the Finance Ministry, said at the conference that Israel's economy must become more competitive. He said the ministry plans to introduce at least 40 reforms "that should accelerate the economy significantly." (Reporting by Steven Scheer; Editing by Jeffrey Heller and Mark Heinrich)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-12-17T14:06:03.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020201214egce01ufp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw77\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1155\pichgoal600
