\par\par \b \uc2 Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Israel \highlight\uc2  to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  in bond,  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  markets as needed, governor says\b0\par\par\uc2 228 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 2020\par 14:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, June 14 (Reuters) - \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the government bond and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to help the economy recover from impacts of the coronavirus crisis, Governor \uc2 Amir Yaron\uc2  said on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Yaron, in a speech at an economic conference, also said that the low interest rate will continue to serve as "the main tool" for retuning inflation to within the 1-3% target range.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held its benchmark rate at 0.1% in its last decision on May 25. Inflation for April stood at a -0.6% rate.\par \par \uc2 Israel is facing an estimated budget deficit of at least 10% of gross domestic product in 2020 and Yaron said that as long as it was a direct result of the crisis, financial markets will allow the state to "continue financing the deficit at low interest rates."\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 The Bank of Israel\uc2  will continue to purchase government bonds, and if necessary, it will take additional steps to directly support the credit market," he said.\par \par \uc2 In May, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $2.6 billion of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb a strengthening shekel, while also continuing a programme to purchase 50 billion shekels of government bonds. ($1 = 3.4750 shekels) (Reporting by Ari Rabinovitch Editing by Steven Scheer)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-6-17T12:54:27.000Z \