\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Israel intervention \highlight\uc2  needs to be bold to halt shekel gains\b0\par\par\uc2 667 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2019\par 09:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Repeats to additional clients story that ran Tuesday; no change in text.)\par \par \uc2 By Steven Scheer\par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, Dec 11 (Reuters) - \uc2 The Bank of Israel\uc2  will need to be aggressive to contain the shekel's rise and push inflation higher through another round of foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases.\par \par \uc2 The shekel has gained more than 7% this year against the dollar, making it one of the world's strongest \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and 9% versus a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of main trading partners, helping to push inflation to near zero but hurting exports.\par \par \uc2 Analysts doubt the trend will change. Israel's economy is growing around 3% a year, the country maintains a current account surplus, foreign investment flows are robust and another large natural gas site is set to begin production.\par \par \uc2 Economists expect dollar-shekel to hold between 3.4 and 3.5 per dollar in 2020, as long as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains aggressive. It stood at 3.465 per dollar on Tuesday, unchanged from two weeks ago and mainly helped by the bank buying $1.3 billion of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  the last week of November and unknown amounts this month.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Abir, head of \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2 's market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  department, told Reuters last week that interest rate reductions remain on the table, but \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is currently the best way to lift inflation and boost Israel's economy\par \par \uc2 Israel's benchmark rate remains at 0.25%. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is reluctant to lower it to 0.1%, zero or even negative rates and hopes buying large amounts of dollars works.\par \par \uc2 "Are they going to change the long-term fundamental trend of the shekel? No. But are they going to be able to put speculators out of the market? Maybe. That's pretty much they way they look at it," said Neil Corney, country manager of Citi \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So far, its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, which began moments after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held its key rate, defying market expectations, has largely held.\par \par \uc2 BOUGHT $94 BILLION SINCE 2008\par \par \uc2 But for it to continue to work, \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2  will need to keep spending, since for most of 2018 and 2019, policymakers let the shekel appreciate. Only when inflation fell to 0.3% by September from 1.5% in May, did the bank -- which seeks to get inflation within an annual target of 1% to 3% -- decide to act.\par \par \uc2 "The turnaround in inflation in the summer was a very explicit signal to them," said Mai Doan, an economist at Bank of America. "They have a dual mandate of inflation and growth, and at this stage there is a risk to both mandates, and so they are under pressure to act ... If you look at the past experience it (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) has to be something quite bold."\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bought some $94 billion of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since it began \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March 2008 to prevent a rapid shekel appreciation, bringing its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to more than $122 billion. Since 2006, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket has appreciated more than 50%.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say that since \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a short-term tool, it cannot work over time.\par \par \uc2 "If they don't do smart \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ... the speculators are going to eat \uc2 the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  up," Bank Leumi Chief Economist Gil Bufman said.\par \par \uc2 "They are going to have to constantly surprise the market with days of large sums of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and then no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all and being in the market at all hours of the day, because a lot of speculation happens when market liquidity is thin."\par \par \uc2 Bufman doubted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will fail to push up inflation, because the excess cash remains in the banking system. "It's not generating an increase in cash in the hands of the public and it's not generating a surge in consumption that will raise prices," he said. (Reporting by Steven Scheer, editing by Larry King)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-12-14T08:00:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020191211efcb00xpx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
