\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Israel \highlight\uc2  cenbank prefers  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\uc2  to rate cuts for now - official\b0\par\par\uc2 715 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2019\par 16:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, Nov 28 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is currently the most suitable way to lift inflation and boost Israel's economy, although interest rate cuts remain on the table, a voting member of \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2 's monetary policy committee (MPC) said.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Abir, head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  department, said that at an annual rate of 0.4% in October, inflation remained well below a target of 1-3% and further appreciation of the shekel would make it more difficult to return inflation to its band.\par \par \uc2 "In the existing environment, what we thought in the recent meeting was the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tool is presently more suitable than further cuts in the interest rate or using other tools," Abir told Reuters on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Israel\uc2  on Monday held its benchmark rate at 0.25% for the eighth decision in a row, confounding expectations among analysts that it would cut borrowing costs for the first time in nearly five years.\par \par \uc2 Analysts figured the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would reduce the rate to 0.1% because the shekel had appreciated more than 7% versus the dollar in 2019 and 9% against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , helping to push inflation lower. At the prior meeting on October 7, policymakers voted 3-2 in favour of holding rates.\par \par \uc2 "The decision (this week) was not a decision not to reduce interest rates but for the moment acting in the exchange market may be more suitable. But we can always go back to reducing rates," Abir said.\par \par \uc2 "Its not something we've taken off the table but for the moment there are other things we can do first that will possibly have more impact," he said, adding rate moves have a long lag on impacting the economy.\par \par \uc2 For most of 2018 and 2019, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  let the shekel appreciate but with inflation falling to a rate of 0.3% by September from 1.5% in May, the bank decided to act -- buying $314 million of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in October and many hundreds of millions in the last few sessions following the rate decision.\par \par \uc2 "There is limited room on interest rates while in theory there is unlimited room on acquiring U.S. dollars ... you can do as much as you like," he said.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, "the barrier to reducing rates below zero is much higher than reducing it to zero or 0.1%," he said.\par \par \uc2 VOLATILITY\par \par \uc2 He noted that for more than a year and a half, policymakers were "comfortable" with the exchange rate and did not deviate from a "wide" window where moves are justified by fundamentals.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not occur, he added, "if it just deviates a few percent."\par \par \uc2 The shekel has gain for a host of factors, including solid economic growth and a current account surplus. "Nine percent is a large move in a period when the dollar has been the strongest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world except for ourselves," Abir said.\par \par \uc2 The shekel on Thursday afternoon stood at 3.468 per dollar, after reached 3.4550 last week, its strongest since March 2018.\par \par \uc2 Abir said global central banks like the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and \uc2 ECB\uc2  have signalled a pause for now on further monetary easing.\par \par \uc2 "We want to prevent it from continuing to appreciate at the pace it's been doing and we want to introduce volatility in the market," Abir said, referring to the shekel. "We want two-way risk in the market. We want people to be worried that there may be times we reappear in the market suddenly."\par \par \uc2 He noted the strong shekel continues to weigh on goods exports, even as service exports were strong. At the same time, the economy, growing around 3% a year, could take a hit from political uncertainty after two inconclusive elections in Israel and the indictment of Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  on corruption charges.\par \par \uc2 "The continuation of the caretaker government and its inability to implement a new budget or new economic planning will have a detrimental effect over the medium term," Abir said. "The longer the present impasse carries on, the more worrying it will be." (Reporting by Steven Scheer, Editing by William Maclean) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-11-28T16:00:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020191128efbs02fi1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
