\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Bank of Israel holds key rate, cautions on political uncertainty\b0\par\par\uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2019\par 15:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Most analysts had expected first rate cut since 2015\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says taking steps to make policy more accommodative\par \par \uc2 * Shekel up 7.5% vs dollar, up 9% vs \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket so far in '19\par \par \uc2 * Inflation rate stands at 0.4% (Adds shekel reaction, analyst comment)\par \par \uc2 By Steven Scheer and Ari Rabinovitch\par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, Nov 25 (Reuters) - \uc2 The Bank of Israel\uc2  left its benchmark interest rate at 0.25% on Monday for the eighth decision in a row, confounding expectations among analysts and investors that it would cut borrowing costs for the first time in nearly five years.\par \par \uc2 The bank said economic growth was solid but warned that political uncertainty may ultimately harm the economy.\par \par \uc2 Ten of 16 economists polled by Reuters had predicted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would lower the key rate to 0.1% as the strong shekel is helping to keep inflation close to zero. Six others forecast no change.\par \par \uc2 "It will be necessary to leave the interest rate at its current level for a prolonged period or to reduce it in order to support a process at the end of which inflation will stabilize around the midpoint of the target range, and so that the economy will continue to grow strongly," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement. It said its analysis was based on a host of local and global factors.\par \par \uc2 "The (monetary policy) committee is taking additional steps as necessary to make monetary policy more accommodative," it added -- a likely reference to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem shekel strength.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gained 7.5% so far in 2019 against the dollar and some 9% versus a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of main trading partners.\par \par \uc2 Annual inflation edged up to a rate of 0.4% in October -- well below the government's 1-3% target -- from 0.3% in September but it was down from a peak of 1.5% in May.\par \par \uc2 "The (shekel's) appreciation continues to make it difficult to return inflation to the target range," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $314 million of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in October, its first major \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2019.\par \par \uc2 POLITICAL UNCERTAINTY\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy continues to grow at around 3% percent, which is considered the country's potential, while the labour market remains tight, the bank said.\par \par \uc2 "However, the political situation remains uncertain, and if the government is forced to operate on a continuance budget for a prolonged period, it may have a contractionary effect."\par \par \uc2 The Israeli economy has so far weathered two inconclusive elections and a year of successive caretaker governments. But the political stalemate means it will be well into 2020 before a new annual budget is passed, triggering months of cutbacks that are likely to drag on economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  was indicted last week on corruption charges, plunging Israel into further political disarray after neither he nor his main challenger, centrist \uc2 Benny Gantz\uc2 , secured a parliamentary majority in April and September elections.\par \par \uc2 Until a new budget is passed, government ministries will be funded according to their 2019 monthly allocations, making it harder to commission new roads, pay contractors and cover a growing deficit.\par \par \uc2 The shekel was flat against the dollar after the decision after gaining as much as 0.5% earlier.\par \par \uc2 Analysts still believe the chance of a rate cut in 2020 remains high. The next decision will be on Jan. 9.\par \par \uc2 "The likelihood of interest rate reductions in the coming months as well as \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases is still significant, especially in the event that we return and see a rapid appreciation of the shekel," said Ofer Klein, head of economics and research at Harel Insurance and Finance. (Additional reporting by Tova Cohen; Editing by Catherine Evans) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-11-25T15:59:27.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020191125efbp02gnp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
