\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Affirms Israel At 'AA-/A-1+'; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 3,528 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2019\par 22:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1 Feb 2019 16:08 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Israel At 'AA-/A-1+'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- We expect Israel's economic growth will remain resilient in the face \par of softer global growth. \par      -- Despite a widening budgetary deficit in 2019, we believe that the new \par government will ensure net public debt remains below 60% of GDP. \par      -- Heightened external security risks emanating from Syria and Iran will \par weigh on the country's creditworthiness. \par      -- We are affirming our 'AA-/A-1+'ratings on Israel. \par      -- The outlook is stable. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On Feb. 1, 2019, S&P Global Ratings affirmed its 'AA-/A-1+' long- and \par short-term foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on Israel. The \par outlook is stable. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The stable outlook on Israel balances external and security risks against \par Israel's solid economic growth prospects. The outlook also factors in our view \par that Israel's net creditor position will remain about 35% of GDP over our \par forecast horizon, providing the economy with substantial buffers in the event \par of an external shock. \par  \par We could take a negative rating action if Israel's economic, \par balance-of-payments, or fiscal performance weakened markedly beyond our \par forecast, or if security risks increased substantially. \par  \par A positive rating action could stem from strong fiscal consolidation efforts \par that result in a material reduction in net general government debt or interest \par payments, or a major and unexpected improvement in the Middle East's security \par environment. \par  \par RATIONALE \par The ratings are supported by Israel's prosperous and diverse economy, strong \par external balance sheet, and flexible monetary policy framework. The ratings \par are constrained by Israel's moderately high public debt burden and, in our \par view, significant security and geopolitical risks. \par  \par Institutional and Economic Profile: A wealthy economy and effective \par institutions support prudent macroeconomic policies \par      -- We project Israel's diversified, competitive, and resilient economy \par will experience average growth of about 3% over the medium term. \par      -- We do not expect major policy shifts after the new government is \par formed following the early election in April 2019. \par      -- High exposure to external and domestic security risks weigh on the \par country's creditworthiness. \par  \par Israel's economy continues to thrive and benefit from diversification, with \par high-value-added manufacturing and services sectors, especially in the \par information technology industry. The information and communication sector \par contributes over 8% of the gross value added, and scientific and technical \par activities about 3%. This is underpinned by sizable expenditures on research \par and development, exceeding 4.5% of GDP on average--the highest among member \par countries of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Organization for Economic Co-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Development\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par (\f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  ). \par  \par We expect Israel's economy will expand by slightly over 3% on average through \par 2022. Growth will stem from private consumption on the back of a strong labor \par market, continued corporate investment activity (not least in the hi-tech \par sector), and the robust performance of services exports. We expect growth will \par benefit from an additional boost in 2020 as a result of the large Leviathan \par gas field and Intel  projects coming online, and then will moderate to 3% over \par the long term, broadly in line with labor and productivity trends. Even though \par we revised our growth forecast for 2019 to 3.2% from 3.5% owing to expected \par moderation of Israel's key trading partners' economic growth, we note that \par levels still remain elevated by \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2   standards and that the projected growth \par comes on top of Israel's already vigorous economic performance. The economy \par has not faced recession in the last 15 years, and GDP in U.S. dollar terms has \par increased by over 55% since 2010, with the unemployment rate remaining at \par historical lows. \par  \par Israel's domestic political situation remains highly fragmented, resulting in \par the break-up of the existing coalition in late 2018 and a subsequent snap \par election due in April 2019. Although it is difficult to forecast the next \par government's composition, public opinion polls suggest only a modest change is \par expected, if any. Even if election outcomes trigger elevated tensions \par regarding the government's formation, and result in challenging budget \par discussions in late 2019, we note that political fragmentation in the past has \par not undermined Israel's commitment to prudent macroeconomic policies, which \par remains included in our base-case assumptions. By international comparisons \par and based on policy outcomes, we view institutional and governance structures \par in Israel as generally effective. \par  \par That said, political polarization could constrain the government's capacity to \par address longer-term structural issues in the economy and society. These \par include excessive red tape, infrastructure gaps, weak labor market \par participation and poor skills of some social groups (mainly Haredi men and \par Arab-Israeli women), and housing-related matters. We believe that domestic \par political volatility--exemplified by recent amendments to basic laws and \par proposals to reshape the Supreme Court's powers--will likely stay elevated \par over the rating horizon. \par  \par In addition, Israel is exposed to persistent geopolitical risks. The announced \par withdrawal of the U.S. forces from Syria, Israel's neighbor on the northern \par border, and the Syrian regime's consolidation of its position, could lead to \par elevated risk of open military tensions with Iran and Iranian-supported \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hezbollah\f28 \uc2 \f2  and other militant groups actively involved in Syria. Russian \par support for the Syrian regime complicates the issue further, despite Russia \par and Israel maintaining broadly cordial relations. \par  \par Even though we expect the U.S. administration will stay committed to \par supporting Israel if regional security risks heighten, any significant armed \par conflict could adversely affect the ratings on Israel, since it would likely \par undermine business confidence and weaken economic growth potential, or could \par result in immediate budgetary pressures. \par  \par We also note continued divergence between the U.S. and other stakeholders \par (notably the EU ) with regards to the peace process with Palestinian \par authorities, especially after the U.S. moved its embassy to Jerusalem. This \par might lead to repeated outbreaks of violence in Gaza and surrounding areas, \par and could trigger a backlash from the international community. \par  \par Flexibility and Performance Profile: Fiscal performance in 2019 will weaken, \par but risk to fiscal stability is contained \par      -- Past pro-cyclical fiscal decisions will likely widen fiscal deficits \par in 2019 to above 3% of GDP, but net public debt is likely to stay below 60% of \par GDP. \par      -- Israel's substantial net external asset position remains a key credit \par strength. \par      -- Monetary policy effectiveness is high, with real estate price dynamics \par posing a key challenge. \par  \par Exceptionally favorable macroeconomic conditions, one-off fiscal revenues, and \par exchange-rate appreciation have supported Israel's public finances in recent \par years. In 2015-2017, the government exceeded its deficit targets, setting \par public debt on a downward trend. Despite a strong headline performance, \par however, the underlying fiscal stance has been pro-cyclical, with a number of \par tax and expenditure measures contributing to widening structural deficits. \par With the business cycle now maturing, we expect general government deficits \par will increase somewhat in 2019 to 3.3% of GDP, resulting in a modest pick-up \par of gross public debt as a share of GDP. \par  \par In our view, Israel's weaker headline performance does not pose risks to \par macroeconomic stability. This is because government debt is now much lower \par than in the past, having declined by over 10 percentage points in the last 10 \par years to an estimated 61.1% of GDP in 2018. At the same time, given elevated \par security risks, the Israeli government might require larger fiscal buffers \par relative to similarly rated peers. In this respect, one of the challenges \par facing the new government will be to re-establish the declining trajectory of \par public debt, while at the same time keeping cost containment measures \par growth-friendly. \par  \par Although downside risks remain, especially if nominal GDP growth is weaker \par then we anticipate, our base-line scenario assumes that headline fiscal \par deficits will decline to less than 3% of GDP from 2020. We base this view on: \par  \par      -- Political consensus on containing public debt, resulting in, we \par assume, a reasonable degree of fiscal discipline, anchored by compliance with \par existing fiscal rules (i.e. the "numerator" rule) and a multiyear spending \par agreement with the defense ministry (the source of fiscal slippages in the \par past); and \par      -- Expected proactive fiscal consolidation measures by the new government \par that would accommodate new spending proposals, including those related to the \par recently announced goal of enhancing public infrastructure (i.e. long-term \par national infrastructure strategy), without compromising fiscal stability. \par  \par Accordingly, we expect net general government debt (that is, gross debt net of \par liquid government assets, mainly in the form of deposits at \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Israel\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par [\f28 \uc2 \f2 BOI\f28 \uc2 \f2 ; the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ]) will stay below 60% of GDP through our forecast \par horizon. \par  \par Strong export performance and the ongoing development of Israel's offshore \par natural gas fields, with significant export capacity, support the country's \par strong external profile. Although we expect Israel's current account \par performance will weaken somewhat due to the strength of domestic demand and \par real effective exchange rate appreciation, which will weigh on the trade \par balance, we assume that resilient high-value-added services exports will keep \par the current account in surplus. This will enhance Israel's position as a net \par creditor versus the rest of the world, with liquid external assets exceeding \par gross external debt by over 50% of current account payments. Israel's net \par external asset position is in the 15 strongest from 133 sovereigns we rate \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1 Feb 2019 16:08 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Israel At -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par globally. These dynamics also contain the country's gross external financing \par needs (payments to nonresidents), indicating low dependence on external \par financing. \par  \par We consider Israel's monetary policy flexibility a credit strength. With the \par output gap closing and unemployment rate at historical lows, headline \par inflation since mid-2018 has been at the lower end of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOI\f28 \uc2 \f2 's 1%-3% target. \par This prompted the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to hike its policy rate last November for the \par first time since 2011. At the same time, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOI\f28 \uc2 \f2 's policy stance remains \par accommodative, not least due to the need to counter the strength of the shekel \par to maintain the competitiveness of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Israel\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's exports. In recent years, \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOI\f28 \uc2 \f2  has \par also \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, over and above its commitment to \par purchase foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to offset the impact of domestic natural gas \par production on the balance of payments. Therefore, we view the exchange rate \par regime as a managed float, which somewhat hampers monetary policy flexibility, \par in our view. \par  \par Despite some weakening of the shekel against the U.S. dollar in 2018 (driven \par in particular by higher rates in the U.S.), we expect Israel's strong \par fundamentals, namely its current account surpluses, strong net foreign direct \par investment inflows, and high GDP growth rates will likely lead to renewed \par shekel appreciation over the long term. The exchange rate will continue to \par pose pricing risk, adding to the need for continued innovation and reduction \par of regulatory pressures for local businesses to remain competitive in external \par markets. \par  \par One of the key challenges to monetary policy continues to be rising house \par prices. Real house prices have increased by over 100% since the end of 2007. \par The \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOI\f28 \uc2 \f2 's past attempts to dampen the housing market by raising interest rates \par delivered limited results. Thereafter, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOI\f28 \uc2 \f2  shifted focus to a series of \par macroprudential measures targeted at the mortgage market. More recently, the \par government has implemented comprehensive measures to cut speculative demand \par and increase the housing supply, including freeing up more land for \par development, changing the tendering criteria, allowing foreign presence in the \par construction market, and accelerating processes for construction permissions. \par Given capacity constraints, relatively low productivity in the construction \par industry, and continued growth in demand, addressing the supply shortage might \par take time, however. \par  \par Israeli banks' exposure to the local real estate sector, mainly to residential \par mortgage loans, has increased in recent years. We estimate the banking \par system's current exposure to loans for construction, commercial real estate, \par and mortgages at over 45% of total bank loans compared with 32% 10 years ago. \par The housing market seems to have cooled off, with real housing price growth \par slowing in 2017-2018 to below an estimated 2%, from elevated levels of 6% in \par 2012-2016. At the same time, the combination of supply constraints and \par economic and population growth will continue to drive moderate house price \par appreciation, in our view. Even though the tightening of macroprudential \par measures has reduced systemic risks to Israel's banking industry, any abrupt \par correction in house prices could still weigh on the economy (for more detail \par on financial stability risks and the Israeli banking system see "Banking \par Industry Country Risk Assessment: Israel," published Nov. 26, 2018, on \par RatingsDirect). \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RATINGS SCORE SNAPSHOT \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par  \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Use Of CreditWatch And Outlooks, Sept. 14, 2009 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments, May 18, 2009 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Global Sovereign Rating Trends 2019, Jan. 14, 2019 \par      -- Emerging Markets Sovereign Rating Trends 2019. Jan. 14, 2019 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings List, Jan. 7, 2019 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings Score Snapshot, Jan. 7, 2019 \par      -- S&P Global Ratings Lowers Brent And WTI Oil Price Assumptions For 2019 \par Through 2020. Jan 3, 2019 \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators, Dec. 13, 2018; a free interactive version \par is available at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.spratings.com/sri }}}\uc2 \f2  \par      -- Banking Industry Country Risk Assessment: Israel," Nov. 26, 2018 \par      -- Default, Transition, And Recovery: 2017 Annual Sovereign Default Study \par And Rating Transitions, May 8, 2018 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to \par make an informed decision. \par  \par After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and explained the \par recommendation, the Committee discussed key rating factors and critical issues \par in accordance with the relevant criteria. Qualitative and quantitative risk \par factors were considered and discussed, looking at track-record and forecasts. \par  \par