\par\par \b \uc2 Remarks by Andrew Abir, Director of the Bank of Israel\u8217\'20\'19s Market  \highlight22\uc2 Operations \highlight\uc2  Department: \u8216\'20\'18Monetary Policy in the Era of Low Inflation\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2018\par \uc2 ENP Newswire\par \uc2 ENPNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2018, Electronic News Publishing. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Release date - 08022018\par \par \uc2 Andrew Abir, Director of the Bank of Israel\u8217\'20\'19s Market \b \highlight22\uc2 Operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  Department, delivered a lecture today at the IBI investment firm, discussing the monetary policy conducted by \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2  during the low inflation era.\par \par \uc2 He mentioned \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s goals as defined in \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2  Law, and the tools with which it can act to achieve its targets. The main tool is, obviously, the interest rate, which has been at the low level of 0.1 percent since 2015 in view of the decline of inflation to below the 1-3 percent target range. Abir mentioned that while the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  began a gradual process of raising interest rates, a number of central banks, chiefly the \uc2 ECB\uc2 , are maintaining a negative interest rate policy. While the interest rate affects short-term yields, other tools can impact long-term bond yields. Similar to other central banks, \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2  uses \u8216\'20\'18forward guidance\u8217\'20\'19, and other central banks have used bond purchasing policies-some still do-which significantly increases their balance sheets. Another tool is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, which also contributes to increases in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s balance sheet. In January 2018, \uc2 the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  significantly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, including after it identified significant selling activity based on models. The major \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  halted the appreciation of the shekel, despite the marked weakness of the US dollar vis-a-vis the other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Is monetary policy succeeding in meeting its targets? The labor market is at full employment, and the economy is growing in line with its potential growth rate. The main transmission mechanism from monetary policy to real activity has, in the past several years, operated mainly through the policy\u8217\'20\'19s effect on private consumption, which has led economic growth. However, the inflation environment remains below the target. Some of the decline in inflation reflects the \u8216\'20\'18import\u8217\'20\'19 of low inflation from abroad which, together with the effect of the shekel\u8217\'20\'19s appreciation, leads to a decline in the prices of tradable goods. In Israel, inflation was also impacted by price reductions initiated by the government, while structural changes that are increasing competition in the market are acting to reduce inflation. These are desirable changes that are affecting price levels, and monetary policy is not trying to act against them or to offset them. However, a prolonged decline of the inflation rate to below the target may have negative ramifications for economic activity over time, and \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s monetary policy is therefore expected to remain accommodative as long as necessary in order to entrench the inflation environment within the target range.\par \par \uc2 Abir discussed the gaps in consumer price levels between Israel and other \uc2 OECD\uc2  countries, and noted that there is a high correlation between the level of the real exchange rate and the price gap. Currently, the shekel is appreciated, so price levels in Israel are higher than abroad, since the appreciation lowers prices abroad in shekel terms more than prices in the domestic economy, where it only affects the prices of tradable goods. A real depreciation may narrow the price gap, as it was, for instance, in the middle of the previous decade.\par \par \uc2 [Editorial queries for this story should be sent to newswire@enpublishing.co.uk]\par \par \uc2 Dokument ENPNEW0020180209ee29000j7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
