\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Affirms Israel At 'A+/A-1'; Outlook Still Positive\b0\par\par\uc2 3,444 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Februar 2018\par 22:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 2 Feb 2018 16:08 ET *S&P Affirms Israel at 'A+/A-1'; Outlook Still Positive\par \par \uc2 2 Feb 2018 16:08 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Israel At 'A+/A-1'; Outlook Still Positive\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par  \par Ratings: \par Foreign and Local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : A+/Positive/A-1 \par  \par For further details see ratings list. \par  \par OVERVIEW \par  \par      -- We expect Israel's economic growth will average 3% over the next four \par years, while the balance of payments performance stays strong. \par      -- At the same time, we expect net general government debt will average \par about 58% of GDP through to year-end 2021. \par      -- We are affirming our 'A+/A-1' ratings on Israel. \par      -- The outlook is positive. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par  \par On Feb. 2, 2018, S&P Global Ratings affirmed its 'A+/A1' long and shortterm \par foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on Israel. The outlook \par remains positive. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par  \par The positive outlook on Israel reflects our opinion that, despite government \par spending pressures, there is a potential for stronger-than-anticipated \par budgetary performance. We also expect that Israel's economic and \par balance-of-payments dynamics will stay strong while security risks remain \par contained. \par  \par We could raise our ratings during the next 18 months if the government's fiscal \par performance strengthens, resulting in a discernible decline in net government \par debt as a percentage of GDP. \par  \par We could revise the outlook to stable if the government's budgetary performance \par were significantly weaker than in 2015-2017, economic growth or \par balance-of-payments performance were weaker than our forecast, or if domestic \par or external security risks increased substantially. \par  \par RATIONALE \par  \par The ratings are supported by Israel's prosperous and diverse economy, strong \par external balance sheet, and flexible monetary framework. The ratings are \par constrained by Israel's relatively moderate debt burden and, in our view, \par significant security and geopolitical risks. \par  \par Institutional and Economic Profile: A wealthy economy and effective \par institutions support fiscal adjustment \par      -- A prosperous, modern, and diversified economy, benefitting from \par relatively high growth rates. \par      -- Strong and accountable institutions despite fragmented domestic \par politics. \par      -- High exposure to external and domestic security risks. \par  \par Israel's economy remains prosperous and diverse, with a high-value-added \par manufacturing and services sectors, especially in the field of information \par technology. The information and communication sector contributes almost 10% of \par the gross value added, and scientific and technical activities about 3%. This \par is underpinned by high expenditure on research and development, exceeding 4% of \par GDP on average, the highest among member countries of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Organization for \par Economic Co-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Development\f28 \uc2 \f2  . \par  \par We assume Israel's economy will expand by about 3.1% on average in 20182021. \par Growth will stem from private consumption, continued corporate investment \par activity, and strong performance of services exports, supported by easing \par monetary policy. In per capita terms, this equates to around 1.2% per year, \par reflecting continued population growth. \par  \par We note that the projected growth comes on top of Israel's already remarkable \par economic performance since the global financial crisis started in 2008. For \par example, GDP in U.S. dollar terms has increased by 50% compared with that in \par 2010, and the unemployment rate is at historical lows. \par  \par Overall, we view institutional and governance structures in Israel to be \par generally effective. Despite the highly fragmented political situation in the \par parliament, the ruling coalition passed the budget for 2017-2018, and we expect \par the 2019 budget will be passed smoothly. Moreover, over the past few years, \par we've observed increased commitment to fiscal prudence, with the government \par meeting its budgetary objectives. \par  \par Yet, in our view, the coalition's diverse composition could constrain the \par government's capacity to address longer-term structural issues of the economy \par and society. These issues include excessive red tape, infrastructure gaps, weak \par labor market participation, poor skills of some social groups (mainly Haredi \par men and Arab-Israeli women), and housing-related issues. \par  \par We also see persistent geopolitical risks. Major outbreaks of violence toward \par the Palestinians could not only inflict social and economic costs, but also \par lead to a backlash from the international community. On the northern border, \par the conflict in Syria and Iraq, as well as potential tensions with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hezbollah\f28 \uc2 \f2  , \par pose a medium-term security threat. The new U.S. administration seems committed \par to supporting Israel in case security risks escalate. Yet we anticipate \par continued divergence between the U.S. and other stakeholders (notably the EU ) \par with regards to the peace process with Palestinian authorities, especially \par after the U.S. took a decision to move its embassy to Jerusalem. Any \par significant armed conflict could have a negative impact on the ratings, since \par it would likely deter investment, weaken the economy's growth potential, or \par result in budgetary pressures. \par  \par Flexibility and Performance Profile: Improved budgetary performance and strong \par external finances \par      -- Budgetary consolidation could continue if accompanied by strong \par economic growth and spending controls. \par      -- External position remains strong on a flow and stock basis. \par      -- Monetary policy effectiveness is high, with real estate price dynamics \par posing a key challenge. \par  \par Exceptionally favorable macroeconomic conditions have supported Israel's public \par finance in recent years. The general government's fiscal deficit shrank to \par about 1.7% of GDP on average in 2015-2017 from an average of 2.5% in 2010-2014, \par while central government deficits averaged 2.1% and 3.3% for the same periods, \par respectively. Fiscal results were again strong in 2017, with the deficit at an \par estimated 2% of GDP against the government's target of 2.9%. Although tax-rich \par consumption growth, one-time non-tax revenues, and low interest rates have been \par key factors behind fiscal consolidation, cost-containment measures have also \par played a role, in our view. Over the past few years, a multi-year spending \par agreement has been reached with the defense ministry--the source of previous \par fiscal slippages. The government has also instituted and generally complied \par with fiscal rules, and tightened control over spending commitments for line \par ministries. \par  \par We see the potential for Israel to outperform our current fiscal forecasts. \par There is a likelihood that solid economic growth, especially if stronger than \par anticipated, could support the government's efforts to reduce the budget \par deficit further. This could, in particular, result from continued \par cost-containment measures to compensate for recent procyclical tax cuts \par (including on personal and corporate income taxes) and public spending hikes. \par Additional revenues from ongoing efforts to downsize tax benefits and tax \par evasion could also benefit Israel's performance. Under this scenario, headline \par general government deficits will likely be contained to about 2% of GDP. As a \par result, general government debt could be put on a noticeably declining path. \par  \par However, in our base-case scenario, we assume central government budget \par deficits will expand moderately to the legislated maximum of 2.9% of GDP as a \par result of expenditure pressure from health care, education, and \par infrastructure--aggravated by potential early elections in 2018--and less \par generous non-tax revenues compared with the last few years. The recently \par proposed draft budget for 2019, in which the government revised its fiscal \par deficit targets upward compared with the projections in the medium-term fiscal \par framework, supports this scenario, in our view. As a result, over 2018-2021, \par average general government deficits will likely stay at around 3% of GDP in \par 2018-2021 and net general government debt (that is, gross debt net of liquid \par government assets, mainly in the form of deposits at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) at just \par under 60% of GDP. \par  \par We estimate that lower headline budget deficits, high nominal GDP growth rates, \par and low inflation (over 50% of Israel's general government debt is linked to \par the consumer price index) pushed Israel's net general government debt down to \par 58.4% of GDP in 2017 from 61.4% in 2015. \par  \par Strong export performance, in particular, booming high-value-added services \par exports, and the ongoing development of Israel's offshore natural gas fields \par with its significant export capacity, support the country's strong external \par profile. This will enhance Israel's position as a net creditor versus the rest \par of the world, with liquid external assets exceeding gross external debt by over \par 50% of current account payments over our entire forecast horizon. These \par dynamics also contain the country's gross external financing needs (payments to \par nonresidents), indicating low dependence on external financing. \par  \par In addition, we consider Israel's monetary policy flexibility to be a credit \par strength. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Israel\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   (\f28 \uc2 \f2 BOI\f28 \uc2 \f2 ; the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) has been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, over and above its commitment to purchase foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to offset the impact of domestic natural gas production on the balance \par of payments. We view the exchange rate regime as a managed float, which \par somewhat hampers monetary policy flexibility, in our view. \par  \par Additionally, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOI\f28 \uc2 \f2  is sticking to accommodative monetary policy, countering \par the strength of the shekel to maintain the competitiveness of Israel's exports. \par It has maintained the historical low of 0.1% as its key policy rate since March \par 2015. Yet, since then, the shekel has continued to appreciate against the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of key trading partners, owing to Israel's strong fundamentals, \par namely its current account surpluses, strong net foreign direct investment, and \par high GDP growth rates. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation was one of the factors for \par negative consumer price inflation in 2015-2016 and only modestly positive \par inflation in 2017. Last year, the shekel appreciated by over 10% in terms of \par the nominal effective exchange rate versus the U.S. dollar, and we expect the \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2 Feb 2018 16:08 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Israel At 'A+/A-1'; -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par moderate appreciation will continue this year. The exchange rate poses pricing \par risk, adding to the need for continued innovation and reduction of regulatory \par pressures for local businesses to remain competitive in external markets, in \par our view. \par  \par One of the key challenges to monetary policy continues to be rising house \par prices. Real house prices have increased by over 100% since the end of 2007. \par The \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOI\f28 \uc2 \f2 's past attempts to dampen the housing market by raising interest rates \par delivered limited results and pushed up the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate. Thereafter, \par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOI\f28 \uc2 \f2  shifted focus to a series of macroprudential measures targeted at the \par mortgage market. More recently, the government has implemented comprehensive \par measures to cut speculative demand and increase the housing supply, including \par freeing up more land for development, changing the tendering criteria, allowing \par foreign presence in the construction market, and speeding up processes for \par construction permissions. Given capacity constraints, relatively low \par productivity in the construction industry, and continued growth in demand, \par addressing the supply shortage might take time, however. \par  \par Israeli banks' exposure to the local real estate sector, mainly to residential \par mortgage loans, has increased in recent years. The banking system's exposure to \par loans for construction, commercial real estate, and mortgages now accounts for \par about 45% of total bank loans compared with 32% 10 years ago. Even though the \par tightening of macroprudential measures has reduced systemic risks to Israel's \par banking industry, and the housing market seems to have cooled in 2017 (real \par housing price growth slowed down to just 2.4% from an average of 6% in \par 2012-2016), any abrupt correction in house prices could still weigh on the \par economy (see \par "Articlehtml \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.capitaliq.com/CIQDotNet/CreditResearch/SPResearch.aspx?ArtObjectId=10298363&ArtRevId=1xt_artcl-mark-hyperlnkOctober"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.capitaliq.com/CIQDotNet/CreditResearch/SPResearch.aspx?ArtObjectId=10298363&ArtRevId=1xt_artcl-mark-hyperlnkOctober }}}\uc2 \f2  \par 30, 2017 06:22:56 AMBanking Industry Country Risk Assessment: Israel," \par published Oct. 30, 2017, on RatingsDirect). \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RATINGS SCORE SNAPSHOT \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA AND RESEARCH \par  \par Related Criteria \par      -- Criteria - Governments - Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology - \par December 18,2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments - May 18,2009 \par      -- General Criteria: Use Of CreditWatch And Outlooks - September 14,2009 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings - April 07,2017 \par  \par Related Research \par      -- Global Sovereign Rating Trends 2018 - Jan. 10, 2018 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History - Jan. 5, 2018 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings List - Jan. 5, 2018 \par      -- Calendar Of 2018 EMEA Sovereign, Regional, And Local Government Rating \par Publication Dates - Dec. 15, 2017 \par      -- Banking Industry Country Risk Assessment: Israel, Oct. 30, 2017 \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators - Dec. 14, 2017, an interactive version is \par available at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/SRI"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.spratings.com/SRI }}}\uc2 \f2  \par      -- Default, Transition, and Recovery: 2016 Annual Sovereign Default Study \par And Rating Transitions - April 3