\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Revises Israel Outlook To Positive; Affirms 'A+/A-1'\b0\par\par\uc2 3,505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2017\par 22:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 4 Aug 2017 16:19 ET *S&P: Israel Outlook Reflects Potential for Stronger Fiscal Performance Over Next 2 Yrs\par \par \uc2 4 Aug 2017 16:19 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Revises Israel Outlook To Positive; Affirms 'A+/A-1'\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2 : \par OVERVIEW \par  \par      -- Israel's fiscal performance has exceeded our expectations, reflecting \par the strength of the underlying economy. \par      -- We expect Israel's economic growth will average 3.2% over the next four \par years, while the country's balance of payments performance stays strong. \par      -- We are therefore revising our outlook on Israel to positive from stable \par and affirming our 'A+/A-1' foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign ratings. \par      -- The positive outlook on Israel reflects our opinion that, despite \par existing spending pressures, there is a potential for stronger-than-anticipated \par fiscal performance over the next two years. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par  \par On Aug. 4, 2017, S&P Global Ratings revised its outlook on the State of Israel \par to positive from stable. At the same time, we affirmed our 'A+/A1' long and \par shortterm foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on Israel. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par  \par The positive outlook on Israel reflects our opinion that, despite existing \par spending pressures, there is a potential for stronger-than-anticipated general \par government fiscal performance over the next two years. We expect that Israel's \par economic and balance-of-payments dynamics will stay strong while security risks \par remain contained. \par  \par We could raise our ratings in the next 24 months if the government makes \par further progress in lowering the public debt burden as a percentage of GDP. \par  \par We could revise the outlook to stable if, over the next 12-24 months, higher \par spending on social or security issues, and/or lower revenues, led to weakening \par budgetary performance of the general government. This would imply the \par stabilization of general government debt over 20172020 broadly in line with \par our base-case scenario. We could also revise the outlook to stable if the \par economy's growth or balance-of-payments performance were weaker than our \par base-case forecasts or if there were a substantial increase in domestic or \par external security risks. \par  \par RATIONALE \par  \par The rating action reflects our opinion that Israel's improved fiscal framework \par and strong economic growth could enable further progress on fiscal \par consolidation over the next few years. Although recent fiscal improvements were \par partly driven by cyclical factors, we believe that institutional measures that \par restrain future expenditure growth could enable the government to resist \par ongoing spending pressures and counterbalance potential revenue \par underperformance due to recent procyclical tax cuts. This scenario is even more \par likely if accompanied by stronger economic growth. Israel's economic \par performance since the global financial crisis has been remarkable, with GDP in \par U.S. dollars now about $100 billion larger than in 2010. What's more, the \par current account is in surplus, and the unemployment rate is the lowest in \par several years. \par  \par Institutional and economic profile: Wealthy economy and effective institutions \par support fiscal adjustment \par  \par      -- A prosperous, modern, and diversified economy, benefiting from \par relatively high growth rates. \par      -- Favorable growth prospects should be conducive to better fiscal \par outcomes. \par      -- Strong and accountable institutions despite fragmented domestic \par politics. \par      -- High exposure to external and domestic security risks. \par  \par Israel's economy remains prosperous and diverse, with high-value-added \par manufacturing and services sectors, especially in the field of information \par technology. The information and communication sector contributes almost 10% of \par the gross value added, and scientific and technical activities around 3%. This \par is underpinned by high expenditures in research and development, exceeding 4% \par of GDP on average, the highest among member countries of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Organization for \par Economic Co-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Development\f28 \uc2 \f2 . We assume Israel's economy will expand \par by about 3.2% on average in 20172020, which is a relatively high rate compared \par with that of peers with similar wealth levels. We note that the projected \par resilient growth comes on top of Israel's already remarkable economic \par performance since the global financial crisis. Specifically, U.S. dollar GDP \par has increased by over 45% compared with that in 2010, and the unemployment rate \par is at historical lows. We expect growth will stem from private consumption, \par continued corporate investment activity, and health services exports, supported \par by relaxed monetary policy. In per capita terms, this equates to somewhat \par weaker growth rates of around 1.2% per year, reflecting robust population \par growth. \par  \par In our view, strong economic prospects could support Israel's fiscal \par performance through higher government revenue intake, despite recent tax cuts. \par In addition, we anticipate that, as both the consumer price index and the GDP \par deflator growth edge toward about 2% on average over our four-year forecast \par horizon, nominal output growth will be notably stronger than real output \par growth. We now believe that the general government's budgetary performance \par (including local authorities) could exceed our present forecasts, owing to \par strong growth and maintenance of fiscal discipline. \par  \par Overall, institutional and governance structures in Israel are generally \par effective, with a satisfactory degree of transparency and accountability. \par Despite highly fragmented domestic politics, the ruling coalition, formed in \par May 2015, passed the biennial budget for 2017-2018 without internal wrangling. \par Over the past few years, we have also observed commitment to fiscal prudence \par and general adherence to existing fiscal rules. However, the coalition's \par structure remains heterogeneous, which--even if there were no early \par elections--may potentially constrain the government's capacity to address \par longer-term structural issues of the economy and society, in our view. These \par issues include excessive red tape, infrastructure gaps, weak labor market \par participation, poor skills of some social groups (mainly Haredi men and \par Arab-Israeli women), high real estate prices, and low availability of housing. \par However, these challenges present a longer-term risk that goes well beyond our \par rating horizon. \par  \par That said, the ratings remain constrained by persistent geopolitical risks. \par Major outbreaks of violence toward the Palestinians could not only inflict \par social and economic costs, but also lead to a backlash from the international \par community. On the northern border, the conflict in Syria and Iraq, as well as \par potential tensions with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hezbollah\f28 \uc2 \f2 , pose a medium-term security threat. The new \par U.S. administration seems committed to supporting Israel in case security risks \par escalate. However, any significant armed conflict could have a negative impact \par on the ratings if it materially deterred investment, weakened the economy's \par growth potential, or strained fiscal flexibility. \par  \par Flexibility and performance profile: High debt is reducing, thanks to improved \par fiscal performance, and the external profile remains very stong \par  \par      -- Increased likelihood the government could cement its recent fiscal \par outperformance due to cyclical and structural factors. \par      -- External position remains strong on a flow and stock basis. \par      -- Monetary policy effectiveness is high. \par  \par Strong tax revenue growth, as well as shrinking interest spending lowered the \par general government's fiscal deficit substantially to about 1.6% of GDP on \par average in 2015-2016 from an average of 2.5% in 2010-2014, while average \par central government deficits stood at 2.1% and 3.3% for the same periods. \par Consumption growth was higher than expected, but cost containment measures have \par also played a role in stronger fiscal outturns, in our view. Over the past few \par years, a multi-year spending agreement has been reached with the defense \par ministry--the source of previous fiscal slippages. The government has also \par instituted and generally complied with fiscal rules, and improved control over \par spending commitments for line ministries, all of which support fiscal \par discipline. \par  \par For this reason, we now see upside potential for our current fiscal forecasts. \par There is an increasing likelihood that solid economic growth could support \par government's efforts to lock in the fiscal overperformance reported over the \par past few years. This could result from continued cost-containment measures to \par compensate for recent tax cuts (including on personal and corporate income \par taxes) and civilian spending hikes. The visibility on such a scenario will \par increase after the expenditure review in November 2017 and during the budget \par cycle for 2019-2020. Additional revenues from higher-than-planned tax revenues \par or ongoing efforts to downsize tax benefits and tax evasion could also benefit \par Israel's performance. Under this scenario, general government deficits are \par likely to be contained to 2.0%-2.1% of GDP, whereas central government deficits \par will stay below existing fiscal targets. \par  \par Such numbers would be stronger than our current base-case forecast, which \par implies a moderate fiscal decline due to increased spending on health, \par education, and infrastructure, aggravated by a potentially early election. As a \par result, average general government deficits will likely approach 3% of GDP in \par 2017-2020 and net general government debt (that is, gross debt net of liquid \par government assets, mainly in the form of deposits at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) will \par stay close to the current level of about 60% of GDP over our forecast period \par through to year-end 2020. High nominal GDP growth rates and low inflation (over \par 50% of Israel's general government debt is linked to the consumer price index) \par pushed Israel's net government debt down to 59.3% of GDP in 2016 compared with \par 61.3% the previous year. \par  \par Strong export performance, in particular, booming high-value-added services \par exports, and the ongoing development of Israel's offshore natural gas fields \par with its significant export capacity, support the country's strong external \par profile. Almost 15 years of current account surpluses have strengthened \par Israel's external balance sheet, turning the country into a net creditor versus \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4 Aug 2017 16:19 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Revises Israel Outlook To -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the rest of the world. We forecast that Israel's liquid external assets will \par continue to outstrip its gross external debt over our entire forecast horizon. \par These dynamics are also lowering the country's gross external financing needs, \par indicating low dependence on external financing. \par  \par In addition, we consider Israel's monetary policy flexibility to be a credit \par strength. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Israel\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (BOI; the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) has been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, over and above its commitment to purchase foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to offset the impact of domestic natural gas production on the balance \par of payment. We view the exchange rate regime as a managed float, which somewhat \par hampers monetary policy flexibility, in our view. \par  \par Additionally, the BOI is sticking to accommodative monetary policy, countering \par the strength of the shekel to maintain the competitiveness of Israel's exports. \par It has maintained the historical low of 0.1% as its key policy rate since March \par 2015. Yet, since then, the shekel has continued to appreciate against the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of key trading partners, owing to Israel's strong fundamentals, \par namely its current account surpluses, strong net foreign direct investment, and \par high GDP growth rates. Over 2016, the shekel appreciated by 3.5% in terms of \par the nominal effective exchange rate, which was one of the factors behind a \par negative consumer price index for the second consecutive year. We expect the \par moderate appreciation will continue in the next few years. The exchange rate \par poses pricing risk, adding to the need for continued innovation and reduction \par of regulatory pressures for local businesses to remain competitive in external \par markets, in our view. \par  \par One of the key challenges to monetary policy continues to be rising house \par prices. After years of relative stability, real house prices have increased by \par over 100% since the end of 2007. The BOI's past attempts to dampen the housing \par market by raising interest rates delivered limited results, only pushing up \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates. The government has implemented a comprehensive set of \par measures to weaken speculative demand and increase housing supply, including \par freeing up more land for development, changing the tendering criteria, allowing \par foreign presence in the construction market, and speeding up processes for \par construction permissions. Given capacity constraints, relatively low \par productivity in the construction industry, and continued growth in demand, \par addressing the supply shortage might take time, however. \par  \par Israeli banks' exposure to the local real estate sector, mainly to residential \par mortgage loans, has grown in recent years. The banking sector's exposure to \par real estate and construction (including residential and commercial construction \par and infrastructure credit) is currently close to 20%, which is the BOI's \par allowed maximum. Even though the tightening of macroprudential measures has \par reduced systemic risks to Israel's banking industry and the housing market \par seems to have cooled early this year, any abrupt correction in house prices \par could still weigh on the economy (see \par "Articlehtml\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.globalcreditportal.com/ratingsdirectshowArticlePage.do?object_id=9914202&rev_id=1xt_artcl-mark-hyperlnkJanuary"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.globalcreditportal.com/ratingsdirectshowArticlePage.do?object_id=9914202&rev_id=1xt_artcl-mark-hyperlnkJanuary}}}\uc2 \f2  \par 02, 2017 08:53:35 AMBanking Industry Country Risk Assessment: Israel," \par published Jan. 2, 2017, on RatingsDirect). \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RATINGS SCORE SNAPSHOT \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA AND RESEARCH \par  \par Related Criteria \par      -- Criteria - Governments - Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology - \par December 23,2014 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments - May 18,2009 \par      -- General Criteria: Use Of CreditWatch And Outlooks - September 14,2009 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings - April 07,2017 \par  \par Related Research \par      -- Global Sovereign Rating Trends Midyear 2017 - July 12, 2017 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History - July 12, 2017 \par      -- Credit Rating Trends: 2016 Sovereign Ratings Update: Outlook And \par CreditWatch Resolutions, April 18, 2017 \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators - July 06, 2017. An interactive version is \par also available at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.spratings.com/sri}}}\uc2 \f2 . \par      -- Default, Transition, and Recovery: 2016 Annual Sovereign Default Study \par And Rating Transitions - April 03, 2017 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to make \par an informed decision. \par  \par After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and explained the \par recommendation, the Committee discussed key rating factors and critical issues \par in accordance with the relevant criteria. Qualitative and quantitative risk \par factors were considered and discussed, looking at track-record and forecasts. \par  \par The committee agreed that debt assessement had improved. All other key rating \par factors were unchanged. \par  \par The chair ensured every voting member was given the opportunity to articulate \par his/her opinion. The chair or designee reviewed the draft report to ensure \par consistency with the Committee decision. The views and the decision of the \par rating committee are summarized in the above rationale and outlook. The \par weighting of all rating factors is described in the methodology used in this \par rating action (see 'Related Criteria And Research'). \par  \par RATINGS LIST \par  \par                                            Rating \par                                            To                  From \par Israel (State of) \par  Sovereign Credit Rating \par   Foreign and Local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                A+/Positive/A-1     A+/Stable/A-1 \par  Transfer & Convertibility Assessment      AA                  AA \par  Senior Unsecured \par   Foreign and Local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                A+                  A+ \par  \par Certain terms used in this report, particularly certain adjectives used to \par express our view on rating relevant factors, have specific meanings ascribed to \par them in our criteria, and should therefore be read in conjunction with such \par criteria. Please see Ratings Criteria at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.standardandpoors.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.standardandpoors.com}}}\uc2 \f2  for further \par information. Complete ratings information is available to subscribers of \par RatingsDirect at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.globalcreditportal.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.globalcreditportal.com}}}\uc2 \f2  and at spcapitaliq.com. All ratings \par affected by this rating action can be found on S&P Global Ratings' public Web \par site at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.standardandpoors.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.standardandpoors.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . Use the Ratings search box located in the \par left column. Alternatively, call one of the following S&P Global Ratings \par numbers: Client Support Europe (44) 20-7176-7176; London Press Office (44) \par 20-7176-3605; Paris (33) 1-4420-6708; Frankfurt (49) 69-33-999-225; Stockholm \par (46) 8-440-5914; or Moscow 7 (495) 783-4009. \par  \par Primary Credit Analyst:  Karen Vartapetov, Frankfurt (49) 69-33-999-225; \par                          karen.vartapetov@spglobal.com \par  \par Secondary Contact:  Alois Strasser, Frankfurt (49) 69-33-999-240; \par                     alois.strasser@spglobal.com \par  \par Research Contributor:  Sarthak Maiti, CRISIL Global Analytical Center, an S&P \par                        Global Ratings affiliate, Mumbai; \par  \par  \par Additional Contact:  SovereignEurope; \par                      SovereignEurope@spglobal.com \par  \par The article with statistical information is available in full at \par \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.standardandpoors.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.standardandpoors.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . Registration is free. To access the full article, \par select "Ratings Actions" in the left navigation under Ratings Resources, then \par choose the Press Releases tab. \par  \par  \par  \par No content (including ratings, credit-related analyses and data, valuations, \par model, software, or other application or output therefrom) or any part thereof \par (Content) may be modified, reverse engineered, reproduced, or distributed in \par any form by any means, or stored in a database or retrieval system, without the \par prior written permission of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's Financial Services LLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  or its \par affiliates (collectively, S&P). The Content shall not be used for any unlawful \par or unauthorized purposes. S&P and any third-party providers, as wel