\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Israel  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  holds key rate, raises 2017 growth estimate\b0\par\par\uc2 495 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 2017\par 16:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, new economic estimates, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor quotes)\par \par \uc2 By Ari Rabinovitch and Steven Scheer\par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, July 10 (Reuters) - \uc2 The Bank of Israel\uc2  left its benchmark interest rate at 0.1 percent for a 27th time since February 2015 on Monday, citing low inflation and solid economic growth and expressing no rush to start raising interest rates.\par \par \uc2 "The Monetary (Policy) Committee intends to maintain the accommodative policy as long as necessary in order to entrench the inflation environment within the target range," Bank of Israel Governor \uc2 Karnit Flug\uc2  told reporters after the rates decision.\par \par \uc2 Prices had declined for 28 months through the end of 2016, with the inflation rate moving to 0.8 percent in May - below the government's annual target range of 1 to 3 percent.\par \par \uc2 Flug noted that a strong shekel, a halt in higher energy prices and Israeli government steps to reduce the cost of living are expected to push the inflation rate lower in coming months before returning to the target range.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecasts an inflation rate of 0.5 percent this year, rising to 1.5 percent in 2018.\par \par \uc2 In an updated forecast on Monday, the bank's own economists projected the key interest rate would remain unchanged through the first quarter of 2018, rise to 0.25 percent in the second quarter and rise again in the fourth quarter to bring the rate to 0.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 With exports expected to improve as global trade strengthens, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its economic growth estimate for 2017 to 3.4 percent from a previous 2.8 percent and left its 2018 forecast unchanged at 3.3 percent. Growth was 4 percent in 2016.\par \par \uc2 Flug reiterated her belief that the shekel was over-appreciated and said the bank would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary.\par \par \uc2 "The shekel is over-valued, a result of the very accommodative policy that some central banks are adopting," Flug said.\par \par \uc2 Without \uc2 the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in which it bought some $3 billion the past three months, the shekel would be even more over-valued, she said.\par \par \uc2 The shekel is hovering at a three-year high versus the dollar at 3.5470 and is at a 17-year high versus a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Israel's largest trading partners.\par \par \uc2 All 10 economists polled by Reuters had forecast no change in rates by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Israel's economy grew an annualised 1.2 percent in the first quarter, its slowest pace in almost two years with changes to the way cars are taxed distorting the numbers.\par \par \uc2 "After a long period in which private consumption drove growth, there are increasing signs at this time there is a return to more balanced growth in the economy, with both exports and investments contributing," Flug said.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Steven Scheer and Ari Rabinovitch, editing by Larry King) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-7-10T15:33:03.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170710ed7a00jwq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
