\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED-Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Israel \highlight\uc2  under fire over decade-long  \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,070 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2017\par 17:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Corrects university affiliation of economist in 20th para of March 8 item)\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bought more than $70 bln sin shekels since 2008\par \par \uc2 * Experts say \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a subsidy to exporters\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  says shekel is overvalued\par \par \uc2 * Shekel hits 15-year high vs euro, 2-1/2 year high vs dollar\par \par \uc2 By Steven Scheer\par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, March 8 (Reuters) - \uc2 The Bank of Israel\uc2  has a problem. After spending almost a decade and huge sums trying to curb the shekel, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still rising relentlessly - to the dismay of the country's exporters.\par \par \uc2 In 2008 the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began what was supposed to be a temporary fix. The plan was to buy large amounts of dollars and halt a rapid rise in the shekel, partly to protect exporters who account for more than 30 percent of economic output and form a strong domestic lobby.\par \par \uc2 But after purchasing more than $70 billion over the years, the bank is still struggling to soften the exchange rate and prevent Israeli exports from becoming relatively more expensive on world markets.\par \par \uc2 In the past 12 months, the shekel has gained 6 percent against the dollar, 11 percent against the euro and 10 percent against a basket of its main trading \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This has taken it in recent weeks to a 15-year high versus the euro, a 2-1/2 year peak against the dollar and its strongest level ever against the basket.\par \par \uc2 With the Israeli economy growing well, some experts and former policymakers say \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is no longer necessary and may be pointless. Buying dollars amounts to little more than a subsidy for sometimes inefficient exporters, at the expense of the rest of the economy, they argue.\par \par \uc2 "Few people thought it should be a permanent part of policy," said Barry Topf, who as head of market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2008-2011 helped to develop and implement the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  plan. "The policy has been in place for nine years. It has to be re-evaluated."\par \par \uc2 The purchases started under former governor \uc2 Stanley Fischer\uc2 , now vice chairman of the U.S. Federal Reserve. In principle a staunch opponent of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he accepted its temporary need to stabilise the market after the shekel had leapt.\par \par \uc2 While Fischer expressed hope at the time that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be used only rarely, Topf said there had been no exit strategy other than to "hope for interest rates in the world to turn higher and that would rectify the situation".\par \par \uc2 The opposite happened following the global financial crisis. To spur economic growth, many central banks cut borrowing costs sharply and some adopted policies such as negative interest rates and bond purchases to flood the financial system with cash. Often these measures remain in place.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, Israel's economy largely weathered the crisis and has outperformed much of the West since, culminating in 4 percent growth in 2016. With the benchmark interest rate at 0.1 percent for the past two years - a measure aimed at pushing inflation back up to the government's 1-3 percent target - \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the only option to weaken the shekel.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Israel Deputy Governor \uc2 Nadine Baudot-Trajtenberg\uc2  told Reuters last week that the shekel had become more overvalued and did not reflect economic realities. She noted that when Israel's two percent annual population growth rate is factored in, the rise in per capita income is not much greater than in many Western countries which have static populations.\par \par \uc2 The bank would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  "if we think the exchange rate will depress the economy", she added.\par \par \uc2 Many economists believe buying dollars is futile since the shekel's strength stems from factors that are not expected to go away for some time. They note annual capital inflows of over $10 billion from foreign companies buying Israeli ones. And while Israel runs a modest trade deficit, the broader current account achieved a $12.4 billion surplus last year.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHIC: Shekel vs dollar and euro {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/29xVm5V"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/29xVm5V}}}\par \par \uc2 GRAPHIC: Foreign trade openness {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/28IjBh8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/28IjBh8}}}\par \par \uc2 FIGHTING THE MARKET\par \par \uc2 Part of the surplus boost is due to the start of natural gas production in 2013, which reduced energy imports. "If you are importing less petroleum and coal it will put long term pressure on the shekel (to strengthen)," said Bank Leumi chief economist Gil Bufman.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began a programme to offset the impact of natural gas on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and has bought more than $10 billion since May 2013 under this scheme.\par \par \uc2 "I am not sure the way to go is \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because you have enormous pressures for (shekel) appreciation over the long term that are not going to go away," Bufman said. "Are you going to fight the market forever? The market has the ability to beat the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 He and others believe the government needs to take over the reins from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and help business to compete better with a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , starting by reducing red tape.\par \par \uc2 In the \uc2 World Bank\uc2 's 2017 Ease of Doing Business Report, Israel fell to 52nd place from 49th.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the government should help manufacturers modernise equipment to become more efficient, offer more job training, allow businesses to write off capital investments for tax purposes in one year, and hook up smaller firms to the natural gas network, economists argue.\par \par \uc2 Omer Moav, professor of economics at the Interdisciplinary Center Herzliya, believes one solution lies in removing barriers on imports such as tariffs and red tape, which would weaken the shekel. That would help exporters as well as lowering prices for the economy. "Why export more than import? Why does that make sense?" he said.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Moshe Kahlon said this week he would not abandon exporters and that Israel needed policies to boost productivity and strengthen local industries.\par \par \uc2 "Israel's economy (in 2016) ended one of its best years ever and the strengthening of the shekel is a direct result of the strong economic data," Kahlon said.\par \par \uc2 Shraga Brosh, president of the Manufacturers' Association, said the shekel's gains were a "fatal blow" to his sector. "It's time to get out of the box and use fiscal tools to take care of the shekel," he said.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Steven Scheer; editing by David Stamp) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-3-9T17:02:12.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170308ed3800i72\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
