\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Israel's shekel becoming more over-valued -cenbank deputy governor\b0\par\par\uc2 728 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2017\par 11:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, March 1 (Reuters) - The shekel has become more over-valued recently and does not reflect the real strength of the Israeli economy, the deputy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief said, adding that the bank will continue to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to soften the shekel.\par \par \uc2 The shekel has appreciated 6 percent against the dollar this year to 3.645, its strongest level since October 2014, and is also hovering at a 15-year peak against the euro.\par \par \uc2 Against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Israel's main trading partners the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen more than 7 percent over the past year.\par \par \uc2 "The shekel is over-valued and that has become more strongly so in the past few months," Bank of Israel Deputy Governor \uc2 Nadine Baudot-Trajtenberg\uc2  said. "We think fundamentals point to a weaker shekel."\par \par \uc2 Europe and the United States are Israel's main trading partners, so a strong shekel against their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weighs on Israeli exports, which account for more than 30 percent of economic activity, as well as local producers battling imports.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is very much on the table and we will continue to use it if we think the exchange rate will depress the economy ... We feel \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is effective," Montreal-born Baudot-Trajtenberg told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 In the second half of 2016, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $2.6 billion of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and another $50 million in January. Market sources said the bank bought hundreds of millions of dollars more in February.\par \par \uc2 Not everyone agrees with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  about the value of the shekel, however. David Woo, head of global rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2 , told the Calcalist newspaper this week that without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the shekel could be as much as 20 percent stronger.\par \par \uc2 Woo and other analysts believe the shekel's strength stems from robust economic data. Israel's economy grew a faster than expected 4 percent in 2016, although it is forecast to slow to 3.2-3.5 percent this year.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also left \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a huge pile-up of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. Since 2008, the country has bought some $70 billion of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , bringing its official exchange reserves to above $100 billion.\par \par \uc2 RATE HIKE?\par \par \uc2 Baudot-Trajtenberg, who has served as deputy to Governor Karnit Flug since March 2014, maintains that economic growth could be stronger, however.\par \par \uc2 "We are seeing nice growth but we are not seeing a real step-up in the growth of the economy," she said, adding that by growth per capita "the economy in Israel is doing OK, but it is not much better than our trading partners".\par \par \uc2 Israel has an annual population growth rate of about 2 percent versus zero for much of the West, she said.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, Israel is competing with expansionary policies around the world that have weakened many \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as well as political and economic uncertainty in the United States and Europe, she added.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Israel\uc2  on Monday held its benchmark interest rate at 0.1 percent for the 24th straight month, dismissing recent strong economic data and instead focusing on inflation, which remains below the government's annual target of 1 to 3 percent.\par \par \uc2 The inflation rate moved to 0.1 percent in January and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expects it to reach the target within a year.\par \par \uc2 "This growth is very much sustainable without getting into an inflationary period. In normal times you would expect inflation to be at a higher level than now," because business sector wages are rising, Baudot-Trajtenberg said.\par \par \uc2 "One reason why we kept rates very low is because inflation has not given us reason for putting brakes on the economy," she said. "We still think that rates are likely to remain low for a substantial amount of time."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  economists expect to see a 15 basis-point rate increase in the fourth quarter and another quarter-point hike in 2018. One policy member believes the pace will be quicker but Baudot-Trajtenberg declined to speculate on the timing.\par \par \uc2 "To raise rates, you need to have a different picture of the economy," she said. "Inflation is low but growth per capita is not faster than our trading partners." (Editing by Tova Cohen and Hugh Lawson) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-3-1T11:33:15.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170301ed3100azy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
