\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Israel central bank \highlight\uc2  chief defends  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2 , opposes more tax cuts\b0\par\par\uc2 442 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2017\par 15:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, Feb 5 (Reuters) - Bank of Israel Governor \uc2 Karnit Flug\uc2  on Sunday defended a near decade-old policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and said excess tax income should not go towards more tax cuts, given strong economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which bought several hundred million dollars last week to try to contain the shekel's appreciation, has come under pressure from analysts who believe \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is no longer necessary.\par \par \uc2 The shekel has gained more than 2.5 percent versus the dollar in the past three weeks to a five-month high of 3.75.\par \par \uc2 Flug noted that with goods exports weak, Israel needed to retain its global competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 "Our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has appreciated quite substantially the last two, three years," she said.\par \par \uc2 "We do not try and offset the fundamental economic forces that affect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but we are operating in an environment where some of our trading partners are pursuing an extremely expansionary (monetary) policy - both in terms of negative interest rates and QE (quantitative easing)," Flug told the Jerusalem Press Club, referring mainly to Europe.\par \par \uc2 In December, \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2  bought $1.1 billion worth of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Flug also said Finance Minister Moshe Kahlon should direct any excess tax revenue towards infrastructure projects and other areas, especially since Israel's tax burden is below the \uc2 OECD\uc2  average while civilian public expenditures were the lowest in the \uc2 OECD\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We don't need extra short-term stimulus of the economy. We are pretty close to full employment," Flug said.\par \par \uc2 Last month, Kahlon said he would consider further reductions in taxes this year if tax revenue continued to exceed expectations. He has already reduced income and corporate taxes at the start of 2017.\par \par \uc2 Kahlon said he would wait until after the first quarter to see data on tax collections, which were 7.5 billion shekels more than forecast in 2016.\par \par \uc2 Flug said Israel's economy was strong, growing by a more than expected 3.8 percent last year, while the jobless rate for those 25 to 64 was also 3.8 percent with rapidly rising wages.\par \par \uc2 Given a deflation trend, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has held its benchmark interest rate at 0.1 percent for two years. The bank's economists forecast the rate rising to 0.25 percent this year and to 0.5 percent by the end of 2018.\par \par \uc2 "This is the forecast. It's not a commitment," Flug said, saying future rate moves will depend on economic and inflationary developments. (Reporting by Steven Scheer; Editing by Andrew Bolton) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-2-5T15:36:09.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170205ed2500gz5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
