\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Affirms Israel 'A+/A-1' Ratings; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 3,187 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2016\par 17:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 5 Aug 2016 11:36 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Israel 'A+/A-1' Ratings; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2 : \par OVERVIEW \par  \par      -- We expect the Israeli economy to remain strong despite risks associated \par with the global economy and international financial markets. Such resilience \par comes from its diversified economy, strong external position, and flexible \par monetary framework. \par      -- We are therefore affirming our 'A+/A-1' foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par sovereign credit ratings on Israel. \par      -- The stable outlook reflects our expectation that the government will \par maintain stable public finances and continue to tackle structural issues in the \par economy, and that the impact of security risks on the Israeli economy will be \par contained over the next two years. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par  \par On Aug. 5, 2016, S&P Global Ratings affirmed its 'A+/A-1' long- and short-term \par foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on the State of Israel. The \par outlook is stable. \par  \par RATIONALE \par  \par The ratings are supported by Israel's prosperous and diverse economy, strong \par external balance sheet, and flexible monetary framework. The ratings are \par constrained by Israel's high general government debt burden and significant \par security and geopolitical risks. \par  \par With per capita GDP at an estimated $37,000 in 2015, the economy is prosperous \par and well diversified, with high value-added manufacturing and service sectors. \par This is underpinned by high expenditure on research and development, amounting \par to 4.1% of GDP in 2014, the second-highest among member countries of the \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Organization for Economic Co-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Development\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2 ). The information \par and communication sector has a 9.8% share of gross value added (GVA) and \par scientific and technical activities have 2.8%. \par  \par We assume Israel's economy will grow at an average rate of about 2.5% in \par 2016-2019, despite risks of weaker global demand, partly thanks to a major \par investment by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Intel\f28 \uc2 \f2 . We expect the key drivers of this growth will be robust \par private consumption, continued corporate investment activity, and healthy \par service exports, supported by both loose monetary policy and continued fiscal \par stimulus. In per capita terms, this equates to growth of around 1% per year, \par reflecting robust population growth. \par  \par The government recently approved a new development plan for the Leviathan gas \par field, after the supreme court had blocked a previous deal. We believe the \par development of Israel's gas resources would benefit the economy and its fiscal \par position, but further court suits could cause more delays. Moreover, in our \par view, the lack of gas distribution network infrastructure could also limit how \par much domestic input cost reduction the gas field could bring to the Israeli \par economy. \par  \par The March 2015 general elections resulted in a right-wing government coalition \par with 61 of 120 seats in Israel's parliament, the Knesset. In May 2016, a new \par right-wing coalition partner, Israel Beitenu, joined in the government, \par increasing the number of supporters to 66 from 61 and resulting in a more \par stable coalition. Nevertheless, it also adds to the already heterogeneous \par coalition structure; hence the inclusion of a new partner is unlikely to \par enhance the government's capacity to deliver sustainable public finances and \par measures that boost economic prospects. \par  \par We believe infrastructure enhancements, especially to transportation, could \par support productivity gains that have been lacking in the Israel economy. \par However, we expect the infrastructure gap to remain, given the capacity and \par administrative constraints facing the sector. We foresee somewhat muted \par progress in tackling the other structural issues facing the Israeli economy, \par given that any controversial measures are unlikely to receive coalition \par support. Moreover, some previously implemented measures to boost educational \par attainment and labor supply have been reversed in order to secure support from \par Ultra Orthodox parties, Shas and United Torah Judaism. \par  \par In 2015, the central government deficit was 2.2% of GDP and general government \par deficit was 2.4% of GDP, a relatively positive fiscal performance arising \par mostly from better-than-expected tax revenue collection. This trend continued \par in first half of 2016, which has now given the government confidence to cut \par taxes. The new coalition government has reached a consensus regarding the \par biannual 2017-2018 budget, maintaining the deficit ceiling of 2.9%. The new \par budget envisages expansionary fiscal measures, cuts to personal and corporate \par income taxes, and increased spending on health, education, and infrastructure. \par Given the government's reasonable track record of containing fiscal pressures \par within a stringent framework, and the potential for a snap election should \par additional parlimentary approval on budgetary matters be required, we expect \par the general government deficit to remain at 2.9% of GDP over the next two \par years. \par  \par A substantial reduction in interest expenditures in 2015 significantly improved \par Israel's debt ratios. Amid the low-inflation environment (around one-half of \par general government debt is CPI linked) and low funding costs, we expect this \par trend to continue over the forecast horizon. Subtracting liquid assets (mostly \par in the form of deposits at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) from gross government debt, we \par estimate that net general government debt remained at below 62% of GDP at the \par end of 2015. Even without taking into account possible land sales and \par privatization proceeds, which could reduce government financing needs, we \par expect the net debt ratio could increase slightly to 64% of GDP in 2019. \par  \par As a result of Israel's strong export performance and sustained current account \par surpluses, its external balance sheet is strong and its net creditor position \par versus the rest of the world continues to grow. We forecast that its liquid \par external assets will outstrip its gross external debt over the next three \par years. This dynamic is also lowering the country's gross external financing \par needs, indicating low dependency on external financing. \par  \par We also consider Israel's monetary policy flexibility to be a credit strength. \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Israel\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (BoI, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) has become increasingly \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventionist\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , over and above its commitment to purchase foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par to offset the impact of domestic natural gas production on the balance of \par payments. We view the exchange rate regime as a managed float, which somewhat \par hampers monetary policy flexibility. \par  \par In addition to making frequent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, \par the BoI has eased its stance on monetary policy, countering the strength of the \par Israeli new shekel in order to maintain the competitiveness of Israeli exports. \par It lowered its policy rate to a historical low of 0.1% in March 2015, but the \par shekel continued to appreciate against Israel's key trading partners. During \par 2015, the shekel weakened by 0.3% against the dollar, but strengthened by about \par 7.3% against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Israel's main trading partners, in terms of the \par nominal effective exchange rate. Since the beginning of the year, the shekel \par continued to appreciate vis-a-vis main trading partners. \par  \par One of the key challenges to monetary policy continues to be Israel's rising \par house prices. Real house prices have increased by around 69% since the end of \par 2007 and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) assesses that the house prices \par in Israel are currently overvalued by 30%. The BoI's earlier attempt to dampen \par the housing market by raising interest rates yielded little, and significantly \par pushed up the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate of the shekel. The government has \par implemented a comprehensive set of measures to address supply-side issues, \par including freeing up more land for development, changing the tendering criteria \par to give first time buyers a discount to market price, and speeding up \par administrative processes for construction permissions. Given the capacity \par constraints in the construction industry, the extended time needed to build \par houses, and continued growth in demand, we do not expect government measures to \par fully address the supply shortage in the near term. The first half of 2016 saw \par little impact on supply of housing from these measures; house prices recorded \par above the 5% annual growth rate in March, however, we understand that house \par price data currently do not take into account of the aforementioned discount \par for first time buyers. \par  \par The tightening of macroprudential measures has reduced systemic risks to \par Israel's banking industry and constrains mortgage leading growth, but any \par meaningful correction in house prices could have other negative economic \par effects (see: \par "\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.globalcreditportal.com/ratingsdirectshowArticlePage.do?object_id=9374818&rev_id=8Articlehtmlxt_artcl-mark-hyperlnkBanking"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.globalcreditportal.com/ratingsdirectshowArticlePage.do?object_id=9374818&rev_id=8Articlehtmlxt_artcl-mark-hyperlnkBanking}}}\uc2 \f2  \par Industry Country Risk Assessment: Israel," published Oct. 23, 2015, on \par RatingsDirect). We expect that by the end of 2016 the Knesset will pass general \par legislation to establish a formal Financial Stability Committee, as recommended \par by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2 , to enhance policy co-ordination. \par  \par Overall, institutional and governance structures in Israel are generally \par effective, with a satisfactory degree of transparency and accountability. \par However, we consider that the persistent territorial dispute with the \par Palestinians threatens political stability and weighs on policy predictability. \par  \par Geopolitical risks continue to weigh on the ratings. Repeated violent clashes \par with the Palestinians not only inflict social and economic costs, but also \par elicit negative reaction from the international community. In Israel's \par neighboring region, the conflict in Syria and Iraq, as well as instability in \par the Sinai region, pose medium-term security risks. Any significant armed \par conflict could have a negative impact on the ratings if it significantly \par deterred investment, weakened the economy's growth potential, or strained \par fiscal flexibility. We do not expect the nuclear agreement between Iran and the \par international community to be either positive or negative for Israel over the \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 5 Aug 2016 11:36 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Israel 'A+/A-1' -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par forecast period, given the continued regional tensions and inherent uncertainty \par regarding the implementation of the agreement. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par  \par The stable outlook on Israel reflects our opinion that the government will \par maintain prudent macroeconomic policies and ensure the stabilization of \par government debt over 2016-2019, despite higher spending concessions agreed by \par the coalition. The stable outlook also reflects our expectation that security \par risks to the Israeli economy will not increase materially. \par  \par We could consider raising our ratings if fiscal consolidation exceeds our \par expectations, or additional income from gas fields sustainably reduces the net \par debt burden to below 60% of GDP, or if there is marked progress in defusing \par external security risks. \par  \par Conversely, we could lower the ratings if the economic growth outlook were to \par weaken substantially, due to an abrupt correction in the housing market or \par unaddressed structural weaknesses. A downgrade would also become more likely if \par the government yields to pressures for more social or security spending and \par allows deficits to widen and government debt to increase significantly above \par our current expectations. Moreover, if a perceived loss of international \par support were to further isolate the Israeli economy, we could lower the \par ratings. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RATINGS SCORE SNAPSHOT \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA AND RESEARCH \par  \par Related Criteria \par      -- Criteria - Governments - Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology - \par December 23, 2014 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Short-Term And Long-Term \par Ratings For Corporate, Insurance, And Sovereign Issuers - May 07, 2013 \par      -- General Criteria: Use Of CreditWatch And Outlooks - September 14, 2009 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments - May 18, 2009 \par  \par Related Research \par      -- Default, Transition, and Recovery: 2015 Annual Sovereign Default Study \par AndRating Transitions, May 24, 2016 \par      -- Global Sovereign Rating Trends Mid-Year 2016, July 13, 2016 \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators, July 6, 2016. An interactive version is also \par available at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.spratings.com/sri}}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to make \par an informed decision. After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and \par explained the recommendation, the Committee discussed key rating factors and \par critical issues in accordance with the relevant criteria. Qualitative and \par quantitative risk factors were considered and discussed, looking at \par track-record and forecasts. \par  \par The committee agreed that the fiscal assessment: debt burden had improved, and \par all other key rating factors remained unchanged. \par  \par The chair ensured every voting member was given the opportunity to articulate \par his/her opinion. The chair or designee reviewed 