\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Bank of Israel to hold rates after data pickup\b0\par\par\uc2 480 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2016\par 09:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, July 22 (Reuters) - \uc2 Israel's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to leave interest rates unchanged for a 17th straight month on Monday, following improved economic data and as policymakers await evidence of any clear impact from Britain's vote to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All 12 economists polled by Reuters believe \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2  will keep its benchmark short-term interest rate at a record low of 0.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 "The chance of any change in the interest rate in the coming months is almost zero, particularly in light of strong private spending and the expected continued rise in housing prices," said Ofer Klein, head of economics and strategy at Harel Insurance and Finance.\par \par \uc2 "The anchor will continue to be global interest rates along with (\uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when necessary."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept its interest rates and policy plans unchanged as expected on Thursday, and said the immediate market stress caused by Brexit had been contained.\par \par \uc2 Last month, all four \uc2 Bank of Israel\uc2  rate-setters voted for unchanged rates. According to minutes of the discussions, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the balance of risks to an annual inflation target of 1 to 3 percent "tends to the downside due to Brexit".\par \par \uc2 Bank of Israel Governor \uc2 Karnit Flug\uc2  has said it is still difficult to know what the final short-term effects of the British referendum result will be, although policymakers doubt there will be a significant impact to Israel's economy.\par \par \uc2 The economy grew faster than previously believed in the first quarter of the year. The government last week raised revised growth estimate to an annualised 1.7 percent from a prior 1.3 percent and a preliminary 0.8 percent.\par \par \uc2 However, economists - including those at \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2  and Finance Ministry - trimmed 2016 growth forecasts to 2.4-2.5 percent in the wake of weak exports and the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  cutting global growth estimates, for which it cited Brexit as a factor.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Israel's annual inflation has been negative for the past 22 months which, with domestic consumer spending remaining robust, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  attributes mainly to external factors such as falling oil and commodity prices.\par \par \uc2 Bond market expectations for inflation in the coming year to rise to 0.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 While the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep a lid on the shekel in June, it bought a combined $2.4 billion in April and May, and dealers reported significant \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in July.\par \par \uc2 The shekel trades at around 3.854 per dollar, up from 3.90 at the time of last month's rates decision.\par \par \uc2 Monday's rates decision is scheduled for 4 p.m. (1300 GMT). (Reporting by Steven Scheer; editing by John Stonestreet)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-7-25T07:47:09.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160722ec7m00eia\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
