\par\par \b \uc2 *S&P Affirms Israel 'A+/A-1' Ratings; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 2,850 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2016\par 17:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 5 Feb 2016 11:33 ET  Press Release: S&P Affirms Israel 'A+/A-1' Ratings; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2 : \par OVERVIEW \par  \par      -- We expect the Israeli economy to weather potential volatility in the \par global economy and international financial market, thanks to its diversified \par economy, strong external position, and flexible monetary framework. \par      -- We are therefore affirming our 'A+/A-1' foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par sovereign credit ratings on Israel. \par      -- The stable outlook reflects our expectation that the government will \par maintain stable public finances and that the impact of security risks on the \par Israeli economy will be contained over the next two years. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par  \par On Feb. 5, 2016, Standard & Poor's Ratings Services affirmed its 'A+/A-1' long- \par and short-term foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on the State \par of Israel. The outlook is stable. \par  \par RATIONALE \par  \par The ratings are supported by Israel's prosperous and diverse economy, strong \par external balance sheet, and flexible monetary framework. The ratings are \par constrained by Israel's high debt and interest burden and significant security \par and geopolitical risks. \par  \par With per capita GDP at an estimated $37,000 in 2015, the economy is prosperous \par and well diversified, with high value-added manufacturing and service sectors. \par This is underpinned by high expenditure in research and development, amounting \par to 4.2% of GDP in 2013, the highest among member countries of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Organisation \par for Economic Co-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Development\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2 ). The information and \par communication sector has a 9.8% share of gross value added (GVA) and scientific \par and technical activities have 2.8%. We assume Israel's economy will grow at an \par average rate of about 2.5% in 2016-2019, despite risks of slower world trade \par and increasing volatility in the international capital market. We expect the \par key drivers of this growth will be robust private consumption, continued \par corporate investment activity, and healthy service exports. In per capita \par terms, this equates to growth of around 1% per year, reflecting robust \par population growth. \par  \par The March 2015 general elections resulted in a right-wing government coalition \par with 61 of 120 seats in Israel's parliament, the Knesset. It was not until \par November 2015 that the Knesset passed the biannual budget for 2015 and 2016. As \par a provisional budget was in place for most of 2015, meaning only one-twelfth of \par the 2014 budget could be spent per month, we estimate the general government \par deficit to be around 2.2% of GDP in 2015. Despite the political and fiscal \par concessions made to form a new coalition government, we expect the general \par government deficit will remain below 3% in the coming years. Fiscal policy is \par moderately expansionary in 2016--additional spending commitments in the \par 2015/2016 budget include reversing the cuts in entitlements to child allowances \par and increasing resources in health and education to reduce waiting time and \par class sizes. On the revenue side, cuts to VAT and corporate tax are also likely \par to widen fiscal deficits. Should there be an increase in defense spending, we \par expect the government to cut civilian spending to offset the increase. \par  \par Subtracting liquid assets (mostly in the form of deposits at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) \par from gross government debt, we estimate that net general government debt \par remained at below 64% of GDP at the end of 2015. Even without taking into \par account possible land sales and privatization proceeds, which could reduce \par government financing needs, we expect the net debt ratio will stabilize at \par below 65% of GDP in 2016-2019. \par  \par As a result of Israel's strong export performance and sustained current account \par surpluses, its external balance sheet is strong and its net creditor position \par versus the rest of the world continues to grow. We forecast that its liquid \par external assets will outstrip its gross external debt over the next three \par years. This dynamic is also lowering the country's gross external financing \par needs, indicating low dependency on external financing. \par  \par We also consider Israel's monetary policy flexibility to be a credit strength. \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Israel\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (BoI, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) has become increasingly \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventionist\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , over and above its commitment to purchase foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par to offset the impact of domestic natural gas production on the balance of \par payments. We view the exchange rate regime as a managed float, which somewhat \par hampers monetary policy flexibility. \par  \par In addition to making frequent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, \par the BoI has eased its stance on monetary policy, countering the strength of the \par Israeli new shekel in order to maintain the competitiveness of Israeli exports. \par It lowered its policy rate to a historical low of 0.1% in March 2015, but the \par shekel continued to appreciate against Israel's key trading partners. Over the \par course of 2015, the shekel weakened by 0.3% against the dollar, but \par strengthened by about 7.3% against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Israel's main trading \par partners, in terms of the nominal effective exchange rate. \par  \par One of the key challenges to monetary policy continues to be Israel's rising \par house prices. After years of relative stability, real house prices have \par increased by around 69% since the end of 2007 and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary \par Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) assesses that the house prices in Israel are currently overvalued by \par 30%. The BoI's earlier attempt to dampen the housing market by raising interest \par rates yielded little, only pushing up the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate of the shekel \par significantly. The new government has implemented a comprehensive set of \par measures to address supply-side issues, including freeing up more land for \par development, changing the tendering criteria, and speeding up administrative \par processes for construction permissions. Given the capacity contraints in the \par construction industry, the time needed to build houses, and continued growth in \par demand, we do not expect the government measures to fully address the supply \par shortage in the near term. The tightening of macroprudential measures has \par reduced systemic risks to Israel's banking industry, but any abrupt correction \par in house prices could still have other negative economic effects (see: \par "\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.globalcreditportal.com/ratingsdirectshowArticlePage.do?object_id=9374818&rev_id=8Articlehtmlxt_artcl-mark-hyperlnkBanking"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.globalcreditportal.com/ratingsdirectshowArticlePage.do?object_id=9374818&rev_id=8Articlehtmlxt_artcl-mark-hyperlnkBanking}}}\uc2 \f2  \par Industry Country Risk Assessment: Israel," published Oct. 23, 2015, on \par RatingsDirect). We expect that the Knesset will pass general legislation to \par establish a formal Financial Stability Committee, as recommended by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  to \par enhance policy co-ordination, by the end of 2016. \par  \par Overall, institutional and governance structures in Israel are generally \par effective, with a satisfactory degree of transparency and accountability. \par However, we consider that the persistent territorial dispute with the \par Palestinians threatens political stability and weighs on policy predictability. \par  \par The ratings remain constrained by geopolitical risks. Repeated violent clashes \par with the Palestinians not only inflict social and economic costs, but also risk \par reactions by the international community. On the northern border, the conflict \par in Syria and Iraq, as well as instability in the Sinai region, pose medium-term \par security risks. Any significant armed conflict could have a negative impact on \par the ratings if it significantly deterred investment, weakened the economy's \par growth potential, or strained fiscal flexibility. We do not expect the nuclear \par deal between Iran and the international community to have a material direct \par impact on the ratings on Israel, given the continued regional tensions. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par  \par The stable outlook on Israel reflects our opinion that the government will \par maintain prudent macroeconomic policies and ensure the stabilization of \par government debt over 2016-2019, despite higher spending concessions agreed by \par the coalition. We also expect the impact of security risks on the Israeli \par economy will continue to be contained. \par  \par We could consider raising our ratings if fiscal consolidation exceeds our \par expectations, resulting in a significantly lower net debt burden or interest \par costs, or if there is marked progress in defusing external security risks. \par  \par Conversely, we could lower the ratings if the economic growth outlook were to \par weaken substantially, due to an abrupt correction in the housing market or \par unaddressed structural weaknesses. A downgrade would also become more likely if \par the government yields to pressures for more social or security spending and \par allows deficits to widen and government debt to increase significantly above \par our current expectations. Moreover, if a perceived loss of international \par support were to further isolate the Israeli economy, we could lower the \par ratings. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RATINGS SCORE SNAPSHOT \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA AND RESEARCH \par  \par Related Criteria \par      -- Criteria - Governments - Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology - \par December 23, 2014 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Short-Term And Long-Term \par Ratings For Corporate, Insurance, And Sovereign Issuers - May 07, 2013 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments - May 18, 2009 \par  \par Related Research \par      -- Global Sovereign Rating Trends 2016, Jan. 6, 2016 \par      -- Default, Transition, and Recovery: 2014 Annual Sovereign Default Study \par And Rating Transitions, May 18, 2015 \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators, Dec. 14, 2015 (a free interactive version is \par available at spratings.com/sri) \par      -- Banking Industry Country Risk Assessment: Israel, Oct. 23, 2015 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 5 Feb 2016 11:33 ET \par Press Release: S&P Affirms Israel 'A+/A-1' -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to make \par an informed decision. After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and \par explained the recommendation, the Committee discussed key rating factors and \par critical issues in accordance with the relevant criteria. Qualitative and \par quantitative risk factors were considered and discussed, looking at \par track-record and forecasts. \par  \par All key rating factors remained unchanged. \par  \par The chair ensured every voting member was given the opportunity to articulate \par his/her opinion. The chair or designee reviewed the draft report to ensure \par consistency with the Committee decision. The views and the decision of the \par rating committee are summarized in the above rationale and outlook. The \par weighting of all rating factors is described in the methodology used in this \par rating action (see 'Related Criteria and Research'). \par  \par RATINGS LIST \par  \par                                            Rating \par                                            To                 From \par Israel (State of) \par  Sovereign Credit Rating \par   Foreign and Local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                A+/Stable/A-1      A+/Stable/A-1 \par  Transfer & Convertibility Assessment      AA                 AA \par  Senior Unsecured \par   Foreign and Local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                A+                 A+ \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \par Certain terms used in this report, particularly certain adjectives used to \par express ou