\par\par \b \uc2 *S&P Affirms Israel 'A+/A-1' Ratings; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 2,761 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2015\par 17:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 7 Aug 2015 11:36 ET  Press Release: \uc2 S&P\uc2  Affirms Israel 'A+/A-1' Ratings; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2 : \par OVERVIEW \par  \par      -- We expect Israel's new coalition government will broadly support the \par previous government's fiscal strategy, maintaining a stable debt burden, \par despite some additional expenditure commitments in coalition agreements. \par      -- We are therefore affirming our 'A+/A-1' foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par sovereign credit ratings on Israel. \par      -- The stable outlook reflects our expectation that the government will \par maintain stable public finances and that the impact of security risks on the \par Israeli economy will be contained over the next two years. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par  \par On Aug. 7, 2015, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ratings Services affirmed its 'A+/A-1' long- \par and short-term foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on the State \par of Israel. The outlook is stable. \par  \par RATIONALE \par  \par The ratings are supported by Israel's prosperous and diverse economy, strong \par external balance sheet, and flexible monetary framework. The ratings are \par constrained by Israel's high debt and interest burden and significant security \par and geopolitical risks. \par  \par With per capita GDP at an estimated $38,000 in 2015, the economy is prosperous \par and well diversified, with high value added manufacturing and service sectors. \par This is underpinned by high expenditure in research and development, amounting \par to 4.2% of GDP in 2013, the highest among \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries. The information and \par communication sector has a 9.8% share of gross value added (GVA) and scientific \par and technical activities have 2.8%. Services sectors dominate the economy, \par followed by manufacturing (21% of GVA), whereas agriculture and mining \par contribute only 4.2% in total. We assume Israel's economy will grow at an \par average rate of about 3% in 2015-2018. We expect the key drivers of this growth \par will be robust private consumption, continued corporate investment activity, \par and healthy exports. In per capita terms, this equates to growth of 1.5% per \par year. \par  \par The March 2015 general elections resulted in a right-wing government coalition. \par As there is only a provisional budget for 2015--only one-twelfth of the 2014 \par budget can be spent per month--we estimate that the general government deficit \par will be around 2.8% of GDP this year, owing to the limitations of last year's \par budget. Despite the political concessions made to form a new coalition \par government, we expect the general government deficit will remain below 3% in \par the coming years. We expect most spending pressure will come from a reversal of \par entitlements to child allowances, grants to soldiers, and subsidies to Jewish \par religious schools and the elderly as of 2016. The general government deficit in \par 2014 amounted to 2.6% of GDP. The Gaza military action, which cost around new \par Israeli shekel (ILS)7 billion (or 0.6% of GDP), was offset by spending cuts in \par the civilian budget and use of fiscal reserves, and was not affected by the \par cancellation of a planned tax increase. \par  \par Subtracting liquid assets (at close to 4% of GDP, mostly in the form of \par deposits at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) from gross government debt, we project that net \par debt will remain at about 64% of GDP at the end of 2015. Even without taking \par into account possible land sales and privatization proceeds, which need to be \par used for debt repayment, we expect the net debt ratio will decline moderately \par by about one percentage point per year in 2015-2018. We expect the interest \par expenditure as a percentage of general government revenue will remain above \par 10%. \par  \par Following strong export performance and sustained current account surpluses, \par Israel's external balance sheet is strong. Israel continues to improve its net \par creditor position versus the rest of the world by running small but consistent \par current account surpluses. We forecast that its liquid external assets would \par outstrip its gross external debt by over 20% of current account receipts (CARs) \par over the next three years. This dynamic is also lowering the country's gross \par external financing needs, which will likely only require 76% of CARs and usable \par reserves in 2015--representing low dependency on external financing. \par  \par We also consider Israel's monetary policy flexibility to be a credit strength. \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Israel\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (BoI, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) has become increasingly \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventionist\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , over and above its commitment to purchase foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par to offset the impact of domestic natural gas production on the balance of \par payments. We consider the exchange rate regime to be a managed float, which \par somewhat hampers monetary policy flexibility. \par  \par In addition to frequent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, the BoI \par has eased its stance on monetary policy to counter the shekel's strength. It \par lowered its policy rate to a historical low of 0.1% in March 2015. Given the \par current low inflation and our assessment of the BoI's high monetary policy \par credibility and policy effectiveness, we consider a move toward unconventional \par monetary policy possible. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had benefitted from a current account in \par a slight surplus and strong inward net foreign direct investment (to average \par 1.7% of GDP in 2015-2018), mitigated by net capital outflows on the financial \par account, but its strength could challenge the export sector. Currently, we \par believe that exports have high valued added and therefore stronger pricing \par power. Nevertheless, the exchange rate poses a pricing risk, adding to the need \par for constant innovation to remain externally competitive. \par  \par One of the key challenges to monetary policy continues to be Israel's rising \par house prices. After years of relative stability, real house prices have \par increased by close to 60% since 2008, surpassed during that period only by \par Brazil and also marignally by Hong Kong. The new government plans to increase \par supply, which is a bottleneck. Freeing up more land for development, and \par speeding up administrative processes for construction permissions should assist \par this goal, although it might only offer a medium-term solution. Further \par tightening of macroprudential measures should reduce systemic risks to Israel's \par banking industry, but any abrupt correction in house prices could still have \par other negative economic effects (see: \par "\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.globalcreditportal.com/ratingsdirectshowArticlePage.do?object_id=8915677&rev_id=1Articlehtmlxt_artcl-mark-hyperlnkBanking"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.globalcreditportal.com/ratingsdirectshowArticlePage.do?object_id=8915677&rev_id=1Articlehtmlxt_artcl-mark-hyperlnkBanking}}}\uc2 \f2  \par Industry Country Risk Assessment: Israel," published Dec. 17, 2014, on \par RatingsDirect). \par  \par Institutional and governance structure in Israel are generally effective, with \par a satisfactory degree of transparency and accountability. However, we consider \par that the persistent territorial dispute with the Palestinians contributes to a \par lack of political stability and weighs on policy predictability. \par  \par The ratings remain constrained by geopolitical risks. Repeated violent clashes \par with the Palestinians not only inflict social and economic costs, but could \par also risk reactions by the international community. On the northern border, the \par conflict in Syria-Iraq, as well as instability in the Sinai region pose \par medium-term security risks. Any significant armed conflict could have a \par negative impact on the ratings if it significantly deterred investment, \par weakened the economy's growth potential, or strained fiscal flexibility. We do \par not expect that the nuclear deal between Iran and the international community \par will affect the ratings on Israel. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par  \par The stable outlook on Israel reflects our opinion that the new government will \par continue to conduct prudent macroeconomic policy and ensure the stabilization \par of government debt over 2015-2018, despite higher spending concessions agreed \par by the new coalition government. We also expect the impact of security risks on \par the Israeli economy will continue to be contained. \par  \par We could consider raising our ratings if fiscal consolidation exceeds our \par expectations, resulting in a significantly lower net debt burden or interest \par cost, or if there is marked progress in defusing external security risks. \par  \par Conversely, we could lower the ratings if the general government's budget \par deficits were to increase again, if a substantial deterioration in security \par results in significant fiscal pressure and more subdued economic activities, or \par if the perceived loss of international support were to adversely affect \par Israel's export sector. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RATINGS SCORE SNAPSHOT \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA AND RESEARCH \par  \par Related Criteria \par      -- Criteria - Governments - Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology - \par December 23, 2014 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Short-Term And Long-Term \par Ratings For Corporate, Insurance, And Sovereign Issuers - May 07, 2013 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments - May 18, 2009 \par  \par Related Research \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators, June 30, 2015 (a free interactive version is \par available at spratings.com/sri) \par      -- 2014 Annual Sovereign Default Study And Rating Transitions, May 18, \par 2015 \par      -- Global Sovereign Rating Trends Mid-Year 2015, July 13, 2015 \par      -- Ratings On Israel Unaffected By Election Outcome, March 18, 2015 \par      -- Global Sovereign Debt Report 2015: Borrowing to Drop By 5.7% To US$6.7 \par Trillion, March 5, 2015 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to make \par an informed decision. After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and \par explained the recommendation, the Committee discussed key rating factors and \par critical issues in accordance with the relevant criteria. Qualitative and \par quantitative risk factors were considered and discussed, looking at \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7 Aug 2015 11:36 ET \par Press Release: \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P\f28 \uc2 \f2  Affirms Israel 'A+/A-1' -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par track-record and forecasts. \par  \par All key rating factors remained unchanged. \par  \par The chair ensured every voting member was given the opportunity to articulate \par his/her opinion. The chair or designee reviewed the draft report to ensure \par consistency with the Committee decision. The views and the decision of the \par rating committee are summarized in the above rationale and outlook. The \par weighting of all rating factors is described in the methodology used in this \par rating action (see 'Related Criteria and Research'). \par  \par RATINGS LIST \par  \par                                            Ratings \par                                            To                 From \par Israel (State of) \par  Sovereign credit rating \par   Foreign and Local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                A+/Stable/A-1      A+/Stable/A-1 \par  Transfer & Convertibility Assessment \par   T&C Assessment                           AA                 AA \par  Senior Unsecured \par   Foreign and Local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                A+                 A+ \par  \par Complete ratings information is available to subscribers of RatingsDirect at \par \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.globalcreditportal.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.globalcreditportal.com}}}\uc2 \f2  and at spcapitaliq.com. All ratings affected by this \par rating action can be found on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  public Web site at \par \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.standardandpoors.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.standardandpoors.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . Use the Ratings search box located in the left \par column. Alternativ