\par\par \b \uc2 Shekel Shrugs Off Elections\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chiara Albanese   \par \uc2 343 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 13:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Elections? What elections? Israel's markets are riding out the country's vote with no impact. For now.\par \par \uc2 The dollar is hovering at around 4 to the shekel as Israelis head to the polls Tuesday to decide whether prime minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  will remain in power.\par \par \uc2 That's around the shekel's weakest point against the buck since 2012, but is less dramatic than it sounds; the rallying dollar is mostly to blame. (The shekel's rally against the euro and stability against sterling tell a different tale.)\par \par \uc2 Could election nerves start to bite?\par \par \uc2 Unlikely, but possible. Mr. Netanyahu, leader of the conservative Likud, is seeking to gain consensus for a third straight term in office, but polls suggest that the race against Isaac Herzog of the center-left Zionist Union is unexpectedly close.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  say they expect the whole process to be a non-event for markets, but "a complete deadlock in the election results may cause some short-term selloff in shekel and in bonds."\par \par \uc2 Betting against the shekel is still popular, but for other reasons. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for some time. Now, add sinking prices to the mix. The annual inflation rate fell to a seven-year low of -1% in February.\par \par \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  has revised its shekel forecasts lower, pitting the dollar at 4.05 in three months and 4.10 in 12 months. "Until the inflation outlook improves, the threat of another surprise dovish innovation from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should keep markets cautious about chasing shekel strength against \uc2 Bank of Israel\uc2 's preference," said analyst Bhaveer Shah at the bank.\par \par \uc2 "Monetary policy divergence and recent evidence that \uc2 the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  has returned to sizeable \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  keep us bearish the shekel. While political uncertainty may marginally accelerate the bearish pressures, we do not think that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's outlook hinges on the election's outcome," Mr. Shah says. \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 17, 2015 08:55 ET (12:55 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150317eb3h001rb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
