\par\par \b \uc2 *S&P Affirms Israel 'A+/A-1' Ratings; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 2,601 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Februar 2015\par 17:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 13 Feb 2015 11:43 ET  Press Release: \uc2 S&P\uc2  Affirms Israel 'A+/A-1' Ratings; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2 : \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- In our view, the outcome of Israel's elections in March 2015 should \par not dampen the country's healthy growth prospects and stable fiscal \par trajectory. \par      -- We are therefore affirming our 'A+/A-1' foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par sovereign credit ratings on Israel. \par      -- The stable outlook reflects our view that the government will maintain \par stable public finances and that the impact of security risks on the Israeli \par economy will be contained over 2015-2018. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On Feb. 13, 2015, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ratings Services affirmed its 'A+/A-1' \par long- and short-term foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on \par the State of Israel. The outlook is stable. \par  \par RATIONALE \par The ratings are supported by Israel's core credit strengths, such as its \par prosperous and diverse economy, strong external balances, and its relatively \par flexible monetary framework. \par  \par We estimate economic growth to have slowed in 2014 to 2.5%, the lowest rate of \par growth since 2009. The conflict with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hamas\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Gaza in the third quarter \par exacerbated lower investment activity that had already started to slip early \par in the year. For 2015-2018, we forecast that GDP growth will return to more \par than 3%. In per capita terms, this equates to trend growth slightly exceeding \par 1.5% per year. Income per capita is close to $38,000 making Israel one of the \par highest-income economies globally while exhibiting higher trend growth versus \par its peers. \par  \par The consequences of the recent military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the instability in the West \par Bank, and the perception of potential escalation at Israel's northern border \par will likely push up defense spending, regardless of the next government's \par political composition after the general elections in March 2015. So far, we \par understand the military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's direct costs are limited. The general \par government deficit in 2014 amounted to just 2.8% of GDP. We anticipate that \par the government will substantially increase the defense budget in 2015. In our \par view, expenditure cuts elsewhere will not fully offset this hike. In addition, \par tax-rich domestic demand will likely dip below-trend and in turn lead to slow \par revenue growth. We believe this combination will likely lead to moderately \par higher deficits. \par  \par Subtracting all liquid assets from gross government debt, we project that net \par debt this year will remain at about 65% of GDP, matching the year-end 2010 \par number using Israel's revised and enlarged GDP figures following the 2013 \par methodological change by under the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Central Bureau of Statistics\f28 \uc2 \f2 . This forecast \par is based on our expectation of average deficits of 3.0% of GDP per year in \par 2015-2018. In light of annual growth prospects slightly above 3%, we regard \par such deficit levels as consistent with a stable net government debt burden. We \par think favorable interest rates will help lower the government's interest \par payments to about 10% of revenues in the next few years. \par  \par By contrast, Israel's external fundamentals remain strong. Israel continues to \par improve its net creditor position versus the rest of the world by running \par small but consistent current account surpluses. We forecast that its narrow \par net external asset position will strengthen to 23%-25% of current account \par receipts (CARs) over the next three years. This dynamic is also lowering the \par country's gross financing needs, which will likely only make up 76% of CARs \par and usable reserves in 2015--an all-time low dependency on external financing. \par  \par We consider Israel's monetary policy flexibility to be a credit strength. \par However, as we have previously stated, we no longer consider the Israeli \par shekel to be a free-floating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Israel\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (BoI, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central \par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) has become increasingly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventionist\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , over and above its commitment \par to purchase foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to offset the impact of domestic natural gas \par production on the balance of payments. \par  \par In addition to frequent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, the BoI \par has eased its stance on monetary policy to counter the shekel's strength. It \par lowered its policy rate to a historical low of 0.25% in August 2014. Given the \par current low inflation, we consider a move toward unconventional monetary \par policy possible. Its policies are partially responsible for the shekel's rapid \par depreciation (roughly 15% in nominal terms since August). The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had \par benefited from a largely balanced current account, very strong inward foreign \par direct investment, and capital inflows on the financial account, but its \par strength was damaging the export sector. \par  \par Another challenge continues to be Israel's rising house prices. The \par government's dissolution in December 2014 will likely eliminate plans to \par cancel value-added tax (VAT) charges on first-time home buyers. Additional \par reforms to increase supply by freeing up more land for development could still \par be implemented, but we think price stabilizing effects remain unlikely in the \par near term. Macroprudential measures appear to have contained systemic risks to \par Israel's banking industry, but any correction in house prices could still have \par other negative economic effects. \par  \par We consider Israel's institutions to be generally effective, with a \par satisfactory degree of transparency and accountability. However, the \par persistent territorial dispute with the Palestinians, by inserting another \par dividing line in the electorate and distorting policy decisions, contributes \par to a lack of political stability and limited policy predictability. \par  \par The constraints on our rating are even greater when we take into account the \par direct geopolitical risks. Repeated violent clashes with the Palestinians not \par only inflict economic costs, but could also risk reactions by the \par international community. The northern border, the conflict in Syria-Iraq, and \par instability in the Sinai region pose medium-term security risks. Any \par significant armed conflict could have a negative impact on the ratings if it \par significantly deters investment, weakens the economy's growth potential, or \par strains fiscal flexibility. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The stable outlook on Israel reflects our opinion that the outcome of the \par March 2015 elections will not undermine prudent public finances and the \par stabilization of government debt over 2015-2018. We also expect the impact of \par security risks on the Israeli economy to continue to be contained. \par  \par We could consider raising our ratings on Israel if fiscal consolidation \par exceeds our expectations or if there is marked progress in defusing external \par security risks. \par  \par Conversely, we could lower the ratings if we see a significant setback in \par reducing the government's high debt, a significant decline in Israel's growth \par prospects, a substantial deterioration in security, or if the perceived loss \par of international support were to carry adverse economic consequences. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RATINGS SCORE SNAPSHOT \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA AND RESEARCH \par  \par Related Criteria \par      -- Sovereign Government Rating Methodology And Assumptions, December 23, \par 2014 \par      -- Methodology For Linking Short-Term And Long-Term Ratings For \par Corporate, Insurance, And Sovereign Issuers, May 7, 2013 \par      -- Criteria For Determining Transfer And Convertibility Assessments, May \par 18, 2009 \par  \par Related Research \par      -- Sovereign Defaults And Rating Transition Data, 2013 Update, April 18, \par 2014 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to \par make an informed decision. \par  \par After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and explained the \par recommendation, the Committee discussed key rating factors and critical issues \par in accordance with the relevant criteria. Qualitative and quantitative risk \par factors were considered and discussed, looking at track-record and forecasts. \par  \par The committee agreed that all key rating factors were unchanged. \par  \par The chair ensured every voting member was given the opportunity to articulate \par his/her opinion. The chair or designee reviewed the draft report to ensure \par consistency with the Committee decision. The views and the decision of the \par rating committee are summarized in the above rationale and outlook. The \par weighting of all rating factors is described in the methodology used in this \par rating action (see 'Related Criteria And Research'). \par  \par RATINGS LIST \par Ratings Affirmed \par  \par Israel (State of) \par  Sovereign Credit Rating                A+/Stable/A-1 \par  Transfer & Convertibility Assessment   AA \par  Senior Unsecured                       A+ \par  \par Complete ratings information is available to subscribers of RatingsDirect at \par \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.globalcreditportal.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.globalcreditportal.com}}}\uc2 \f2  and at spcapitaliq.com. All ratings affected by \par this rating action can be found on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  public Web site at \par \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.standardandpoors.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.standardandpoors.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . Use the Ratings search box located in the left \par column.  Alternatively, call one of the following \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  numbers: \par Client Support Europe (44) 20-7176-7176; London Press Office (44) \par 20-7176-3605; Paris (33) 1-4420-6708; Frankfurt (49) 69-33-999-225; Stockholm \par (46) 8-440-5914; or Moscow 7 (495) 783-4009. \par  \par Primary Credit Analyst: Elliot Hentov, PhD, London (44) 207-176-7071; \par                         elliot.hentov@standardandpoors.com \par Secondary Contact: Alois Strasser, Frankfurt (49) 69-33-999-240; \par                    alois.strasser@standardandpoor