\par\par \b \uc2 *S&P Affirms Israel Ratings At 'A+/A-1'; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 2,577 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2014\par 17:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 19 Sep 2014 11:45 ET  Press Release: \uc2 S&P\uc2  Affirms Israel Ratings At 'A+/A-1'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2 : \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- In our view, the recent Gaza conflict will lead to only a modest \par weakening of Israel's fiscal trajectory. Although Israel may temporarily \par reverse its fiscal consolidation, we expect its gross general government debt \par ratio to remain largely flat in the next three years. \par      -- We are therefore affirming our 'A+/A-1' foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par sovereign credit ratings on Israel. \par      -- The stable outlook reflects our view that the government will maintain \par stable public finances and that the impact of security risks on the Israeli \par economy will be contained. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On Sept. 19, 2014, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ratings Services affirmed its 'A+/A-1' \par long- and short-term foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on \par the State of Israel. The outlook is stable. \par  \par RATIONALE \par Although the recent fighting in Gaza is a reminder of the long-term threat \par posed by geopolitical risks, we consider that in the short term, the effect \par will only be to accentuate the economic slowdown and modestly weaken the \par fiscal account. The fighting has not changed our view of Israel's core credit \par strengths, such as its prosperous and diverse economy, the contribution of \par natural gas production to a healthy external balance, and its relatively \par flexible monetary framework. \par  \par Economic activity was already slowing in the first half of 2014, and the \par July-August fighting is likely to have constrained economic activity in the \par third quarter as well. As a result, we are lowering our projection for 2014 \par real GDP growth to 2.3%, which represents the lowest rate of growth since \par 2009, nearly one point lower than our estimate six months ago. The new \par projection includes an estimated contribution from new natural gas production \par of about 0.3%. For 2015-2017, we forecast that GDP growth will return to \par 2012-2013 levels, slightly above 3%. In per capita terms, this equates to \par trend growth of slightly less than 1.5% per year. Income per capita is now \par above $38,000, making Israel one of the highest-income economies, in our view. \par Israel's trend growth is also at the higher spectrum of its peer group. \par  \par Weaker growth and the implications for future defense policy of the recent \par fighting make improving public finances more difficult. However, the military \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's direct costs were limited and the general government deficit in \par 2014 should only amount to 3.3% of GDP. Although the government has not yet \par agreed on the 2015 budget, we anticipate that the defense budget will enjoy a \par sizable increase that cannot fully be offset by expenditure cuts elsewhere. In \par addition, below-trend economic growth is likely to produce a slight revenue \par shortfall. The combination is likely to lead to moderately higher deficits. \par  \par We now expect general government debt to increase by an average of 3.1% of GDP \par per year through 2017, 0.5% higher than we forecast before the 2014 conflict \par began. We consider this level of debt consistent with a stable net government \par debt burden. Subtracting all liquid assets from gross government debt, we \par project that net debt levels will remain about 65% of GDP, which was the 2013 \par year-end level. We expect favorable interest rates to help lower the \par government's interest payments to about 10% of revenues by 2017. \par  \par By contrast, Israel's external fundamentals remain strong. Israel continues to \par improve its net creditor position regarding the rest of the world--its narrow \par net external asset position is forecast to strengthen to 25% of current \par account receipts (CARs) over the next three years. We project that the current \par account will remain consistently positive in 2014, averaging 1.5% of GDP, and \par that the country's gross financing needs will continue to decline to 79% of \par CARs and usable reserves in 2014. \par  \par We consider monetary policy flexibility to be a credit strength. however, as \par we have previously stated, we no longer consider the Israeli shekel to be a \par free-floating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Israel\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (BoI, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) has become \par increasingly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventionist\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , over and above its commitment to purchase \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to offset the effect of natural gas production on the balance \par of payments. \par  \par The BoI has begun employing ultra-loose monetary policy to counter the \par strength of the shekel. In August, it lowered the policy rate to a historical \par low of 0.25%. Given the current low inflation, we consider a move toward \par unconventional monetary policy possible. Its policies are helping to stem, if \par not reverse, the upward pressure on the shekel. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had benefited \par from a largely balanced current account, very strong inward foreign direct \par investment, and capital in-flows on the financial account, but its strength \par was damaging the export sector. \par  \par In this regard, the timing may be fortunate as government policy uncertainty \par may have temporarily cooled off the housing sector. The government has not yet \par implemented its plans to eliminate value-added tax (VAT) charges on first-time \par home buyers and increase supply by freeing up more land for development, so \par their effect remains uncertain. Meanwhile, macroprudential measures appear to \par have contained systemic risks to Israel's banking industry. \par  \par We consider Israel's institutions to be generally effective, with a \par satisfactory degree of transparency and accountability. The persistent \par territorial dispute with the Palestinians contributes to a lack of stability \par and limited policy predictability by inserting another dividing line in the \par electorate and distorting policy debates. \par  \par The constraints on our rating are even greater when we take into account the \par direct geopolitical risks. Repeated violent clashes with the Palestinians not \par only inflict economic costs, but could also risk inviting international \par repercussions. The war in Syria-Iraq and instability in Sinai pose medium-term \par security risks, although recent trends have been favorable to Israel. Any \par significant armed conflict could have a negative impact on the ratings if it \par significantly deters investment, weakens the economy's growth potential, or \par strains fiscal flexibility. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The stable outlook reflects our opinion that the consensus in Israel's \par government to maintain prudent public finances will continue to anchor fiscal \par planning and stabilize government debt. We also expect the impact of security \par risks on the Israeli economy to continue to be contained. \par  \par We could consider raising our ratings on Israel if fiscal consolidation \par exceeds our expectations or if it makes material progress in defusing external \par security risks. \par  \par Conversely, we could lower the ratings if we see a significant setback in \par reducing the government's high debt burden, a significant decline in growth \par prospects, a substantial deterioration of the security situation, or a loss of \par international support. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RATINGS SCORE SNAPSHOT \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA AND RESEARCH \par  \par Related Criteria \par      -- Sovereign Government Rating Methodology And Assumptions, June 24, 2013 \par      -- Methodology For Linking Short-Term And Long-Term Ratings For \par Corporate, Insurance, And Sovereign Issuers, May 7, 2013 \par      -- Criteria For Determining Transfer And Convertibility Assessments, May \par 18, 2009 \par  \par Related Research \par      -- Sovereign Defaults And Rating Transition Data, 2013 Update, April 18, \par 2014 \par      -- Banking Industry Country Risk Assessment: Israel, Aug. 28, 2013 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to \par make an informed decision. \par  \par After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and explained the \par recommendation, the Committee discussed key rating factors and critical issues \par in accordance with the relevant criteria. Qualitative and quantitative risk \par factors were considered and discussed, looking at track-record and forecasts. \par  \par The committee agreed that all key rating factors were unchanged. \par  \par The chair ensured every voting member was given the opportunity to articulate \par his/her opinion. The chair or designee reviewed the draft report to ensure \par consistency with the Committee decision. The views and the decision of the \par rating committee are summarized in the above rationale and outlook. The \par weighting of all rating factors is described in the methodology used in this \par rating action (see 'Related Criteria and Research'). \par  \par RATINGS LIST \par Ratings Affirmed \par  \par Israel (State of) \par  Sovereign Credit Rating                A+/Stable/A-1 \par  Transfer & Convertibility Assessment   AA \par  Senior Unsecured                       A+ \par  \par Complete ratings information is available to subscribers of RatingsDirect at \par \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.globalcreditportal.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.globalcreditportal.com}}}\uc2 \f2  and at spcapitaliq.com. All ratings affected by \par this rating action can be found on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  public Web site at \par \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.standardandpoors.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.standardandpoors.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . Use the Ratings search box located in the left \par column.  Alternatively, call one of the following \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  numbers: \par Client Support Europe (44) 20-7176-7176; London Press Office (44) \par 20-7176-3605; Paris (33) 1-4420-6708; Frankfurt (49) 69-33-999-225; Stockholm \par (46) 8-440-5914; or Moscow 7 (495) 783-4009. \par  \par Primary Credit Analyst: Elliot Hentov, PhD, London (44) 207-176-7071; \par                         elliot.hentov@standardandpoors.com \par Secondary Contact: Alois Strasser, Frankfurt (49) 69-33-999-240; \par                    alois.strasser@standardandpoors.com \par Additional Contact: SovereignEurope; \par                     SovereignEurope@standardandpoors.com \par  \