\par\par \b \uc2 Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Israel \highlight\uc2  Continues  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 2014\par 18:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 12:31 EDT - \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Wednesday to slow the pace of appreciation of the shekel. In recent weeks the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been buying $200M to $300M a month as part of a purchase program to offset effects of local natural gas production. Despite pressure from politicians and exporters, \uc2 Israeli \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have said they would not favor setting an exchange rate floor, like was done in Switzerland and the Czech Republic. The shekel trading at 3.426 per US dollar. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com; @chiaraalbanese)\par \par \uc2 12:31 EDT - Over a nearly 4-decade timespan, the minimum wage in Canada barely budged on an inflation-adjusted basis, says Statistics Canada. It averaged C$10.14/hour last year, versus 1975's C$10.13 when converted into 2013 dollars. It fell on an inflation-adjusted basis through 1986 to C$7.53 before rising to C$8.81 in 1996. It increased another C$2 from 2003-13. Statcan says Canada's adjusted minimum wage represents 46% of average hourly wages paid to Canadian employees in 2013. (paul.vieira@wsj.com, @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 12:30 EDT - A proposal by a US financial regulator to expand its public online complaint database to provide more details about consumer gripes against financial institutions is likely to aggravate friction with the banking industry. The proposal, released today by the CFPB, would give consumers the option to allow a detailed description of their complaints to be posted on the bureau's website, expanding on short summaries the bureau already publishes. "By publicly voicing their complaint, consumers can stand up for themselves and others who have experienced the same problem," says CFPB Director Richard Cordray. (alan.zibel@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:27 EDT - Demand for the aluminum F-150 due out later this year will be strong, predicts \uc2 UBS\uc2 . Importantly, consumers may be willing to pay $3K more for it, though the added cost for Ford (F) using the metal versus steel is estimated at just $600/vehicle. F's real risk with the truck may be on the production side. Can it build 600K aluminum F-150s annually? No one has built even 10% as many all-aluminum vehicles in a year before. F rises 1.2% to $17.75. (michael.ramsey@wsj.com; @MRamseyWSJ)\par \par \uc2 12:25 EDT - Capital Economics, who have been bears about Canada's housing market and, to an extent, its economy, say the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's policy statement is "essentially the same message as before." It says that, as it had forewarned, the \uc2 BOC\uc2  now expects further delays in returning to full capacity. CapEcon notes the \uc2 BOC\uc2  downgraded its economic growth outlook, though not greatly, in response to the downgrade to its global outlook. The bottom line for CapEcon is that the \uc2 BOC\uc2 's statement supports its view that "interest rates will have to remain low for longer than the consensus view." (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 12:24 EDT - Manufacturing growth slowed to a crawl in June with production inching up 0.1%, slowest pace since January's weather related decline. Sectors with the greatest monthly growth included furniture and related products, which was up 1.4%, and fabricated metal products, up 1.2%. The losers: food, petroleum and coal products, which fell 2.7%. Chad Moutray, chief economist for the National Association of Manufacturers, said manufacturers are cautiously optimistic about production in 2H, but improvements need to come from a broader base. Manufacturing capacity utilization fell slightly to 77.1% in June from 77.2% in May, but remains above the 75.5% rate in January. (clare.ansberry@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:22 EDT - California's electric-grid manager says the state's solar resources have caught up with wind in terms of maximum output. Fortunately, they reach their peaks at different times of the day, making it easier for engineers to balance out their intermittent output. The latest solar peak of 4,795 megawatts of production was set at 11:43am June 1 while the wind-generation peak, at 4,768 MW, was achieved at 5:48pm April 12. The grid manager, called the \uc2 California Independent System Operator\uc2 , is responsible for making sure there's sufficient electricity being put on the state's electric network to satisfy demand. California's goal is to garner 1/3 of its electricity from renewable resources by 2020. It will need some 22,600 MW of renewable capacity to do so; there's 15,200 now. (rebecca.smith@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:20 EDT - US ethanol output remained robust last week, a sign the lowest corn prices since 2010 are enticing ethanol makers to grind more of the grain, according to Arlan Suderman at Water Street Solutions. Strong export demand is encouraging production as well, and should continue "at least into the third quarter," adding, "Ethanol plants still have good incentive to pump corn through the plant." US ethanol output rose to 943K barrels/day, versus 927K the prior week and 876K a year earlier, says the EIA. Signs of healthy demand from the ethanol sector and foreign corn buyers help corn futures rebound slightly today; CBOT September is up 1.2% at $3.785/bushel. (david.kesmodel@wsj.com; @davidkesmodel)\par \par \uc2 12:20 EDT - Global assets under management rose 13% in 2013 to $68.7T, but most of that growth was due to the surge in asset prices, according to \uc2 Boston Consulting Group\uc2 's 12th annual study of the industry. New asset flows increased to 1.2% of prior assets under management, their strongest post-crisis gain for the second year in a row, up from 1.2% in 2012. But pre-crisis gains ranged from 3% to 6%, the report says. The 2013 flows were driven primarily by retail investors' revived interest in equity markets as well as the continued resilience of institutional investors, the consulting group says. Asset management continues to rank among the most profitable industries, it says. (daisy.maxey@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:16 EDT - Who were highest rollers among World Cup fans in Brazil? According to a study of 20 World Cup nations by Brazilian credit- and debit-card processor Cielo, tourists from the Netherlands were the biggest spenders. The Dutch charged an average of 446 Brazilian reais ($200.70) per purchase to their cards in Brazil between June 12 and July 8. Switzerland and Russia rounded out the top-three spenders by nation, while cash-strapped Argentines were the thriftiest tourists, charging an average of just BRL127 on their credit and debit cards. US tourists, with average receipts of BRL198, were the third-lowest spenders after Spain and Argentina. (paul.kiernan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:12 EDT - Bank of Canada Governor Stephen Poloz acknowledges the significance of the lower Canadian dollar for the country's struggling export sector in remarks at a news conference Wednesday, describing the exchange rate as "a very important variable." But Poloz's recognition of the assist exports get from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped well short of the strong rhetoric that's been coming from RBA Governor Glenn Stevens, who has described the Australian dollar's levels as essentially out of alignment with economic fundamentals. Make no mistake, though, the \uc2 BOC\uc2  will keep a very close eye on the loonie, and a sustained strengthening in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would play a role in its policymaking process. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 12:10 EDT - Gross margin has been a good story for Intel (INTC) lately, as it showed with the company's 2Q results and prediction of a near-record 66% for 3Q margin. Enjoy it while it lasts, advises RBC. INTC this fall will begin recording higher costs for initial chips built using its next production process, and the producer is projecting a 63% gross margin for all of 2014. That implies a substantial 4Q dip. Investors are seemingly focused on the now, with INTC up 7% at $33.93 in hitting a fresh 10-year high and making up 1/4 of the Dow industrial's 60-point advance. (don.clark@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com \par Cynthia Lin, 212 416-4403, Cynthia.lin@wsj.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181, john.shipman@wsj.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326, Patrick.sullivan@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 16, 2014 12:31 ET (16:31 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140716ea7g002tj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
