\par\par \b \uc2 Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Israel \highlight\uc2  Continues  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,450 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 2014\par 18:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 12:31 EDT - \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Wednesday to slow the pace of appreciation of the shekel. In recent weeks the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been buying $200M to $300M a month as part of a purchase program to offset effects of local natural gas production. Despite pressure from politicians and exporters, \uc2 Israeli \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have said they would not favor setting an exchange rate floor, like was done in Switzerland and the Czech Republic. The shekel trading at 3.426 per US dollar. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com; @chiaraalbanese)\par \par \uc2 12:31 EDT - Over a nearly 4-decade timespan, the minimum wage in Canada barely budged on an inflation-adjusted basis, says Statistics Canada. It averaged C$10.14/hour last year, versus 1975's C$10.13 when converted into 2013 dollars. It fell on an inflation-adjusted basis through 1986 to C$7.53 before rising to C$8.81 in 1996. It increased another C$2 from 2003-13. Statcan says Canada's adjusted minimum wage represents 46% of average hourly wages paid to Canadian employees in 2013. (paul.vieira@wsj.com, @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 1629 GMT [Dow Jones]--Spain's Budget minister Cristobal Montoro Wednesday said minor changes to tax reform are expected in new bill to be unveiled July 25. But across-the-board income tax cuts should stay, while VAT will stay unchanged. Responding to critics, Mr. Montoro said in an interview that Spain's recovering economy will still allow for higher receipts--via the Laffer curve--while making it easier to cut expenses as a percentage of GDP, after not-quite-radical austerity cuts during the previous two years of economic contraction. (david.roman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1627 GMT [Dow Jones]--Spain's Budget minister Cristobal Montoro Wednesday rejected calls to give Catalonia the power to collect taxes. In an interview, he said Spain is not prepared to embark on what he called a "reinvention" of the state to mollify the wealthy region's separatist leadership. Comments come as Spanish yields remain close to all-time lows, evidence, Mr. Montoro says, that markets are not spooked by the prospect of a Nov. 9 Catalan referendum on independence that Madrid vows to stop, or at least ignore. (david.roman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:25 EDT - Capital Economics, who have been bears about Canada's housing market and, to an extent, its economy, say the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's policy statement is "essentially the same message as before." It says that, as it had forewarned, the \uc2 BOC\uc2  now expects further delays in returning to full capacity. CapEcon notes the \uc2 BOC\uc2  downgraded its economic growth outlook, though not greatly, in response to the downgrade to its global outlook. The bottom line for CapEcon is that the \uc2 BOC\uc2 's statement supports its view that "interest rates will have to remain low for longer than the consensus view." (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 12:12 EDT - Bank of Canada Governor Stephen Poloz acknowledges the significance of the lower Canadian dollar for the country's struggling export sector in remarks at a news conference Wednesday, describing the exchange rate as "a very important variable." But Poloz's recognition of the assist exports get from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped well short of the strong rhetoric that's been coming from RBA Governor Glenn Stevens, who has described the Australian dollar's levels as essentially out of alignment with economic fundamentals. Make no mistake, though, the \uc2 BOC\uc2  will keep a very close eye on the loonie, and a sustained strengthening in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would play a role in its policymaking process. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 1605 GMT [Dow Jones]--A small group of troika experts in Athens for talks departed Wednesday, and the full team will return in the second half of September to kick off the fifth review of the country's bailout program. While the hope is that the process will wrap up in October, \uc2 European Union\uc2  officials warn that --if the past is anything to go by-- the review will run for longer and the debt negotiations could be pushed further back to the end of the year. Another key piece of Greece's debt puzzle, that is the EUR11 billion left over in its bank-recapitalization fund, won't be available to slot in until after the ECB completes its stress tests and asset-quality review in October. Given these two roadblocks, official talks between Greece, its euro-zone and IMF creditors and the other troika institutions may not properly start until well into November. The timing could affect the electoral calendar in Greece too, as analysts have seen a successful conclusion of the debt talks as a boon for Prime Minister Antonis Samaras, and a good springboard for early elections. (Matina.Stevis@wsj.com / @MatinaStevis)\par \par \uc2 1602 GMT The Irish government's plan to sell down most of the 32 billion euros ($43.52 billion) in loan assets held by the \uc2 National Asset Management Agency\uc2  by 2016, years earlier than planned, is further evidence of economic recovery. The sales reduce the country's liabilities, but there could be a more tangible benefit: the accelerated schedule of NAMA asset sales and the repayment of the special bank bonds will also "enhance the value" of \uc2 Allied Irish Banks PLC\uc2 , one of the country's largest banks and almost entirely government-owned, when the government eventually decides to sell a stake, Finance Minister Michael Noonan said. Wednesday. (eamon.quinn@wsj.com \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @EamonQuinnNews)\par \par \uc2 12:02 EDT - Stanley Druckenmiller, CEO of the Duquesne Family Office, continues to hammer the Fed's policies, but he stops short of saying he's betting on an economic downturn. "I don't have great certainty in how this will end. I don't even know it will end badly. What I am sure is they are making a bad bet -- a bad risk/reward," he says. "You can [win] the lottery and it was still a bad bet." Speaking at CNBC's Delivering Alpha conference, Druckenmiller also dismissed deflationary risk, pointing out that CPI growth has been steady. "It is long past time to move on," from low interest rates under the guise of deflationary pressures, he says. (rob.copeland@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:01 EDT - Weather has been the underlying theme of all the factors behind the 14% gas price drop this summer. As unseasonably cold as it was this winter, driving demand for gas heating, the summer has matched it, sapping demand from the market. It just isn't hot enough for people to turn on the air conditioners, and that means they aren't using electricity and the natural gas that fuels it. Last week alone, cooling degree days were down 21.3% nationwide compared to the same week a year ago, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says. The northern states from New England to the Midwest were the coolest: Cooling degree days there all dropped more than 35% from last year, the bank says. (tim.puko@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:53 EDT - Capital Economics forecasts that total return from emerging market dollar-denominated bonds will be barely positive next year, as less accommodative US monetary policy dulls investors' appetite for risk. This will in turn prompt a gradual rise in the yields of underlying US Treasurys. Economist John Higgins is more bullish on equities, expecting the total return from emerging market equities to reach nearly 7%. "This is roughly double the return we project from developed market equities, partly because the valuation of emerging market equities still appears quite low in relative terms," he says. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com; @chiaraalbanese)\par \par \uc2 1545 GMT [Dow Jones] --Plans to make Greece's debt more manageable while keeping the country on a short leash by linking debt-relief steps to reforms may not be credible, Eurasia's Europe director Mujtaba Rahman says. The IMF, which has demanded cuts to Greece's massive debt, is likely to seek harder commitments from the Europeans when talks kick off in earnest later this year. The fear is that linking reforms necessary today with debt relief that will yield benefits a decade or more down the line won't exert the hoped-for pressure on the Greek government . "The IMF's experience in Greece will make them cautious, as even with serious conditionality, full Troika oversight and extensive peer pressure, the Greek government has only been in compliance with a fraction of its commitments," Mr. Rahman says. (Matina.Stevis@wsj.com / @MatinaStevis)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 16, 2014 12:31 ET (16:31 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140716ea7g002xs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
