\par\par \b \uc2 Limited Growth Spurt from French Labor Tax Cuts -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,200 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 2014\par 15:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1315 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--French President Francois Hollande's cuts to labor taxes will only have a full effect on jobs in several years' time, says Oddo Securities economist Bruno Cavalier. "You do not create a job just to receive a tax credit," says Mr. Cavalier. Adds employers are still not convinced uncertainty over taxes is over. "The immediate support for growth will be non-existent or very modest," says Mr. Cavalier. (william.horobin@dowjones.com)z\f28 \par \par \uc2 9:12 EDT - US retail sales for June appear better under the hood, but the dollar's reaction was restrained regardless as investors are holding their powder ahead of Yellen's Humphrey-Hawkins appearance. The retail sales control group--which includes volatile items such as gas, food and building materials--came in slightly higher than forecast, and that's what matters for the GDP calculations, says Vassili Serebriakov at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . The euro fell against the dollar to $1.36-even from $1.3612 pre-release while the dollar rose to Y101.66 from Y101.60. (james.ramage@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:03 EDT - Bond prices now tick lower as traders focus on bright side of economic data. While headline June retail sales disappoint, the core sales excluding autos, building materials and gasoline stations, a gauge economists use to track 2Q growth, posted a bigger gain of 0.5%. NY Fed Empire State index hit the highest level in more than four years. Traders say fresh signs of improvement in the economy would pile pressure on Yellen. Some investors lighten up on bonds as they're concerned Yellen may sound hawkish in upcoming testimony. The 10-year Treasury note is 2/32 lower, yielding 2.556%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 8:51 EDT - Two weeks ago, auto makers reported another gain in US vehicle sales. But the Commerce Department says today in its retail report that dollar-value sales at auto dealers slipped 0.2% last month. The divergent between the two isn't unknown given differences in what's measured, timing and seasonal adjustments, but retail-sales forecasters expected a better showing in the retail number for autos. (kathleen.madigan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:42 EDT - Consumers cut back on many big-ticket items in June, a development which could signal weakness for the auto and housing markets. Overall retail sales climbed 0.2% from May--the slowest sequential increase since January. That was driven by higher purchases of food, health-care products, sporting goods and general merchandise. But sales of cars and auto parts fell 0.3% and building materials and garden equipment, which are closely linked to the housing market, fell 1% to log its sharpest drop since October. That suggests consumers remain cautious about spending and are restrained by weak wage growth, factors keeping the economy from shifting into a higher gear of growth. (joshua.mitchell@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:39 EDT - Treasury bonds are marginally higher after June retail sales rose 0.2% vs economists 0.6% forecast. That's offset somewhat by NY Fed's gauge of manufacturing which rose more than expected. A main focus today is Yellen's testimony before Congress at 10:00am. Traders believe Yellen may stick to her stance of taking time in raising interest rates. Any hint from Yellen signaling a sooner-than-expected rate hike would send bond yields higher. The 10-year Treasury note is 1/32 higher, yielding 2.545%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 8:31 EDT - The \uc2 New York Fed\uc2  factory survey shows gains in business conditions, new orders and shipments for this month, putting numbers at multiyear highs. Employment also picks up. Fed inflation watchers will also notice the pickup in prices. The New York State report shows indexes covering both input prices and selling prices rising in July, although price pressures earlier in 2014 were higher. (kathleen.madigan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1226 GMT The Israeli shekel gains sharply against the dollar after the \uc2 Israeli government\uc2  overnight accepted a ceasefire proposal. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at its strongest level versus the EUR/USD basket since 2002, \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says. "This market reaction is surprising to the extent that ILS did not come under any significant selling pressure through the past eight days of conflict \u8722\'22\'12 which we previously argued was consistent with the historical resilience of ILS to political risk," the strategists suggested. \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  highlighted \uc2 the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's potential \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the greater risk for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese)\par \par \uc2 1226 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Turkish lira has managed to fend off both the USD and EUR over the past couple of months despite a decline in the lira's carry advantage, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Turkey\uc2  continues to ease policy. Economists at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  expect the CBT to deliver a further 75bps rate cut Wednesday and say that the accumulation of recent cuts, combined with inflation that continues to surprise on the upside may start to weigh on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , leading them to stick with an overall bearish TRY view over coming months. USD/TRY now at 2.1210, EUR/TRY 2.8870. (gary.stride@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1208 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Weaker inflows into euro-zone equities are likely to translate into a weaker euro, says Jens Nordvig at Nomura. "This is important, since strong portfolio flows have consistently supported the euro over the last 1-2 years," he says, adding that the ECB is clearly going to remain easy for very long. This is not reflected yet in rate differentials. "Hence, the euro's resilience in crosses has a lot to do with global rates still being under downward pressure," he says. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com; @chiaraalbanese)\par \par \uc2 1151 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Credit market valuations are becoming less and less attractive for investors, says Giordano Lombardo, chief investment officer at Pioneer Investments. "Spread compression has continued across the whole ratings spectrum. The hunt for yield induced by central banks' zero interest rate policies has pushed the share of credit assets in household portfolios to unprecedented levels," he says. Meanwhile, liquidity in the trading market is fading. Even if investment-grade and high-yield credit retain some appeal in comparison with core government bonds, the upside potential is very limited. "Credit markets need to be handled carefully," he says. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese)\par \par \uc2 1142 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Gilts remain in the red after Tuesday's inflation data surprised to the upside (June CPI 1.9% y/y vs 1.6% expectations). This has pushed forward rate hike expectations, according to analysts at ING, who now see the first move in November. "This would also remove political concerns of waiting until early next year when election campaigning is in full swing and should keep sterling supported," says ING. The September gilt contract is 0.24 lower at 110.41 and the 10-year yields 2.631%. (nick.cawley@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 15, 2014 09:15 ET (13:15 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140715ea7f001w4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100