\par\par \b \uc2 *S&P Affirms Ratings On Israel At 'A+/A-1'; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 2,425 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 06:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 28 Mar 2014 01:02 EDT  Press Release: S&P Affirms Ratings On Israel At 'A+/A-1'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2 : \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- In our opinion, Israel's fiscal consolidation is on track, bolstered \par by extra one-off revenues in 2013 and upward revisions to GDP due to new \par calculation methodologies. We expect a modest decline in the gross general \par government debt ratio as a result. \par      -- We are therefore affirming our long- and short-term foreign and local \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on Israel at 'A+/A-1'. \par      -- The stable outlook reflects our view that the government will continue \par to consolidate the public finances and that the effect of security risks on \par the Israeli economy will be contained. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On March 28, 2014, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ratings Services affirmed its long- and \par short-term foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on the State of \par Israel at 'A+/A-1'. The outlook is stable. \par  \par RATIONALE \par The affirmation reflects our view of Israel's prosperous and diverse economy \par as well as the positive medium-term impact of natural gas production on the \par external account. The fiscal profile is improving but remains a rating \par weakness, while geopolitical risks are the biggest rating constraint. \par  \par We project real GDP growth of 3.2% in 2014, which is similar to the past two \par years. This includes the contribution of natural gas (about 0.4% of growth in \par 2014). For 2015-2017, we forecast growth to remain stable at about 3.4% \par annually, suggesting a modest upswing due to the impending recovery in \par Israel's key export markets. In GDP per capita terms, this equates to trend \par growth of close to 1.5% annually. At above $38,000, we now view Israel as a \par high-income economy, with trend growth at the higher end of its peer income \par group. \par  \par Public finances are improving due to stable growth, as well as last year's \par revisions to the national income accounts by the statistical office in line \par with OECD standards--notably the methodology for calculating GDP, which \par increased 2012 GDP by 6.9%. In combination with one-off revenues from \par corporate transactions of 0.6% of GDP, the general government deficit in 2013 \par amounted to 3.2% of GDP. Since the bulk of fiscal consolidation has been \par scheduled for 2013-2014, we expect an average annual increase in the nominal \par level of general government debt of 2.6% of GDP in the medium term \par (2014-2017). This should enable Israel's net government debt burden to \par modestly decline over the 2014-2017 forecast horizon to 61% of GDP by 2017 \par from 63% currently. This will largely be due to the increase in nominal \par general government debt over the period being slightly lower than the increase \par in GDP. Notably, the favorable interest rate environment is also helping lower \par the government's interest payments, which we expect to average about 10% of \par government revenues. \par  \par Israel's external fundamentals remain strong; it continues to improve its net \par creditor position vis-a-vis the rest of the world. We project its narrow net \par external asset position will strengthen to 42% of current account receipts \par (CARs) over the forecast horizon. We forecast the current account will remain \par narrowly positive again in 2014 at 0.8% of GDP before gradually increasing \par thereafter. We estimate that the country's gross financing needs will continue \par to decline and stand at 77% of CARs and usable reserves in 2014. \par  \par We consider monetary policy flexibility to be a rating strength. However, we \par no longer view the shekel as a free-floating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and we expect \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank \par of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Israel\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (BoI, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) to become increasingly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventionist\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . This \par is over and above its commitment to purchase \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in line with its \par assessment of the effect of natural gas production on the balance of payments \par and therefore to ward off appreciation pressures on the shekel. Further, a \par largely balanced current account, very strong foreign direct investment, and \par capital inflows on the financial account have added to appreciation pressures \par on the shekel, which is complicating the BoI's policy mix. Macro-prudential \par measures have failed to stem rapid price appreciation in the housing sector, \par but they do appear to have contained systemic risks. \par  \par We consider Israel's institutions to be generally effective with a \par satisfactory degree of transparency and accountability. Recent electoral \par reforms could address the weakest element in Israel's governance, namely a \par lack of stability and limited policy predictability. Nonetheless, we believe \par the effects will only be seen at the earliest in the next electoral cycle. \par Meanwhile, external security threats and territorial disputes weaken political \par stability by inserting another dividing line in the electorate and distorting \par policy debates. \par  \par Geopolitical risks are a rating constraint. In this respect, \par Israeli-Palestinian relations continue to be prone to violent outbreaks and \par also risk inviting other international repercussions. The war in Syria and \par instability in Sinai pose medium-term risks for deterioration, though recent \par trends have been favorable to Israel. Any significant armed conflict with \par Israel could further constrain the ratings if it significantly deters \par investment, weakens the economy's growth potential, or strains fiscal \par flexibility. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The stable outlook reflects our opinion that consensus within the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Israeli \par government\f28 \uc2 \f2  to consolidate the public finances will continue to anchor fiscal \par planning and reduce government debt. We also expect the impact of security \par risks on the Israeli economy to remain contained. \par  \par We could consider raising our ratings on Israel if it makes material progress \par in defusing external security risks. In our view, such progress would have \par positive implications for domestic stability, economic growth, and investor \par confidence. We could also consider an upgrade if fiscal consolidation \par continues to be successfully implemented, leading to declines in Israel's debt \par and interest burden. \par  \par Conversely, we believe that a significant setback in the government's fiscal \par position, a significant decline in growth prospects, or a substantial \par deterioration of the security situation in Israel could put downward pressure \par on the rating. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA AND RESEARCH \par  \par Related Criteria \par      -- Sovereign Government Rating Methodology And Assumptions, June 24, 2013 \par      -- Methodology For Linking Short-Term And Long-Term Ratings For \par Corporate, Insurance, And Sovereign Issuers, May 7, 2013. \par      -- Criteria For Determining Transfer And Convertibility Assessments, May \par 18, 2009 \par  \par Related Research \par      -- European Sovereign Debt Report 2014: Gross Commercial Borrowing To \par Decline 2.4% To EUR1.3 Trillion, Feb. 28, 2014 \par      -- Banking Industry Country Risk Assessment: Israel, Aug. 28, 2013 \par      -- Sovereign Defaults And Rating Transition Data, 2012 Update, March 29, \par 2013 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to \par make an informed decision. \par  \par After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and explained the \par recommendation, the Committee discussed key rating factors and critical issues \par in accordance with the relevant criteria. Qualitative and quantitative risk \par factors were considered and discussed, looking at track-record and forecasts. \par The chair ensured every voting member was given the opportunity to articulate \par his/her opinion. The chair or designee reviewed the draft report to ensure \par consistency with the Committee decision. The views and the decision of the \par rating committee are summarized in the above rationale and outlook. \par  \par RATINGS LIST \par Ratings Affirmed \par  \par Israel (State of) \par  Sovereign Credit Rating                A+/Stable/A-1 \par  Transfer & Convertibility Assessment   AA \par  Senior Unsecured                       A+ \par  \par Complete ratings information is available to subscribers of RatingsDirect at \par \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.globalcreditportal.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.globalcreditportal.com}}}\uc2 \f2  an