\par\par \b \uc2 Israel  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  chief warns of more steps to cool housing prices\b0\par\par\uc2 By Steven Scheer   \par \uc2 524 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 14:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, March 4 (Reuters) - \uc2 The Bank of Israel\uc2  may take further steps to cool the housing market if fast growth in mortgages continues, Governor Karnit Flug said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Speaking at a conference, Flug noted home prices rose 8 percent in 2013, which also saw a rapid increase in the number of new mortgages taken out. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already taken measures such as tightening mortgage rules.\par \par \uc2 But with interest rates very low and supply rising more slowly than demand, real estate has become a preferred mode of investment in Israel. Home prices have jumped 80 percent in nominal terms since 2007 and the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  recently said Israeli property prices were 25 percent above their equilibrium level.\par \par \uc2 "The continued rapid growth in the number of mortgages and in their weight in the bank's credit portfolio are a risk factor, and if additional measures are required, we will not hesitate to take them," Flug said.\par \par \uc2 She said the rise seen in building starts and completions in 2013 was a positive development, adding that if the trend continues and if it includes areas of high demand, "it will act to reduce the pressure and stabilise home prices".\par \par \uc2 Despite rising home costs, \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2  unexpectedly lowered its benchmark interest rate to 0.75 percent from 1.0 percent on February 24. It was the first cut since September and reflected concerns about weak global economic growth and the local labour market.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Flug noted that Israeli economic growth had slowed to below 2.5 percent in the second half of 2013, hit by weak exports that she said were mainly concentrated in the pharmaceuticals, chemicals and electronics sectors.\par \par \uc2 Flug cited worries that most of the country's job growth has been in non-governmental public services, while private sector work hours have declined and involuntary part-time jobs have risen. "These reflect a certain weakness in the labour market," she said.\par \par \uc2 Flug also pointed to the fragile recovery in Europe, recent weak data in the United States and some weakness in large emerging market countries. "Growth in Israel moves in line with, and higher than, that of the other advanced economies, and we expect that both the correlation and the gap will continue this year as well," she said.\par \par \uc2 Inflation has been tame at an annual rate of 1.4 percent in January and expectations for the coming year are well within the government's 1-3 percent range.\par \par \uc2 "The decline in inflation, together with expectations of moderation in inflation, enabled and even supported the reduction of the interest rate, which is intended to support activity and growth," Flug said.\par \par \uc2 She added that \uc2 the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as it battles a strong shekel. It bought $1.73 billion in January to bring foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves above $83 billion. Flug said studies show that high reserves reduce the likelihood of a financial crisis. (Reporting by Steven Scheer; Editing by Catherine Evans)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-7T13:18:05.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140304ea3400lx2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
