\par\par \b \uc2 Treasurys Claw Back Earlier Declines -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,197 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2014\par 14:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 8:39 EST - The euphoria following Turkey's bold interest-rate hike hasn't lasted, with emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  sliding against the dollar and US stock-index futures moving deeper into the red by the minute. That renewed risk-off environment helps Treasurys claw back earlier weakness. But despite the EM-fueled volatility, analysts believe it won't change the Fed's path on tapering further; another $10B is anticipated from today's FOMC statement, of which a key focus will also be whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthens its forward guidance and how they characterize recent mixed data. Ten-year Treasurys are now flat, yielding 2.746%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 1336 GMT [Dow Jones] After a brief, post-rate-hike respite for the rand, it sinks sharply, with USD/ZAR racing up to a fresh day high of 11.2432 from 11.05. USD/TRY is dragged along for the ride, wiping out its post rate-hike fall seen overnight to trade at a fresh day high of 2.2599. JPY grabs a strong safety bid, USD/JPY now 100sen lower on the day at 102.48, EUR/JPY almost 200sen lower at 139.46, ECB's Noyer's warning on euro strength also weighs here. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1336 GMT [Dow Jones] The Hungarian forint weakens significantly versus EUR as the weakening Turkish lira affects all emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . 4Cast economist Gabor Ambrus says \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rate hikes by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  both seem possible as the new Monetary Policy Council is facing an unprecedented dilemma. EUR/HUF jumped to above 308 earlier today, although the cross has since retreated to 306.90. Matters and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement in Turkey are affecting Hungary more than other EM countries because both countries have reached historically unprecedented degrees of looseness in monetary policy - and also in both, there are valid question marks being raised about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  independence, Mr. Ambrus says. Furthermore, due to elevated foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt levels, both economies are sensitive to \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, he adds. In a multi-week framework, 4Cast expects further pressure on HUF with rising odds of significant market disturbances. (veronika.gulyas@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1329 GMT [Dow Jones] Euro racks up a series of fresh day lows after ECB's Noyer headline on Reuters that "it is clear any rise in Euro would be negative". EUR/USD falls to 1.3603 from around 1.3635, EUR/JPY sinks to 139.70 from 140.05, EUR/CHF 1.2251 from 1.2260 and EUR/GBP 0.8221 from 0.8240, no sign of any bounce yet. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1255 GMT 0000 GMT (Dow Joes) \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's widely suspected but officially unconfirmed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the shekel on Monday and Tuesday hasn't had much in the way of lasting effect. Indeed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already returned to the status quo ante and is above the level it hit prior to the BOI's move. Traders expected a bounceback given the modest, $400 million or so estimated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Indeed, Etyan Admoni, manager of financial markets at the Bank of Jerusalem derided it as 'peanuts.' The shekel remains in demand given Israel's strong fundamentals, however much local exporters might like a break. (saratoth@gmail.com)\par \par \uc2 1243 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD erases session gains and the break of 1.3660 sees the downside accelerate to a fresh day low of 1.3632, which just happens to be its 20DMA, which proved to be the base on Tuesday. Now a touch higher at 1.3639. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1203 GMT [Dow Jones] So, has Turkey drawn up the definitive blueprint for other embattled emerging market central banks to follow when their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  face investors' wrath? That's today's big question, no doubt, or at least it will be until we hear from the Fed. Capital Economics attempts and answer, and thinks not. Its analysis suggests that, while a handful of monetary authorities might follow Turkey's lead, most will hold back as domestic considerations trump all else. david.cottle@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1202 GMT [Dow Jones] The forint has fallen sharply against the euro Wednesday. One trader at least wonders if the market might be trying to force the \uc2 Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hand having seen Turkey's shock-and-awe policy tightening. We don't know why the forint is weakening this sharply but maybe markets think Hungary is the next place where they can force a rate hike, he says, seeing no other obvious reason for the forint's slide. Hungarian rates were cut to 2.85% last week, in the eighteenth straight cut since the start of 2012, and, although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stuck to its dovish stance, some economists think its come to the end of the easing cycle. Governor Matolcsy is slated to speak at 1215 GMT. (margit.feher@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1151 GMT [Dow Jones] The \uc2 Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's overnight rate hikes may have shaken things up in emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but Standard Bank says there's been little change in its indicators of developed market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trends. One standout is that indicators remain biased to JPY strength, which the bank says could indicate that volatility and weakness in 'riskier' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is set to persist. USD/JPY just slipped to a fresh day low of 102.60, USD/TRY trades at 2.2370. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1145 GMT [Dow Jones] Failing at the previous point of break-down at 103.44, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  technical analyst Karen Jones says USD/JPY's rebound has not done enough to restore upside pressure. While capped below trendline resistance at 103.70, the near-term outlook is now for a decline to target 101.00, possibly 100.00. Now at 102.68. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1133 GMT [Dow Jones] Sterling took a knock last Friday when BOE Governor Mark Carney mentioned that its strength had hurt exports. He speaks again today at 1315GMT in Edinburgh, his subject being Scottish Independence and sterling. It looks like the market is taking no chances this time around, positioning itself short ahead of the event with sterling slipping to fresh day lows of $1.6558 and EUR0.8258. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1132 GMT [Dow Jones] Core government bonds have shrugged off the 'risk-on' effect of the overnight Turkish rate hikes and pushed back into positive territory. The March Bund is 0.10 higher at 142.56, after having hit a low of 142.25, with the market comfortably placing a EUR 5 billion 10-year Bund tap mid-morning. In the UK bond market, March gilts are 0.15 higher at 109.46, off a low of 109.05, as the UK DMO looks prices a successful syndicated sale of GBP 4.75 billion 2068 i/l gilts. The \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  remains negative, just, trading 1/32 lower at 125-015 after having hit a nadir of 124-230. (nick.cawley@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 29, 2014 08:39 ET (13:39 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140129ea1t0021p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
