\par\par \b \uc2 UK Market Talk Roundup: Brokers Comments\b0\par\par\uc2 3,148 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2014\par 16:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Broker comments in the UK today. Compiled by Dow Jones Newswires Markets Desk, markets.eu@dowjones.com \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1509 GMT [Dow Jones] UK's FTSE 100 index rises 0.2% to 6851.49, supported by a positive outlook on the economy. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  hikes its UK growth outlook for 2014 to 2.4% from an earlier forecast for growth of 1.8%. Attention now shifts to the US, where investors have returned to their desks after a three-day break owing to the Martin Luther King holiday. US stocks have started on the front foot, buoying sentiment in the UK, after \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  injected funds into money markets. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Unilever\f28 \uc2 \f2  continues to post strong gains, with shares rising 1.9%, following well-received 4Q results. Outside the FTSE 100, \f28 \uc2 \f2 African Barrick Gold\f28 \uc2 \f2  advances 3.6% as 4Q gold production beats FY production and cash cost guidance. (nina.bains@wsj.com; Twitter: @NinaBains1) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1433 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 PZ Cussons\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PZC.LN) has managed to deliver 1H pretax profit in-line with market expectations, despite \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  headwinds continuing to build for the maker of personal healthcare products and consumer goods. Investec Securities expects better rates of progress as the group hopes to start offsetting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  effects, like that of the Indonesian Rupiah, more effectively in 2H. Investec adds that the numbers remain reliant on continued progress in Nigeria, which, although showing some increase in momentum, remains fragile. Investec rates the stock at 'hold' with a target price of 400p. Panmure Gordon is more wary about PZ Cusson's ability to tackle building \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  headwinds. Their analysts trim the FY EPS forecast by 2% to 17.98p. Even so, that would still imply 8.1% growth, much better than its UK consumer staples peers. Panmure reiterates a 'buy' recommendation and 445p target price. The shares fall 3.2% to 384p Tuesday.(nina.bains@wsj.com; Twitter: @NinaBains1)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1430 GMT [Dow Jones] UK markets get a doubly whammy of first-tier events Wednesday with the November unemployment rate and the January BoE meeting minutes. Both are out at 0930 GMT, and there's the problem, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 UniCredit\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The bank looks for the unemployment rate to fall to 7.2%, ever closer to the BoE's 7% threshold and would be unequivocally sterling positive. However, the BoE minutes will likely include a prominent line that rates will remain on hold even when unemployment hits 7%. Additionally, with inflation now on target and exports looking dismal, the BoE will warn that further sterling appreciation is a risk to the strength/balance of the recovery. Back in December the BoE expressed discomfort with sterling's appreciation, and since then the BoE's effective sterling index is 1.5% higher. Overall, \f28 \uc2 \f2 UniCredit\f28 \uc2 \f2  sees the net impact on sterling as negative, sees GBP/USD breaking back below 1.64 and is happy with its 1Q 2014 forecast of 1.59. GBP/USD now at 1.6470. gary.stride@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1427 GMT [Dow Jones] Turkey \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's monetary policy committee kept the one-week repo rate at 4.5% and overnight borrowing rate and lending rate at 3.5% and 7.75%, respectively. "However, the Committee introduced a new rate today, saying that on days of additional monetary tightening interbank rates will settle at 9%," says Inan Demir, chief economist with Finansbank. "The bottom line is, we do not expect today's decision to provide any respite for TRY. On the contrary, we continue to see further depreciation until we see CBRT forcefully defending the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . In our view, such a defence should involve a "proper" rate hike that would push short-term rates firmly into double digit territory," he notes.(yeliz.candemir@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1426 GMT xx-DJ The Israeli shekel is weakening slightly against the dollar Tuesday, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Israel's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  setting the representative exchange rate at ILS 3.4980, 0.143% higher than Monday's rate. "It's not a big move or a big issue, but there is a nice level of support at this rate," said Rony Gitlin, head of spot trading at Bank Leumi Le Israel's capital markets division. The slight weakening comes mainly from market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Israel\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the finance ministry over the past two weeks. The bank has already bought about $500 million in dollars this month to help offset the shekel's value; this is about twice the amount the bank buys in an average month, according to traders. The finance ministry has also hedged about $340 million in debt, also more than average, traders said. Even though the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has slightly weakened the shekel against USD, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continues to strengthen against EUR, which means that foreign buyers are still maintaining a strong interest in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Gitlin said. The main factors driving foreigners to the shekel include Israel's relatively high interest rates, steady economic growth, and declining government deficit. (saratoth@gmail.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 9:24 EST - Scotiabank says the 1% gain in November manufacturing shipments looks strong even after stripping out the volatile aircraft sector. The measure rose 0.4% from October without it. "The broad takeaway is that this adds further reason behind why the [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] is likely to be highly reticent to engineer a rate cut. That lies on top of the potential pass-through effects of CAD depreciation on import prices and overall CPI." The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's first interest-rate statement and monetary-policy report of the year come tomorrow morning. (don.curren@wsj.com @dbcurren)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1418 GMT [Dow Jones] Following the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 15bp cut today to 2.85% and subsequent forint weakness back over 300 per EUR (last seen January 15th) Takarekbank economist Gergely Suppan expects to see the key rate bottoming between 2.55-2.70%, staying there until the last quarter of this year. Mr. Suppan forecasts gradual rate hikes starting at the end of this year or early next year at the latest. Hungary's key rate could thus rise to 3.75-4.00% by the end of 2015. Downward risk in terms of the rate hikes to this forecast is a more-than-expected disinflationary effect of the Hungarian government's household utility price cuts, while upward risks include sudden volatility in international markets or faster-than-expected monetary tightening by core market central banks. (veronika.gulyas@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1409 GMT [Dow Jones] The Russian ruble slipped to a five-year low of RUB 39.39 against the euro-dollar basket following a WSJ interview with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  deputy chairwoman Ksenia Yudaeva, who said that banks could withstand a ruble rate deviation of up to 30%. Versus the dollar, the ruble presently trades at RUB 33.95, a level last seen in mid-2012. But it still has chances to rebound in the first half of this year, says Anton Zakharov, an analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Promsvyazbank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , who expects Brent crude price to rise towards $114.5 per barrel. He blames outflows from Russian sovereign bonds for the ruble weakness and expects it to firm to RUB 32.85 per dollar. (andrey.ostroukh@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 09:07 EST - USD/CAD briefly topped 1.10 overnight for the first time since 2009, but BMO Capital Markets notes that the next 100 points will be difficult for the USD/CAD to climb through. From a technical standpoint, USD/CAD appears overbought on daily and weekly charts, BMO says. That could see a prolonged correction materialize with support seen at 1.0965. "Caution is warranted for those looking to add to or establish new longs at these levels," BMO says. USD/CAD up 0.3% at 1.0980. (david.george-cosh@wsj.com; @itsdgc)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 09:00 EST - After crossing the 1.10 handle overnight, USD/CAD slips back into the 1.09 range after Canada reports better-than-expected manufacturing sales for November. That report doesn't typically move CAD, and the fact that it did today suggests CAD is sensitive to data at the moment, with the BOC's interest-rate decision due on Wednesday. The policy statement could put the pressure back on CAD, particularly if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strengthens its dovish tilt. Despite the loonie's post-data rebound, USD/CAD is still up 0.25% today, at 1.0978. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 8:50 EST - Scotiabank says Peru's sol could appreciate slightly against the dollar this year as there's "a balance of favorable and unfavorable fundamentals." It forecasts the greenback finishing 2014 at PEN2.76 and averaging PEN2.78; it finished 2013 at PEN2.80 and its down 10% the past year. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1345 GMT [Dow Jones] Hungarian forint weakening will persist, Equilor brokerage says after Hungary cuts its key rate by 15bp to fresh record low of 2.85%. Level of 304 could stop HUF weakness in the short term, but large swings could come in the months ahead, it adds. A EUR/HUF level of as high as 310 is on the cards, economist Akos Horvath says. The ongoing rate cuts are gradually eroding Hungary's rate premium over other central banks' policy rates, he adds. "With parliamentary elections drawing closer, concerns over the level of public debt, the government's plan to aid foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  mortgage holders, and prospects that inflation will accelerate may easily worsen so-far positive investor sentiment toward Hungary," Mr. Horvath says. Thus he recommends shortening the average duration of government bond portfolios. Expects Hungary's easing cycle to bottom out at 2.5% in the coming months. EUR/HUF is at 302.19 versus below 300 at the start of the year. Hungary will hold parliamentary elections April 6. (margit.feher@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 8:34 EST - No US economic data releases today should leave Treasurys pretty flat, bond traders say. Overnight, prices came under a bit of pressure as Chinese stocks rose on the PBOC's fresh cash injection. Ten-year notes off 4/32 to yield 2.843% as US trading gets under way. A WSJ article by Fed-watcher Jon Hilsenrath is getting plenty of attention, saying that the Fed is on track to cut stimulus again next week. But traders don't expect this to dent Treasurys as a $10B reduction is already widely expected. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 21 Jan 2014 10:09 EDT \par UK Market Talk Roundup: Brokers Comments -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1433 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 PZ Cussons\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PZC.LN) has managed to deliver 1H pretax profit in-line with market expectations, despite \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  headwinds continuing to build for the maker of personal healthcare products and consumer goods. Investec Securities expects better rates of progress as the group hopes to start offsetting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  effects, like that of the Indonesian Rupiah, more effectively in 2H. Investec adds that the numbers remain reliant on continued progress in Nigeria, which, although showing some increase in momentum, remains fragile. Investec rates the stock at 'hold' with a target price of 400p. Panmure Gordon is more wary about PZ Cusson's ability to tackle building \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  headwinds. Their analysts trim the FY EPS forecast by 2% to 17.98p. Even so, that would still imply 8.1% growth, much better than its UK consumer staples peers. Panmure reiterates a 'buy' recommendation and 445p target price. The shares fall 3.2% to 384p Tuesday.(nina.bains@wsj.com; Twitter: @NinaBains1)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1413 GMT [Dow Jones] The UK's Carphone Warehouse (CPW.LN) has delivered a broadly solid update for 3Q trading, with the mobile phone retailer continuing to drive market share gains across the profitable postpay [contract] consumer segment, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Carphone Warehouse reported its sixth consecutive quarter of like-for-like revenue growth. "Management's renewed retail focus on CPW Europe is becoming more evident, with a priority to reinforce scale in Europe through the Connected World partnerships [strategy]," says Citi. Analysts add that trends around UK 4G adoption and average revenue per user uplifts remains a key driver into FY15. Citi rates the stock at 'buy' with a target price of 320p. The shares rise 0.1% to 280p Tuesday afternoon in London. (nina.bains@wsj.com; Twitter: @NinaBains1)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1310 GMT [Dow Jones] European stocks have certainly remained in favour with fund managers in January, with a lot of this optimism riding on expectations of stronger economic growth and an improving EPS outlook. But, should either disappoint, the consensus "overweight" investment case on European equities could start to crack. EU stocks remain global investors' destination of choice for the fifth month in a row in January, while the "conviction" to own EU equities strengthens further from last month with a net 34% of fund managers wanting to overweight the region on a 12-month view, according to the BofA Merrill Lynch Fund Manager Survey for January. But despite elevated growth and profit expectations, investors still regard EU equities as undervalued. Only 6% of EU fund managers reckon stocks are over-valued, while 31% think EU stocks are still cheap, according to BofA-ML. Nevertheless, the proportion seeing Europe as 'fairly valued' surged to 63% this month, the most since September 2009, according to the survey. BofA-ML reckons that high cash levels and valuations are likely to support EU stocks, but a lot rests on corporate earnings delivering. With a net 41% of fund managers of the view that consensus EPS expectations for Europe are too high, there's plenty of scope for disappointment this earnings season. (nina.bains@wsj.com; Twitter: @NinaBains1)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1308 GMT [Dow Jones] UK's FTSE 100 index is up 0.1% at 6843.29, mostly wavering in positive territory as investors weigh positive developments in China against some mixed domestic economic data. Equity markets in Europe are broadly higher after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  injected funds into the money markets to stave off a potential liquidity crunch. However, gains in the UK somewhat lag peers following a mixed reading of the CBI quarterly industrial trends survey. Investors are also eyeing US markets for direction following Monday's closure owing to Martin Luther King Day. Shares in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Unilever\f28 \uc2 \f2  rise 3% after 4Q sales growth comes in better than feared. \f28 \uc2 \f2 SABMiller\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares retreat 1.2%, as the brewer announced it struggled with sluggish growth in North America and Europe during 3Q. At 1430 GMT, the IMF is due to release the update of the World Economic Outlook. (nina.bains@wsj.com; Twitter: @NinaBains1)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1145 GMT [Dow Jones] Property analysts at banking giant \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  have initiated coverage of ten UK property companies with a positive outlook, with their target prices offering an average 15.5% potential return and are 16.5% above consensus. "A resurgent UK economy is on the cusp of spurring rental growth and leading UK commercial real estate into phase 3 of its recovery - a phase of prolonged and sustainable capital value growth." \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  initiate Capital&Counties (CAPC.LN), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Land Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  (LAND.LN), LondonMetric (LMP.LN) and Shaftesbury (SHB.LN) with a 'neutral' rating and \f28 \uc2 \f2 British Land\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BLND.LN), Derwent London (DLN.LN), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Great Portland Estates\f28 \uc2 \f2  (GPOR.LN )Hammerson (HMSO.LN), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Intu Properties\f28 \uc2 \f2  (INTU.LN) and Segro (SGRO.LN) with 'overweight' recommendations. (nick.cawley@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1131 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 African Barrick Gold\f28 \uc2 \f2 's (ABG.LN) 4Q gold production comes in ahead of the miner's FY production and cash cost guidance, helping to give the shares a lift Tuesday. "The company has in 2013 perfected the art of under-promising and over-delivering, and the 4Q delivered more of that," says Investec Securities. "While certainly better than under-delivering, we note that consistent material over-delivery can signal its own issues, including potentially raising uncertainty in the accuracy of the reserve models." In addition, Investec says that it cannot be overly encouraged by the company's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  improvements until it becomes net cash generative. Investec rates the stock at 'sell' with a target price of 137p. The shares rise 7.7% to 216p Tuesday, topping the FTSE 250 index. (nina.bains@wsj.com; Twitter: @NinaBains1)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1007 GMT [Dow Jones] While robust demand for \f28 \uc2 \f2 SABMiller\f28 \uc2 \f2 's (SAB.LN) low-price and premium beers in emerging markets helped to offset sluggish growth in North America and Europe over 3Q, many of the brewer's key countries have seen their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continue to materially weaken against the dollar. Shore Capital says it is cautious on the stock in the near term, arguing that while \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  effects could prove temporary, there remains the possibility of a "latent impact on consumer spending" should imported inflation feed through. Shore rates the stock at 'hold'. The shares fall 1.9% to 3035p Tuesday.(nina.bains@wsj.com; Twitter: @NinaBains1)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0925 GMT [Dow Jones] Shares in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Unilever\f28 \uc2 \f2  (ULVR.LN) rise Tuesday after the consumer products giant delivered a "better than feared" 4Q result, which comes as something of a relief following a disappointing 3Q. Underlying 4Q sales growth of 4.1% came in ahead of more gloomier forecasts. Still, Oriel Securities notes that 4Q growth remained driven by emerging markets. "\f28 \uc2 \f2 Unilever\f28 \uc2 \f2 's growth rate remains hampered in our view by too much emphasis on emerging markets and sustained underperformance in mature markets," according to analysts. Oriel rates the stock at 'sell' with a target price of 2170p. The shares rise 4.2% to 2539p in London Tuesday, topping the FTSE 100 index. (nina.bains@wsj.com; Twitter: @NinaBains1)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0755 GMT [Dow Jones] UK's FTSE 100 index is expected to rise at the open Tuesday, tracking gains in Asia after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  injected liquidity into the financial system. "Persistent fears of a credit crunch and recent data pointing towards a slowdown have had markets cautious that the world's second largest economy could be reaching a tipping point," says Jonathan Sudaria of Capital Spreads. "However, the loosening of money markets has given Asian indices a boost, and we are set to see that optimism carrying over into Europe this morning." Capital Spreads calls the FTSE 100 up 22 points at 6859. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Unilever\f28 \uc2 \f2  4Q results are in focus Tuesday, as is \f28 \uc2 \f2 SABMiller\f28 \uc2 \f2 's 3Q trading update. The UK CBI quarterly industrial trends survey is at 1100 GMT. (nina.bains@wsj.com; Twitter: @NinaBains1) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9288 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 21, 2014 10:09 ET (15:09 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140121ea1l00202\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
