\par\par \b \uc2 Tel Aviv Index Trades Lower with Shares Mixed-- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,284 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Januar 2014\par 10:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0907 GMT [Dow Jones]--The Tel Aviv-25 is down 0.52% at 1,332.65 at 0850GMT, with shares mixed and direction looking uncertain for the rest of the session. "The market is waiting to see what global markets will do," says Zach Herzog, head of international sales at Psagot Securities Ltd. Overall, traders expect a low-volume day, after local stocks finished 2013 in positive territory. Leading the gainers are \f28 \uc2 \f2 Perrigo Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., up 0.45% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Strauss Group Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., up 0.40%. The sharpest losses are in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Delek Group Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., down 1.03%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Israel Corp. Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., down 1.11% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Hapoalim\f28 \uc2 \f2 , down 1.01%. Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 0849 GMT 0000 GMT (Dow JOnes)--The shekel continues to weaken in morning trading Thursday, with USD/ILS up 0.5% at 3.4888 at 0834GMT after \uc2 the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, buying about $200 million in its latest attempt to hold its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , confirmed by traders but not by the bank, seems to have been of similar size to other recent efforts and is in line with the bank's average monthly \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases. But the weakening of the shekel was sharper than usual because recent days have also seen several calls, including by a former deputy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, for the bank to set an upper limit for the shekel in order to protect Israel's export-dependent economy.\par \par \uc2 0844 GMT [Dow Jones]--And this is despite having closed its recent short recommendation at 106.05. Technical strategist Richard Adcock says, "the immediate risk is for a recovery as a reaction to the latest decline, although we will be looking to use this and/or a close below 105.97 as an opportunity to re-establish shorts. The first resistance focus is now 107.04, the mid-point of the latest sell-off." \uc2 UBS\uc2  target 102.45. The March gilt currently trades 0.43 lower at 106.13. (nick.cawley@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0827 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is higher on short-covering after recent losses, say two local traders. The pair is at 29.979, off an earlier low of 29.770, versus 29.950 late Tuesday in Taipei. But they say the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is believed to have bought the USD around 29.910 to keep the pair in its target band. "The U.S. dollar reversed its recent weak trend, as some market participants said they regretted selling the greenback too early," one trader says, but adds the pair is unlikely to reach 30.000 anytime soon, as some traders remain cautious about moves by foreign investors after the New Year holidays. The second trader says rises in the pair were likely because of importer buying. They tip the pair to trade in a 29.850-29.950 band Friday. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0825 GMT [Dow Jones] That would confirm both the flash estimate and the improvement over November's 51.6 reading. Beforehand, France (0848 GMT) should slip to 47.1 from 48.4 last, while Germany (0853GMT) rises to 54.2 from 52.7 in November. EUR/USD now at 1.3721, the final readings don't usually trigger much reaction unless they come in very wide of flash estimates. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 0824 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD bases at 1.3716 as the final December Spain manufacturing PMI springs back into expansion, at 50.8 from 48.6 in November. EUR/USD now at 1.3725, next resistance is 1.3780 and 1.3810. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Hong Kong November retail sales were likely up 3.3% on year by value, down from a 6.3% rise in October, according to the median forecast of three economists polled by The Wall Street Journal. In volume terms, retail sales were likely up 3.0% on year in November, easing from a 5.8% rise in October, the poll shows. Economists remain cautious on the outlook for consumer spending this year. "The positive wealth effect related to rising housing prices, which boosted consumption over the past few years, should dissipate," says Hang Seng Bank economist Ryan Lam, adding the cloudy economic outlook could weigh on local consumers. He expects the retail sales value to grow by 13% in 2014. The government is scheduled to release the data at 0830 GMT Thursday.(chester.yung@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0811 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilt futures open the best part of half-a-point lower, pushing the yield on 10-year cash back up to levels last seen in mid-2011, as core fixed income starts 2014 on the back foot. Ahead, UK manufacturing PMI at 0930 GMT. The March gilt is 0.48 lower at 106.08 and the 10-year gilt is quoted at 3.075%. (nick.cawley@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0800 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD slipping in late Asia trade with a fresh 2014 low of 1.3726 just seen. \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  technical analyst Karen Jones says next key support is the uptrend line at 1.3711, a break and daily close below here would alleviate immediate upside pressure, and put the focus on the October high of 1.3646. Topside, Jones looks for any rebounds to struggle with resistance at 1.3810 and 1.3833. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 0754 GMT [Dow Jones] ..with China's official December print coming in at 51.0 against 51.2 expected, while the \uc2 HSBC\uc2  reading also declined to 50.5 from 50.8 last. Sweden's effort was even more disappointing with a December reading of 52.2 from 56.0 in November and expectations of 55.0. Coming up, French manufacturing is expected to slip deeper into contraction at 47.1 vs 47.8 last, while Germany steams ahead at 54.2 from 52.7. The Eurozone print is seen 52.7 from 51.8 last. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 0740 GMT [Dow Jones]--German fixed income continues to weaken with the March bund contract opening the New Year 35 ticks lower at 138.82, pushing the yield on the 10-year cash 3bps higher to 1.96%. Analysts at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  note that 10-year yields have already increased over 25bps during December and, with German supply kicking off only in two weeks and no 10-year Bund issuance due until the end of the month, "further upside appears limited in the near-term." Euro zone PMI manufacturing data released at 0900 GMT. (nick.cawley@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0624 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals prices moved steadily higher in Asian trade, ignoring the HSBC China manufacturing purchasing managers' index, published Thursday, which fell to 50.5 in December from 50.8 in November. LME three-month copper touched an intraday high of $7,445/ton, the highest level since June 5. Copper and base metals are usually sensitive to economic data, especially from China--the world's biggest consumer of industrial metals. Some economists say that China's economic growth is slowing and a recovery in demand remains fragile. Looking ahead, unemployment and ISM manufacturing data from the U.S. on Friday is likely to provide further cues to metal prices. LME three-month copper is at $7,438/ton, up $79, while aluminium is at $1,814.75/ton, up $14.75 from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 02, 2014 04:07 ET (09:07 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140102ea12000gj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
