\par\par \b \uc2 Potential No.2 For Fed: An Activist, More Or Less\b0\par\par\uc2 713 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Dezember 2013\par 01:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A4\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (FROM THE WALL STREET JOURNAL 12/13/13) \par    By Jon Hilsenrath and Joshua Mitnick \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As governor of the Bank of Israel from 2005 through this June, Stanley Fischer confronted two potential crises: The risk of a global financial meltdown washing onto Israel's shores, and tensions at home with Palestinian authorities over domestic finances.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Mr. Fischer would face very different challenges if he lands the job of Federal Reserve vice chairman, but would still need a combination of financial know-how and political acumen. He is President Barack Obama's choice for the job, though the pick hasn't been finalized, say people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 The Fed leadership transition is unfolding as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  weighs whether to pull back on its complex easy-money policies. Mr. Fischer will be asked to play a delicate subordinate role to Janet Yellen, who is awaiting Senate confirmation of her nomination to become the Fed's chairwoman in February. His nomination also would be subject to Senate confirmation.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Fischer, 70 years old, has compiled a four-decade career that provides clues about how he would fill the role as the second-in-command at the world's most powerful economic institution.\par \par \uc2 Palestinian \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chief Jihad Al Wazir suggested Mr. Fischer was skilled at navigating political sensitivities to focus on economic challenges.\par \par \uc2 "During Stan's tenure [at \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2 ], the relations remained on a professional level," Mr. Al Wazir said from Ramallah. "It was devoid of politics. There was an understanding of the issues."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Fischer's record shows he has supported an activist \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past to help the economy, even though he has expressed some skepticism about the Fed's vigorous efforts to spur growth and bring down unemployment in recent months.\par \par \uc2 In 1977, Mr. Fischer wrote a seminal research paper laying out the case for an activist monetary policy to counter short-run economic downturns. The paper was a counterpunch at \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2  economists who saw government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as ineffective and became a key part of his world view as a policy maker.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Fischer taught at Chicago in the early 1970s, but with the 1977 paper he became a leader in the "New Keynesian" academic movement, which accepted some Chicago arguments but still saw a role for government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the 1990s, as the deputy director of the International Monetary Fund, Mr. Fischer helped build what came to be known as the "Washington Consensus" on economic policy, which called for flexible exchange rates, free capital flows between countries and balanced budgets.\par \par \uc2 Critics on the right said the IMF was too quick to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in countries jarred by market instability during this period. Critics on the left said the IMF demanded overly harsh policies, such as interest-rate increases and spending cuts in economies in Latin America, Asia and Russia when it did \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilize them.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 , his \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  won their share of supporters.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Al Wazir said Mr. Fischer helped ease strain in the Palestinian banking system during recurring liquidity crises because of shortfalls in foreign aid and freezes in Israeli transfers of tax revenue collected on the behalf of Palestinians. He helped to get shipments of cash into the Gaza Strip's blockaded economy.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Fischer was one of the first central bankers to cut interest rates during the 2008 crisis. He also took the controversial step of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to help Israeli exporters and build up the country's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, bolstering confidence in the economy.\par \par \uc2 "It was a sign that there was someone in control," said Vered Dar, former chief economist at Psagot investment house.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Fischer wasn't universally beloved in Israel.\par \par \uc2 His most consistent detractor, Yaron Zelekha, a former accountant general in the Israeli Finance Ministry, said Mr. Fischer didn't raise interest rates soon enough, thus contributing to a real-estate bubble in Israel.\par \par \uc2 Last summer, Mr. Fischer was criticized by many in Israel's ultraorthodox Jewish community for pointing to their high birth ates and low labor-force participation as unsustainable for Israel's economy in the long term.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 12, 2013 19:55 ET (00:55 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131213e9cd0001w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
