\par\par \b \uc2 Shekel gains halted after \uc2 Bank of Israel\uc2  buys up to $300 mln of  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Steven Scheer   \par \uc2 424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2013\par 13:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, Sept 2 (Reuters) - \uc2 The Bank of Israel\uc2  bought as much as $300 million of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday to halt a three-day rally of the shekel, which is being influenced by possible military action on Syria.\par \par \uc2 Speculation about possible Western military action against neighbouring Syria weakened the shekel to a six-week low of nearly 3.67 against the dollar last week. But after Britain and the United States delayed any strikes, the Israeli \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounced.\par \par \uc2 It reached close to 3.59 per dollar and 4.75 per euro early on Monday, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to act.\par \par \uc2 A dealer at \uc2 Israel Discount Bank\uc2  estimated \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2  bought some $150 million of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  while the head of a trading desk at another large Israeli bank said it was between $200 million and $300 million.\par \par \uc2 Following the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the shekel has weakened to 3.6150 to the dollar and 4.78 per euro.\par \par \uc2 "The shekel is back to the same point before Syria," said the head of the trading desk, referring to talk of military action.\par \par \uc2 When strikes were believed to be imminent, foreign banks bought dollars while Israeli exports enjoyed the higher dollar rate, he said. "They were hoping that if something happened, the dollar would go up," he added.\par \par \uc2 But now, they were forced to sell the dollar, the dealer noted.\par \par \uc2 The shekel has been one of the best-performing emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year but last week it shed almost half its 2013 gains on fears of Western military action in Syria that could spark a wider conflict in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 A strong shekel is a particular concern for policymakers since a robust \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  harms exports, which account for some 40 percent of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic activity. In addition to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  from time to time in the market, \uc2 the Bank of Israel\uc2  lowered its benchmark interest rate twice in May, citing a strong shekel that neared a two-year peak.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent stability was one reason the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cited for leaving rates on hold last week.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it would also buy $2.1 billion of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2013 to offset gains made to Israel's current account surplus in the wake of the start to natural gas production at an offshore well. (Reporting by Steven Scheer; editing by Ron Askew)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 ISRAEL\b0 \highlight\uc2 -SHEKEL/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  | ABN | D | E | RBN | M   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130902e992000jk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
